      "youtube_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3lwuhm-v2GA",
      "video_id": "3lwuhm-v2GA",
      "duration_seconds": 445.84,
      "status": "completed",
      "notes_markdown": "# CF M02 C02 Future Value\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Future value quantifies how today's investment grows over time through compounding; the formula FV = C₀(1 + R)ᵀ captures the dual impact of a longer horizon and higher interest rate on wealth accumulation.\n### Time Value Of Money\n- **Money has time value:** A rupee today is worth more than a rupee received in the future.\n- **Risk-return tradeoff:** A safe rupee is worth more than a risky rupee.\n- **Future Value (FV):** The value at some point in time in the future of an investment made today.\n- **Present Value (PV):** The value today of a cash flow to be received in the future.\n- **Compound vs. Simple Interest:** Under compounding, interest earns interest (e.g., year-2 interest of 7.49 includes interest on the year-1 interest of 7). Under simple interest, the yearly amount remains constant (7 rupees on a 100-rupee principal).\n- **General FV Formula:** FV = C₀(1 + R)ᵀ, where C₀ is the initial investment, R is the annual rate, and T is the time in years.\n- **Key Drivers:** Longer investment horizon (T) and higher interest rate (R) both increase FV.\n- **Compounding Frequency:** The lecture focuses on annual compounding unless otherwise specified.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n- **Formula:** FV = C₀(1 + R)ᵀ\n- **Example from lecture:** Invest 100 rupees at 7% per year compounded annually.\n  - After 1 year: 100 × 1.07 = **107 rupees**.\n  - After 2 years: 100 × 1.07² = **114.49 rupees** (intermediate: 107 × 1.07 = 114.49; verified: 107 + 7.49 = 114.49).\n- **Interest progression:** Year-1 interest = 7; Year-2 interest = 7.49 (higher due to compounding).\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming future value grows linearly with time. → **Reality:** FV grows exponentially; doubling the horizon more than doubles the future value due to the (1 + R)ᵀ effect.\n2. **Trap:** Using simple interest logic for long-term investments. → **Reality:** Most real-world scenarios use compounding; ignoring compounding significantly understates FV over extended periods.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
      "slides_json": "[]",
