      "notes_markdown": "# CF M02 C09 Applying NPV and Rate of Return to a Project\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** A project with a $700,000 initial outlay and a certain $800,000 payoff in one year is worth undertaking, as both the NPV and rate of return criteria yield a positive accept decision when using a 7% risk-free opportunity cost of capital.\n### NPV And Rate Of Return\n- **Timeline:** Initial cash outflow of -$700,000 at t=0; certain cash inflow of +$800,000 at t=1.\n- **Opportunity Cost of Capital (R):** 7%. Because the $800,000 payoff is a \"sure shot\" (risk-free), the appropriate benchmark is the government bond rate, not the risky stock market return of 12%.\n- **NPV Calculation:** \n  - PV = $800,000 / 1.07 = $747,664\n  - NPV = $747,664 - $700,000 = **$47,664** (positive → accept)\n- **Rate of Return Calculation:** \n  - Return = ($800,000 - $700,000) / $700,000 = 1/7 ≈ **14.3%**\n  - Since 14.3% > 7% opportunity cost, the project is accepted.\n- **Decision Consistency:** Both NPV and rate of return criteria produce the same accept/reject decision in this scenario.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** The opportunity cost of capital for this project is 12% (the stock market return). → **Reality:** The opportunity cost is 7% because the project's cash flow is risk-free; only government bond returns are the appropriate benchmark.\n2. **Trap:** The rate of return is 12%, so the project should be accepted. → **Reality:** The computed rate of return is ~14.3%; comparing this against the 7% opportunity cost yields acceptance, but the 12% figure is a distractor representing risky-market returns.\n3. **Trap:** A positive NPV alone is sufficient to accept, but the rate of return criterion would give a different answer. → **Reality:** In this case, both criteria agree; if the return were between 7% and 14.3%, NPV would still be positive while the rate of return rule would reject.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
      "slides_json": "[]",
