      "notes_markdown": "# CF M02 C17 Loan Amortisation with Equal Principal Repayments\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Under an equal principal repayment structure, the principal portion of each payment remains constant while interest declines on the reducing balance, causing total annual payments to decrease over time; the present value of all payments equals the original loan principal.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n$$PV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{Total\\ Payment_t}{(1+r)^t} = \\text{Principal}$$\n\n**Year-by-Year Breakdown:**\n\n| Year | Beginning Balance | Interest (10%) | Principal | Total Payment | Ending Balance |\n|:----:|:-----------------:|:--------------:|:---------:|:-------------:|:--------------:|\n| 1 | $1,000 | $100 | $250 | **$350** | $750 |\n| 2 | $750 | $75 | $250 | **$325** | $500 |\n| 3 | $500 | $50 | $250 | **$300** | $250 |\n| 4 | $250 | $25 | $250 | **$250** | **$0** |\n### Interest And Principal Dynamics\n- **Interest payment ↓** each year because it is calculated as `10% × Beginning Balance`, and the outstanding principal shrinks annually.\n- **Principal repayment →** remains fixed at $250/year per the loan agreement.\n- **Total payment ↓** each year as the declining interest component offsets the constant principal portion.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming total payments remain constant across all years. → **Reality:** Total payments decline each year because the interest component shrinks while the principal portion stays fixed.\n2. **Trap:** Believing the ending balance after Year 3 is $500. → **Reality:** The ending balance after Year 3 is $250 (the remaining principal to be repaid in Year 4).",
