      "notes_markdown": "# CF M02 C23 Applying the Four Present Value Shortcuts\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** This lecture demonstrates four PV shortcuts, perpetuity, growing perpetuity, annuity, and growing annuity, using a $1B base cash flow at 8% discount. Crucially, when growth rate (G) equals the discount rate (R), the growing annuity formula simplifies to $C \\times T / (1+R)$, yielding a higher PV than lower growth scenarios.\n### Valuation Rules & Criteria\n- **Perpetuity:** PV = $C / R$. With $C = \\$1B$ and $R = 8\\%$, PV = **$12.5B**.\n- **Growing Perpetuity:** PV = $C / (R - G)$. With $G = 4\\%$, PV = **$25B** (since $4\\% < 8\\%$).\n- **Annuity:** PV = $\\frac{C}{R} \\times [1 - (1+R)^{-T}]$. For $T = 20$, PV = **$9.818B**.\n- **Growing Annuity (G ≠ R):** PV = $\\frac{C}{R-G} \\times [1 - (\\frac{1+G}{1+R})^{-T}]$. For $G=4\\%$, PV = **$13.247B**.\n- **Growing Annuity (G = R):** Special case formula: PV = $C \\times T / (1+R)$. For $G=8\\%$, PV = **$18.519B**.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** The growing perpetuity formula $C / (R - G)$ applies regardless of the relationship between $R$ and $G$. → **Reality:** The formula is valid **only when $G < R$**; if $G \\ge R$, the present value is infinite or requires a different approach.\n2. **Trap:** Annuity and growing annuity formulas are interchangeable. → **Reality:** A standard annuity assumes constant payments ($G=0$), while a growing annuity requires a growth rate $G$; using the wrong formula produces incorrect PV values.\n3. **Trap:** In a growing annuity where $G = R$, the standard formula $\\frac{C}{R-G} [1 - (\\dots)^T]$ can be used directly. → **Reality:** This causes division by zero; the correct special case formula is $C \\times T / (1+R)$.",
