      "notes_markdown": "# CF M02 C25 Relationship Between APR and EAR\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** EAR adjusts APR for compounding frequency; whenever M > 1, EAR > APR, and the future value formula must use the periodic rate (APR/M) raised to total periods (M × T).\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n- **Relationship:** $1 + \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{\\text{APR}}{M}\\right)^M$\n- **Example:** APR = 10% compounded semi-annually → M = 2\n  - $1 + \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.10}{2}\\right)^2 = 1.1025$\n  - **EAR = 10.25%**\n- **Future Value Formula (any compounding frequency):**\n  $$\\text{FV} = C_0 \\left(1 + \\frac{\\text{APR}}{M}\\right)^{M \\times T}$$\n- **Periodic Interest Rate:** $\\text{APR} / M$ (e.g., 10% / 2 = 5% per half-year)\n- **Total Periods:** $M \\times T$ (e.g., 2 intervals/year × 3 years = 6 total periods)\n### APR EAR Relationship\n- **M definition:** Number of compounding intervals per year (6-month → M=2; quarterly → M=4; monthly → M=12; daily → M=365).\n- **APR vs EAR:** When M = 1 (annual compounding), EAR = APR. When M > 1, EAR is strictly greater than APR because compounding \"interest on interest\" amplifies the effective return.\n- **Unit transformation:** Shifting from \"per year\" to \"per period\" allows the formula to work for any frequency; the time unit effectively becomes the compounding interval.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming EAR is always equal to APR regardless of compounding frequency.  \n   **Reality:** EAR > APR whenever M > 1; they are only equal when compounding occurs annually (M = 1).\n2. **Trap:** Using APR directly in the future value exponent without dividing by M and multiplying by total periods.  \n   **Reality:** The correct FV formula is $C_0(1 + \\text{APR}/M)^{M \\times T}$; using just APR ignores compounding frequency effects.",
