      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now let us talk about as to how to value a bond. Before we talk about bond valuation, let me ask you a very simple question. How do you find out the value of an asset or the present value of an asset? You essentially look at expected future cash flows from the asset. And when you discount those cash flows depending on their riskiness, you arrive at present value. So therefore, present value of an asset is nothing but present value of expected future cash flows from the asset. If you hold a bond, what are the payments that you have been promised? As you understand, if you hold a bond, you have been promised two kinds of payments by the issuer. The first payment is the coupon payment, which is going to be period after period. And the second kind of payment is going to be the payment of face value, which is one-time payment going to be made to you at the time of maturity of the bond. Okay. Now these coupon payments, as we discussed, these are going to be made period after period. So every period, you are going to receive the same coupon. Why? Because coupon is computed by multiplying coupon rate every period by face value. And since your coupon rate or the face value do not change, therefore your coupon payments period after period remain the same. And therefore these coupon payments take a pattern similar to what we discussed previously. Can you identify the pattern that these coupon payments take? You are getting the same coupon period after period and only for a fixed number of periods. So this pattern is as you can recall annuity. And we know how to find out the present value of an annuity. On the other hand you are also going to get one time payment of face value at the time of maturity. And you already know how to compute the present value of a one time payment. And therefore if you sum the present value of coupon payments and the present value of face value, you will arrive at the present value of your bond. Now, as we discussed previously if the market is functioning fairly in that case, the market value of an asset should be same as its present value. Which means that the assets are neither overvalued nor they are undervalued. They are fairly valued, which means that their market value has to be equal to present value. So this is how we can find out the price of a bond. Yes, so price of a bond or market value of a bond should be equal to the present value and this present value should be equal to the sum of present values of coupon payments and face value. Now, there is another important thing which is worth noting. Bond prices are usually expressed as a percentage of face value. Remember this.",
