      "transcript_text": "[music] Let us look at an example. You have purchased a 3-year US government bond. The bond has an annual coupon rate of 11.25% paid semiannually. The face value of the bond is $1,000. Now let me tell you one important fact and that fact is US government bonds always have a face value of $1,000. Indian government bonds have a face value of rupees 100. Both US government bonds and Indian government bonds make coupon payments semiannually. Remember these facts. So even if the face value was not given to you, you could have assumed that face value to be $1,000 because it is a US government bought. Now the question before you is if investors demand a return of.17% per year compounded semiannually what is the price of the bond? Now the first thing you should do in such a case is you should put your variables or given information on a sheet of paper. So let us put this information out. Okay. So here what is the face value which is given to us? The face value is given to us as $1,000. Okay. Can we compute our coupon? Now this coupon will depend on coupon rate every period. What is our coupon rate every period? Our coupon rate is given to us as 11.25%. And since this is expressed as an APR and since you are going to get two coupons every year, so therefore you will get a coupon which will depend on your half yearly coupon rate. Yes, which is going to be 11.25% divided by two. And this is percent of what? This is percentage of your face value which is $1,000. So what does this give you? So essentially if you solve this you will get $56.25 of coupon every half year. Okay. What is our R? Do we have any information on R yield to maturity given to us? If you look at the question, it says investors demand a return of.17%. Per year compounded semiannually. What is this? This is nothing but the yield required by investors. So therefore our yield to maturity is given to us as.17%. Again this is expressed as an APR and therefore to find out yield every half year we will have to divide it by two. So therefore our yield to maturity every half year will be 085% every half year. Okay. How many half years do we have to maturity? we have 3 years because it is a three-year US government bond. So therefore we have 6 half years to maturity. So we have six half years to maturity and therefore let us try to find out the price of the bond which is going to be C which is 56 which is 56.25 divided by R which is 0.85 85 into 1 - 1 / 1 0 0 85 raised to the power 6. Okay. Plus f. What is f here? It is 1,000 divided by again 1.0000 0 0 0 85 raised to ^ 6. So if you solve this what will you get your price as? You will get your price as dollar 1 331.41. So if you know how to operate your scientific calculators correctly, which I assume you will know, you will get $1331 approximately as the price of your bond. Now let us look at part B of this problem. If investors demand a return of 8% per year compounded semiannually, what is going to be new price of the bond? So now what has happened? Essentially your yield to maturity has gone up from.17% to 8% now. And therefore what is going to happen to your price? So if your discount rate goes up, what happens to your present value? Your present value will essentially come down and therefore your price of the bond now is going to reduce from $1331. However, we have to find out the new price of the bond. I am leaving that as an exercise for you to do it yourself. However, note that the price of the bond will come out as $1085 approximately. [music]",
