      "notes_markdown": "# CF M03 C09 Government Bonds and Bond Market Quotes\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Government bonds are uniquely identified by coupon rate and maturity year (e.g., 7.1s of 2030), and their market quotes always present bid/ask prices as percentages of the $1,000 face value, where the ask price is what buyers pay and the bid price is what sellers receive.\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n- **Classification by maturity:** T-bills (≤1 year), T-notes (1-10 years), T-bonds (>10 years).\n- **Face values vary:** US bonds = $1,000; Indian bonds = 100 rupees.\n- **Coupon payment frequency:** Most US/UK/Canada/Japan bonds pay semi-annually; most Eurobonds pay annually.\n- **Identification:** Bonds are named by coupon rate and maturity year (e.g., 5.5s of 2028; GS = government security).\n- **Bid vs. Ask price:** \n  - **Bid price:** Dealer’s price to *buy* the bond.\n  - **Ask price:** Dealer’s price to *sell* the bond.\n  - **Buyer pays Ask price**; **Seller receives Bid price**.\n- **Bid-Ask Spread:** The difference between ask and bid prices; represents the bond dealer’s profit.\n- **Ask Yield:** Yield to maturity based on the ask price (quoted as APR).\n\n### Quote Example (from transcript)\n\n| Element | Value | Interpretation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Maturity | 15 Aug 2028 |, |\n| Coupon rate | 5.5% | APR, expressed as % of $1,000 face value |\n| Bid price | 105.01% | = $1,050.10 |\n| Ask price | 105.02% | = $1,050.20 |\n| Ask yield | 3.694% | YTM based on ask price |\n### Government Bonds and Bond Market Quotes Principles\n\n- **Bid price ↑** → Dealer pays more to buy → **Trader’s cost to acquire bond ↓**\n- **Ask price ↑** → Buyer pays more to acquire → **Bond becomes more expensive**\n- **Bid-Ask spread ↑** → Dealer profit margin widens → **Liquidity cost for traders rises**\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n\n1. **Trap:** The bid price is the price a buyer pays to purchase the bond.  \n   **Reality:** The **ask price** is what the buyer pays; the bid price is what the seller receives.\n\n2. **Trap:** The bid-ask spread is the total cost of buying the bond.  \n   **Reality:** The spread is the dealer’s profit margin; the buyer’s actual cost is the ask price.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"Which of the following statements correctly classifies a government bond that matures in exactly 18 months?\", \"options\": [\"A) It is a Treasury Bill (T-Bill).\", \"B) It is a Treasury Note (T-Note).\", \"C) It is a Treasury Bond (T-Bond).\", \"D) It is a Eurobond.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"Treasury bills mature in one year or less. A bond maturing in 18 months exceeds the T-bill threshold but falls within the 1 to 10 year range for T-notes. T-bonds require maturity beyond 10 years.\", \"key_concept\": \"Maturity Bucket Classification\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"In the US government bond market, if an investor wants to purchase a bond from a dealer, which price must they pay?\", \"options\": [\"A) Bid price\", \"B) Ask price\", \"C) Face value\", \"D) Par value\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy | Foundational\", \"explanation\": \"The ask price is the price at which the bond dealer is willing to sell. To buy, the investor transacts at the ask price. The bid price is the price the dealer pays to buy from the investor.\", \"key_concept\": \"Bid-Ask Price Relevance\"}]",
