          "model_answer": "The 30-year zero coupon bond carries greater interest rate risk because the present value of distant cash flows is more sensitive to changes in the discount rate (yield to maturity). As explained in the lecture, when the yield changes from 0% to 10%, the price of the 3-year bond is calculated as $1,000 / (1.1)^3, which results in a relatively small decrease in price. In contrast, the price of the 30-year bond is calculated as $1,000 / (1.1)^30, which results in a drastic decrease in price. This demonstrates that for the same change in interest rates (R), the bond with the longer maturity (T) experiences a much larger price fluctuation. Therefore, the longer the maturity, the greater the interest rate risk, as the present value of the distant face value payment is heavily dependent on the discount rate.",
