      "transcript_text": "So, now let us move on to our next example. In the last example, what would have the bond holder earned during the year if the bond yield continued to remain at 3% per year annually compounded, 1 year per year later. Again, let us compute the price today and the price 1 year from today. Assuming that our R remains at 3%, our P0 when R is 3%. We already computed that in the last example, it was 5 divided by 0.03 into 1 minus 1 divided by 1. 1.03 raised to the power 6 plus 1000 divided by 1.03 raised to the power 6 which came out as 110.834. Now, we have to compute price at the end of 1 year from today, assuming that our R is 5% per year. hour R remains at 3%, which means that your R1 is going to remain at 3%. Given that can we not compute price at the end of 1 year from today. So, let us compute it. It will be again 5 divided by 0.03 into 1 minus 1 divided by 1.03 raised to the power 6. So, what do you get as your P1? So, you will get your P1 as 109.1.834.5. So, what do you get as your P1? So, you will get your P1 as 109.1.834.5. And therefore, can you not compute your rate of return now? Your rate of return now, again it will be C plus P1 minus P0 which is your change in price divided by your initial price of P0. It will be 5 plus P1 which is 109.159 minus P0 which is 110.834 divided by your initial price of 110.834. You compute it, what will you get? You will get 3% as your rate of return over this period. And this 3% is equal to your YTM of 3%. How much is your YTM? It is 3%. Therefore, what do you conclude from this particular example? You conclude that if your YTM remains the same over a period, the rate of return over that period will be same as your YTM. And in a similar way, if your YTM increases over a period, your rate of return over that period will be lower than your initial YTM.",
