          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the relationship between nominal cash flow, real cash flow, and the inflation rate. Using the example from the lecture where a bond pays Rupees 110 after one year and inflation is 7%, calculate the real cash flow and explain what this value represents in terms of purchasing power compared to today.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The relationship is defined by the formula: Real Cash Flow = Nominal Cash Flow / (1 + i)^T, where i is the inflation rate and T is the time period.\n\n**Calculation:**\n* Nominal Cash Flow = Rupees 110\n* Inflation rate (i) = 7% or 0.07\n* Time (T) = 1 year\n* Real Cash Flow = 110 / (1 + 0.07)^1 = 110 / 1.07 = Rupees 102.80\n\n**Purchasing Power Interpretation:**\nThe real cash flow of Rupees 102.80 represents the purchasing power of the future payment in today's terms. It means that Rupees 102.80 today can buy the same amount of goods and services as Rupees 110 will be able to buy after one year of 7% inflation. If you had only Rupees 100 today, your purchasing power would decrease relative to the bond's payout adjusted for inflation. The 7% inflation erodes the value, so while the nominal amount grows to 110, the real growth in purchasing power is only from 100 to 102.80.",
