          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The coupon rate and face value of a bond are contractual terms specified in the bond certificate at the time of issuance. They do not change when market interest rates fluctuate because they represent the fixed obligations of the issuer to the bondholder. The coupon rate determines the fixed annual or periodic interest payment, and the face value represents the principal amount that must be repaid at maturity. \n\nFor example, in the lecture, the professor discussed a 15-year bond issued with a face value of Rs. 1,000 and a coupon of Rs. 50 per year. Even if interest rates in the economy increased shortly after issuance, the coupon rate would remain at Rs. 50 per year and the face value would remain at Rs. 1,000. What would change is the bond's yield to maturity and its market price. If market rates rise, the yield to maturity on the bond increases, and since the bond's fixed coupon is now lower than the prevailing market rate, the bond's price would fall below its face value, trading at a discount. Conversely, if the coupon rate were higher than the new market rate, the price would rise above the face value, trading at a premium. \n\nThe key distinction is that while the coupon rate and face value are static variables defined at issuance, the bond price and yield to maturity are dynamic variables that adjust to reflect current economic conditions.",
