          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The exact Fisher equation is `1 + r_real = (1 + r_nominal) / (1 + i)`, which accounts for the compounding effect of inflation on the real value of returns. The approximation formula is `r_real ≈ r_nominal - i`, which simplifies calculations but ignores compounding.\n\nA financial analyst would prefer the exact formula when inflation rates are high or when precision is required for capital budgeting or valuation decisions. For example, if the nominal return is 12% and inflation is 8%, the approximation gives `12% - 8% = 4%`. However, the exact formula gives `(1.12 / 1.08) - 1 = 3.7%`. The difference, though seemingly small, can materialize in large-scale financial analysis. The approximation is suitable for quick mental checks or when inflation is low (typically below 5-6%).\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of exact vs. approximate formulas: 4 marks.\n- Correct identification of when to use each formula: 3 marks.\n- Accurate numerical illustration: 3 marks.",
