      "transcript_text": "Let us look at now some examples of trading in common stocks. Let us look at example one. The following table shows a portion of the limit order book for Boeing at some time on November 20, 2017. So, we have bid prices and shares on the one side and we have ask prices and shares on the other side. For example, if you look at the bid side, it tells you that somebody is willing to buy 1100 shares of Boeing at a price of dollar 263.76 each. Similarly, somebody else is willing to buy 100 shares of Boeing at a price of $263.73 each. On the other hand, if we look at the ask side, it tells us that somebody is willing to sell 200 shares at a price of $264.07 each and somebody else is willing to sell 1 share of Boeing at a price of $2. 64.07 at a price of $2.64.07 each and somebody else is willing to sell 1 share of Boeing at a price of $2.64.12. Now, if you look at this particular table, this is a portion of the limit order book. This is not the complete limit order book. If you look at the bid side, can you observe the order of the prices? Have the prices been arranged in any particular order? If you observe closely, you will realize that bid prices have been arranged in a descending order. The highest bid price appears at the top and then your bid prices come down as we go down the table. Similarly, if you look at the ask side, there the prices have been arranged in a price of $ in an ascending order. So, you have lowest ask price at the top followed by other prices in an ascending order. Why bid prices have been arranged in a descending order and ask prices in an ascending order? Can you think of a reason behind this arrangement? For example, when you place an order to sell shares, which bid prices relevant for you, you would be interested in selling your shares at as high a price as possible. In other words, you are interested in looking at the highest bid price. Similarly, when you want to buy shares, you are interested in spending as less money as possible. And therefore, you are interested in looking at the lowest ask prices. So, that is a reason as to why bid prices have been arranged in a descending order and therefore, you have the highest bid price appearing at the top. On the other hand, our ask prices have been arranged in an ascending order and the lowest ask price appears at the top. Now, let us look at these questions and answer them. Invester A places a market order to sell 100 shares. The question before you is at what price will her order be executed? Remember, it is a market sell order. First of all, for a sell order, which side will you look at? You will look at the bid side, because you will be able to sell shares only if somebody is willing to buy them. And investors are willing to buy them at bid prices. So, therefore, bid prices relevant for you when you want to sell your shares. You want to sell 100 shares. So, therefore, which bid prices relevant for you? For you, the highest bid price is relevant. And what is the highest bid price? It is $263.76. So, therefore, your order invester A's order will be executed at a price of $263.76. Let us look at the next part. It says, invester B places a market order to buy 100 shares. At what price will his order be executed? First of all, remember, it is a market buy order. And therefore, which side would you look at? You would look at the ask side. Why? Because you will be able to buy shares only if somebody is willing to sell them to you. And somebody is willing to sell them to you at the ask prices. However, because you want to buy, you would want to spend as little money as possible. So, therefore, you are interested in the lowest ask price. And what is the lowest ask price here? The lowest ask price here is $264.07. So, therefore, your order will be executed at a price of $264.07 each.",
