      "transcript_text": "Now let us come to another example. Here is a small part of the order book for Mesquite Foods. Again we have a portion of the limit order book. We have bid prices and quantities of shares on the one side. And we have asked prices and quantities of shares on the other side. Let us look at party. It says Geogina submits a market order to sell 100 shares. What price will she receive? Remember it is a market sell order and therefore this order will be executed immediately. At what price this order will get executed, at the highest bid price. At what is the highest bid price? The highest bid price is $103. So therefore Geogina will receive $103 each for each of her 100 shares. Now let us look at part B. Nonmen submits a market order to buy 400 shares. What is the maximum price per share that he will pay? Remember now nonmen wants to buy 400 shares. Therefore, which side of the limit order book will you look at? You will look at the ask side of the limit order book. Now if you look at the ask side, it tells you that somebody is willing to sell 200 shares at a price of $103.5 each and somebody else is willing to sell 200 shares at a price of $103.8 So therefore since nonmen wants to buy 400 shares, he will end up buying 200 shares at a price of $103.5 each and another 200 shares at a price of $103.8 each. And therefore what is the maximum price per share that nonmen is paying? He is paying a maximum price of $103.8 each. Let us look at part C. Carlos submits a limit order to buy 200 shares at $105 per share. The question is will this order get executed immediately? Now remember that it is not a market order. It is a limit order. A limit order may or may not get executed immediately. The execution of a limit order depends on the limit price. Now what is the limit price here? The limit price here is $105. So essentially it is a limit buy order and this order will get executed immediately only if you are able to get a price of $105 or lower. Now the question is are you getting a price of $105 or lower for 200 shares? The answer is yes because if you look at the ask site since this is a buy order, you will have to look at the ask site of the limit order book. And you observe that somebody is willing to sell 200 shares at a price of $103.5 each. So therefore this order will get executed immediately. Now remember that each of the three parts that we have talked about, they are independent.",
