      "video_id": "XIgHFgT-YKo",
      "duration_seconds": 638.2,
      "status": "completed",
      "notes_markdown": "# CF M03 C34 Valuing a Constant Growth Stock\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** When a firm just paid a dividend, the current stock price is the present value of all *future* dividends, calculated as $D_1 / (r - g)$. If the firm is *about to pay* a dividend today, the stock price equals that dividend plus the growing perpetuity value ($D_0 + P$), or equivalently, $c \\cdot (1+r) / (r-g)$ shifted one period forward.\n### Valuing a Constant Growth Stock Principles\n- **Growing Perpetuity Formula:** $P = D_1 / (r - g)$, where $D_1$ is the dividend at the end of Year 1.\n- **Key Distinction:** Only future dividends count toward stock price. A dividend just paid ($D_0$) is excluded from the PV calculation but added separately if the payment is imminent.\n- **Variable Shifts:** $D_1 = D_0 \\cdot (1+g)$; if the first cash flow is at $t=0$, the value at $t=-1$ is $c/(r-g)$, which must be multiplied by $(1+r)$ to find $P$ at $t=0$.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n- **Part A (Just Paid Dividend):** $D_0 = 1.35$, $g = 2.75\\%$, $r = 9.5\\%$.\n  - $D_1 = 1.35 \\times 1.0275 = 1.387125$\n  - $P = 1.387125 / (0.095 - 0.0275) = 1.387125 / 0.0675 = \\mathbf{20.55 \\text{ rupees}}$\n- **Part B (About to Pay Dividend):** Add $D_0$ back.\n  - $P = 1.35 + 20.55 = \\mathbf{21.90 \\text{ rupees}}$\n- **Alternative Method:** Value at $t=-1$ = $1.35 / (0.095 - 0.0275) = 20.00$. Shift to $t=0$: $20.00 \\times 1.095 = \\mathbf{21.90 \\text{ rupees}}$.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** You include the just-paid dividend ($D_0$) in the growing perpetuity formula $D_1/(r-g)$. → **Reality:** $D_0$ is a past cash flow; only future dividends ($D_1$ onward) are discounted. The formula uses $D_1 = D_0(1+g)$.\n2. **Trap:** You value the growing perpetuity at $t=0$ when the first dividend starts at $t=-1$. → **Reality:** The formula $c/(r-g)$ gives value one period *before* the first cash flow. You must shift it right by multiplying by $(1+r)$ to get the price at $t=0$.",
