      "transcript_text": "Now let us look at another example to find out stock value by fundamentals. Here is the example. A firm just paid an annual dividend of rupees 1.35 per share. It's a mature company. And its future dividends are expected to grow at 2.75 per year. The cost of equity is 9.5 per cent. The question before you is what is the firm's current stock price? Let us try to put these cash flows on a timeline to begin with. is just paid out a dividend of 1.35 rupees. And future dividends are expected to grow at 2.75 per year. So therefore dividend at the end of the first year will be 1.35 into 1.0275. dividend at the end of the second year will be 1.35 into 1.0275 square. And dividend at the end of the third year will be 1.35 into 1.0275 cube. And so on and so forth. We understand that current stock price is equal to present value of all expected dividends in the future. Now in this case as you can figure out, your dividends are growing at a constant rate. And therefore this particular pattern of dividends is nothing but a growing per per patchuity. And we also understand that the growth rating dividends is 2.75 per cent, which is less than our cost of equity, which is 9.5 per cent. Now I have a question before you. My question is would you count a dividend of 1.35 rupees, which has just been paid? Remember that current stock price is equal to present value of all expected future. dividends. I am highlighting the word future. So you have to take into account only future dividends because the person who is going to hold the stock or the share is going to receive only future dividends. So since we are looking at future dividends, therefore we will ignore the dividend of 1.35 rupees, which has just been paid. So we will essentially ignore this. And therefore what will our stock price be in the case of part A? It will simply be, dive 1 divided by r minus g because dividends are growing at a constant rate in per patchuity. And therefore we can apply the present value formula for a growing per patchuity. What is our dive 1? It is 1.35 into 1.025 divided by r, which is 0.095 minus g, which is 0.025. What does that give you? That gives you, if you solve it, that gives you 20.55 rupees as your stock price. Okay, now let us move to part B. What does part B say? It says, how is your answer going to change if the first sentence of the problem is replaced with the following. A firm is just about to pay an annual dividend of rupees 1.35 per share. So in this case, as you can figure out, 1.35 has to be taken into account because this is what you are going to receive today. This is no more a past dividend. So let us again put our cash flows on a timeline. Let us again put our cash flows on a timeline. You will receive now 1.35 rupees at the end of the first year, 1.35 into 1.025 at the end of the first year, 1.35 into 1.025 into 1.025 square at the end of the second year. And so on and so forth. As we discussed, now you will have to take into account a dividend of 1.35 rupees, which is going to be paid very soon. So therefore, what will your stock price be? So therefore, in the case of part B, you have an additional dividend of 1.35 rupees, which you are going to receive today. So what you have to do is to be able to find out the current stock price, you have to compute present value of 1.35 rupees that you are going to receive today. Plus present value of all other dividends, which you computed in your last step, which was 20.55. Present value of 1.35 rupees that you are going to receive today is 1.35 rupees itself. So therefore, your current stock price will be 1.35 plus 20.55, which will give you rupees 21.90. By the way, there is an alternative way to solve this problem. Let us see how. Again, let us put these cash flows on a timeline. You are going to receive 1.35 rupees at the end of the 0th year, which means today you will receive a dividend of 1.35 into 1.0275 at the end of the first year, 1.35 into 1.0275 square at the end of the second year. Okay, now as you understand, this particular pattern is a growing perpetuity. However, here, when is our first cash flow starting? Our first cash flow is starting at time t is equal to 0. Therefore, c divided by r minus g is going to be our value 1 period before your first cash flow. Yes, so therefore, your value at time t is equal to minus 1. That will be equal to c divided by r minus g. What is c here? 1.35. What is r here? It is 0.095 minus g here. What is g here? 0.0275. Okay, if you solve this, you will get rupees. However, remember, this is value at time t is equal to minus 1. You have to find out present value, which means value at time t is equal to 0, which means what will you do? You will have to shift this value 1 period to the right. So therefore, you will be multiplying this value, which you have computed at t is equal to minus 1 with 1 plus r. What is this value? 20 rupees and what is 1 plus r? It is 1.095. What will that give you? Again, you will get 21.90 as your answer. So therefore, whether you solve using this method or you solve using the previous method, you are going to get the same answer.",
