          "model_answer": "The classification of working capital changes follows a consistent cause-and-effect logic based on the firm's cash position.\n\n*   **Increase in Working Capital (Year-over-Year)**: This is an incremental cash outflow. When working capital increases, it means the firm must invest additional funds into inventories and receivables to support the project's operations. This tie-up of funds represents a cash outflow at the project's initiation or during its life.\n\n*   **Decrease in Working Capital (Year-over-Year)**: This is an incremental cash inflow. When working capital decreases, it means some of the previously invested working capital is being released back to the firm. This release of funds generates a cash inflow.\n\n*   **Recovery of Working Capital at Project End**: This is an incremental cash inflow. Once the project terminates, the firm no longer needs the inventories and receivables built up during the project. The ability to recover the initial investment in these assets generates a cash inflow at the termination of the project.\n\nThe underlying principle is that any change in the level of working capital represents a cash movement directly tied to the project's operational requirements.\n\n**Marking Rubric (Total 10 Marks):**\n*   **Core Conceptual Understanding (5 Marks):** Correctly identifying the direction of the cash flow (inflow vs. outflow) for each of the three scenarios described in the question. Demonstrating understanding that working capital changes are incremental.\n*   **Analytical Application (5 Marks):** Providing a clear logical explanation for why an increase is an outflow and a decrease is an inflow, referencing the 'tie-up' and 'release' of funds. Correctly identifying recovery as an inflow linked to project termination.",
