      "transcript_text": "Let us look at another problem on identifying incremental cash flows. So, you have to tell which of the following should be treated as incremental cash flows when deciding whether to invest in a new manufacturing plant. It is already owned by the company, but existing buildings would need to be demolished. First part, the market value of the site and existing buildings. Is it an incremental cash flow? You understand that you have an existing resource and this existing resource has an opportunity cost. If this resource is not used in this project, this can be used somewhere else and therefore, when you decide to use the site and existing buildings, you cannot use them for any other thing. In other words, this opportunity cost of the site and existing buildings represents an incremental cash outflow. Next one, demolition costs and site clearance. Clearly, you will have to incur these costs and therefore, these are incremental cash outflow. The next one, the cost of a new access road put in last year. Now, this new access road which you have put in, last year, this is the cost of a new access road. This is essentially a sunk cost. You cannot recover this money. This is irreversible and therefore, this is not an incremental cash flow. The next one, lost earnings on other products due to executive time spent on new facility. What is this? This is clearly a negative incidental effect of taking up the project and therefore, it is an incremental cash outflow. The next one, a proportion of the cost of leasing the precedence jet airplane. What is that? This is merely an allocation of existing overhead costs and therefore, it is not an incremental cash flow. The next one, future depreciation of the new plant, depreciation is merely allocation of cost. It is not a cash expense and therefore, it is not an incremental cash flow. Next one, the reduction in the corporation's tax bill resulting from tax depreciation of the new plant. So, essentially, because of this depreciation expense, the company is going to save in taxes and therefore, the savings in taxes represents an incremental cash inflow as a consequence of taking up the making of this project. The next one, the initial investment in inventories of raw materials. What is it? It is clearly an incremental cash outflow because you have to invest some money today in inventories of raw materials. The last one, money already spent on the engineering design of the new plant. What is that? This money has already been spent. Now, irrespective of whether you accept or reject the project, you are not going to recover this money and therefore, it is a sunk cost. And, some cost as we discussed previously, it is not an incremental cash flow.",
