      "transcript_text": "Now that we have OCF, can we not compute our net cash flow or free cash flow from the project? Yes, we can. You understand that your free cash flow or your net cash flow in any year, it is simply easy. equal to your OCF for that year plus cash flow from capital investment and disposal plus cash flow from changes in capital. Now, let us compute cash flow from capital investment and disposal. Today, how much amount are you increasing for your capital investment? You are increasing an amount of 12,000 rupees today and this is clearly a cash outflow. So, therefore, your cash flow from capital investment today is negative 12,000 rupees. However, if you look at the problem, what did the problem tell you? It told you that this machinery can be dismantled and sold for net proceeds estimated at rupees 1,949 at the end of year 7. Now, therefore, our question is what is going to be your cash flow from disposal in year 7? So, let us look at cash flow we know that our cash flow from our cash flow from capital investment in year 0 at the end of year 0 is negative 12,000. What is our cash flow from capital investment? In year 0 at the end of year 0 is negative 12,000, what is our cash flow from capital investment? disposal of our machinery, it is going to occur at the end of year 7. However, as we discussed previously, what should it be equal to? Whatever is your salvage value? So, whatever is your salvage value? If from the salvage value you subtract taxes that you have to pay on the sale of this equipment that will give you cash flow from disposal net of taxes. Now, what is your salvage value? It is given to you as 1 9 4 9 rupees. Now, how will you compute taxes? Your taxes will be equal to your tax rate into salvage value minus minus net book value. So, this is going to be 1 9 4 9 minus tax rate which is 21 percent into salvage value which is 1 9 4 9 minus net book value. Your net book value at the end of year 7 is 0. And therefore, if you solve this, how much amount will you get? You will get rupees 1 5 4 0. So, you have computed cash flow from capital investment and disposal. Your capital investment has occurred today which is the end of year 0 and your disposal has occurred at the end of year 7 and you have computed cash flow from disposal at the end of year 7. Now, we need to compute cash flow from changes in networking capital. We have to compute cash flow from changes in networking capital. And let us say that we are computing this entry for year 1. So, therefore, cash flow from changes in networking capital. We understand that this is going to be negative of change in networking capital for year 1. What is this equal to? Change in networking capital in year 1 equals networking capital of year 1 minus networking capital of year 0. What is your networking capital for year 1? Your networking capital for year 1 is given to us as 550. What is your networking capital for year 0? It is 0. So, therefore, your cash flow from changes in networking capital in year 1 comes out to be negative 550 rupees. In a similar way, can you not compute cash flow from changes in networking capital for all other years? You can. And therefore, we can also compute our net cash flow. So, therefore, our net cash flow for year 1 that is equal to OCF for year 1 plus cash flow from changes in networking capital. Networking capital in year 1 plus cash flow from capital investment and disposal. We computed operating cash flow in year 1 just a while ago. What was that? Operating cash flow in year 1 if you recall was negative 1,56 rupees. So, it is negative 1,56 rupees. What is cash flow from changes in networking capital? Cash flow from changes in networking capital which we just computed is negative 550 rupees. What is your cash flow from capital investment and disposal? In year 1, you have neither made any investment in capital nor you have disposed of your equipment. So, therefore, your cash flow from changes in capital investment and disposal is 0. So, therefore, if you sum this up, what will you get as your net cash flow in year 1? You will get it as negative 2,1,1,6 rupees. In a similar way, you can compute net cash flow for each of the years as shown here. And now what you have to do is you have to discount your net cash flow using your nominal discount rate which is given to you as 20 percent. So, therefore, our project NPV. It is simply going to be negative 1,5, 1,6,0 minus 2,1,1,6 divided by 1.2 plus 2,8, double 0 divided by 1.2 square plus 7,7,1,2, divided by 1.2 cube plus 13,0,8,4 divided by 1.2 raised to the power 4 plus 11,7,8,4 divided by 1.2 raised to the power 5 plus 6 8 3 2 divided by 1.2 raised to the power 6 and finally we have our last cash flow of 3 5 4 2 divided by 1.2 raised to the power 7. And if you solve for this you will get plus 3 8 0 6 rupees as your NPV. And now what decision should we take based on this? We should essentially accept this project why? Because the project has a positive NPV and we understand that if we accept this project this is going to add to shareholders wealth and therefore you should accept this project.",
