          "model_answer": "**Core Differences:**\n1. **Range**: Covariance is unbounded, ranging from $-\\infty$ to $+\\infty$, while correlation is strictly bounded between $-1$ and $+1$.\n2. **Units**: Covariance has units derived from the product of the two variables' units, making it sensitive to the scale of the assets. Correlation is unitless (a pure number), making it scale-invariant.\n3. **Information**: Covariance only tells us the **direction** of the linear relationship (positive or negative). Correlation tells us both the **direction** and the **strength** of the linear relationship.\n4. **Relationship**: The covariance of a variable with itself is exactly its variance ($\\sigma_{xx} = \\text{Variance of } x$).\n\n**Why Correlation is Preferred:**\nBecause covariance depends on the units of measurement, a covariance of 5 between Infosys and Reliance is not directly comparable to a covariance of 5 between Tata and Adani if the stock prices or return scales differ. Correlation removes the unit effect, allowing a portfolio manager to objectively compare which pair of assets has a stronger linear relationship, which is essential for effective diversification strategies.",
