      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about our portfolio. Suppose we have a portfolio and this portfolio comprises of two stocks, stock 1 and stock 2. We have to find out the variance of the portfolio. The variance of returns on this portfolio. How can we find that out? To find that out in an easy manner, what we simply do in this case is that we construct a 2 by 2 matrix. So let us do that. So we have stock 1 in the portfolio and let us say the weight of stock 1 in the portfolio. So the portfolio is x1. We have stock 2 in the portfolio and let us say the weight of stock 2 in the portfolio is x2. So we have stock 1 with the weight of x1 and we have stock 2 with the weight of x2. Now our portfolio variance which I will denote it by sigma p square. How can we find that out? So here if you look at this matrix, so here we have diagonal terms and here we will have of diagonal terms. So let us talk about our diagonal terms first. The diagonal terms are going to be the variance terms. In this case it will be x1 square sigma 1 square. Similarly here the second diagonal term will be x2. x2 square and sigma 2 square. What are going to be our of diagonal terms? Our of diagonal terms will be x1, x2 into sigma 1 2. Here also our of diagonal term will be x1, x2 into sigma 1 2. So if you notice our diagonal terms are the variance terms and our of diagonal terms are covariance terms. And our portfolio variance is simply going to be the sum of all these terms. We can say that our portfolio variance equals x1 square sigma 1 square plus x2 square sigma 2 square plus 2 x1 x2 sigma 1 2. Now we know that what is rho 1 2? We understand that rho 1 2 which represents the correlation coefficient between the returns of stock 1 and stock 2. That equals sigma 1 2 divided by sigma 1 into sigma 2. So this implies sigma 1 2 equals rho 1 2 into sigma 1 into sigma 2. Now if you substitute the value of sigma 1 2 from equation 2 in equation 1. What do we get? We get sigma p square as x1 square sigma 1 square plus x2 square sigma 2 square plus 2 x1 x2 rho 1 2 sigma 1 sigma 2. Now if rho 1 2 is plus 1 if the correlation coefficient between the returns on the two stocks is plus 1. What will happen? It will imply that sigma p square equals x1 square sigma 1 square plus x2 square sigma 2 square plus 2 x1 x2 sigma 1 sigma 2. What will this be equal to? This will be equal to x1 sigma 1 plus x2 sigma 2 whole square. So this implies that our sigma p will be equal to x1 sigma 1 plus x2 sigma 2. It tells you that portfolio standard deviation is weighted average of the standard deviations. Of individual securities in the portfolio only when correlation coefficient between the returns on the securities is plus 1. In a similar way what happens when rho 1 2 is minus 1? What will happen? In that case our sigma p square equals x1 square sigma 1 square plus x2 square sigma 2 square minus 2 x1 x2 sigma 1 sigma 2. This implies that sigma p square equals x1 sigma 1 minus x2 sigma 2 whole square. So this implies that sigma p equals x1 sigma 1 minus x2 sigma 2. Here since standard deviation cannot be negative therefore we will have to take a modulus of this particular term. Now let me ask you now if not just rho 1 2 is equal to minus 1 but also if x1 sigma 1 is equal to x2 sigma 2. sigma 2 what will happen? In that case you will observe that your sigma p will be 0. When your correlation coefficient between the returns on the two stocks is negative 1 and when you can set the weights of the securities such that x1 sigma 1 is equal to x2 sigma 2. What will happen to sigma p? It will become 0. However, remember that the possibility of rho 1 2 being equal to minus 1 is almost impossible in real life. And therefore if correlation coefficient is never equal to negative 1 in that case our sigma p will also never be equal to 0 in real life.",
