      "transcript_text": "Now, let us look at the benefit of diversification. Suppose you add a new stock to the portfolio. Let us see what will happen to your portfolio return and portfolio standard deviation when you add another stock to your portfolio return. Now, let us look at the stock to the existing portfolio. Assume that you have already made an investment in stock 1 and stock 2 as given in the previous example. Which means that you have already invested $55 in stock 1 and $45 in stock 2. Now, you invest $1.00 in stock 3. Whose expected return is 25% with an annualized standard deviation of 22%. Assume a correlation coefficient of 0.5 between the existing portfolio and stock 3. The question before you is what will be the portfolio return and risk between the existing portfolio? Now, so here you already have your existing portfolio and this portfolio comprises of stocks 1 and 2. Now, to existing portfolio what you have done is you have added stock 3. So, now first of all what was your expected return on existing portfolio comprising of stocks 1 and 2. We just computed it as 14.5%. What is the expected return? The expected return on stock 3 it is given to us as 25%. Now, let us look at standard deviation of returns on existing portfolio. We computed it as 17.04% and for stock 3 the standard deviation of returns. is given to us as 22%. Now, let us look at weight. If you recall you invested $100 in existing portfolio comprising of stocks 1 and 2 and you have invested another $100 in stock 3. So, therefore, your weight of existing portfolio will be 0.5 and weight of stock 3 in your new portfolio will also be 0.5. Now, here the correlation coefficient is given to us as 0.5. Can we not compute new portfolio? Standard deviation if you compute it it will come out as 16.95%. Okay, let us also look at new portfolio. What will that be? That will be weighted average of your existing portfolio return and stock 3 which means 0.5 into 14.5% plus 0.5% into 25%. What does that give you? That gives you 19.75%. Okay, now what do you observe? What was the expected return on your existing portfolio? It was 14.5%. What is the expected return on your new portfolio? It is 19.75%. So, the expected return on your new portfolio is higher than that on your existing portfolio. What do you observe about standard deviation? You observe that the standard deviation of returns on your existing portfolio was 17.04%. But, the standard deviation of returns on your new portfolio is 16.95%. What is that telling you? So, you are having lower risk in the case of your new portfolio and yet higher returns. And what made it possible? Well, this was possible simply because of diversification. Of course, it may not always be the case. It is only happening in this problem that we are having a lower risk and yet higher return. These things may happen sometimes and this is attributable to diversification. Now, even a little diversification can provide a substantial reduction in variability. So, for example, if you plot portfolio standard deviation on number of stocks in the portfolio, you will observe that your average standard deviation of portfolios containing different number of stocks, it will keep decreasing as you increase the number of stocks in the portfolio. You can get most of the benefit of diversification with relatively few stocks in the portfolio. Now, diversification works because prices of different stocks do not move exactly together. In other words, this works because price changes are less than perfectly correlated. In other words, your correlation coefficient is less than 1.",
