      "transcript_text": "Now let us look at some problems. Here is the problem. You are given the information about three stocks. So we have stocks 1, 2 and 3. You are given with standard deviation of returns on each of the stocks. You are given with the beta of each of the three stocks. Now the question before you is which stock is safest for a diversified investor. You understand that if an investor is diversified. Another is holding is only carrying market risk because specific risk has been diversified away. And therefore what should be the measure of risk for a diversified portfolio? It should be beta. So therefore that stock is safest for a diversified investor which has the lowest beta. of 0.4 and therefore this stock is safest for a diversified investor. I must also mention that in corporate finance we assume that investors are diversified and therefore the measure of risk which is important for diversified investors is beta. Let us look at part B. It says which stock is safest for an undiversified investor who puts all her money in one of these stocks. So now if the investor is undiversified then the measure of risk which is important for that investor is going to be standard deviation of returns. And if we look at the three stocks the stock with the lowest standard deviation is going to be the safest stock for an undiversified investor. And here stock 1 has the lowest standard deviation of 12% among all the three stocks. And therefore stock 1 is safest for an undiversified investor as well. Now let us look at part C. It says consider a portfolio with equal investments in each stock. What would be this portfolio's beta? You remember that the portfolio's beta is weighted average of the beta's of individual stocks in the portfolio. So therefore let us call beta of this portfolio as beta p. So beta p is equal to weight of stock 1 into beta of stock 1 plus weight of stock 2 in the portfolio into beta of stock 2 plus weight of stock 3 into beta of stock 3. Okay now what is weight of stock 1 in the portfolio? If you look at the problem it says you have made equal investments in each of the three stocks. So therefore weight of each of the stocks in the portfolio is simply 1 by 3. So 1 by 3 into beta of stock 1 which is 0.4 plus weight of stock 2 which is 1 by 3 again into beta of stock 2 which is 1.4 plus weight of stock 3 which is again 1 by 3 into beta of stock 3 which is 0.6. And if you compute it you will get pointed as the beta of your portfolio.",
