      "notes_markdown": "# What is a Family Business?\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** A family business is defined by three interlocking characteristics, ownership, management, and succession, not merely by the familial relationship of founders. Being listed or having related founders does not automatically qualify a business as a family business.\n\n### Key Characteristics\n\n- **Ownership:** Shareholding held by the family; the family must hold a significant equity stake.\n- **Management:** Operations run according to the family’s vision; decision-making reflects family priorities.\n- **Succession:** Intent to build a business that lasts across generations; planned transfer of leadership.\n\n### Examples Analyzed\n\n- **Zerodha:** Founded by two brothers (Nikhil & Nithin). Related founders alone do not make it a family business without the three characteristics.\n- **Mamaearth:** Husband-and-wife founding team. Same principle, foundership ties are necessary but insufficient.\n- **Byju’s:** Three relatives (Byju Raveendran, Divya Gokulnath, Riju Raveendran). Demonstrates that more relatives do not automatically trigger the classification.\n- **TCS:** Listed company. Ownership structure (promoter holding) determines family business status, not listing status.\n- **EaseMyTrip:** Listed company started by the Pitti brothers. Listing status does not preclude a family business designation if family ownership and management persist.\n\n### Mechanic\n\n`Ownership (Family Shareholding) → Management (Family Vision) → Succession (Generational Intent) → Family Business Classification`\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n\n1. **Trap:** If founders are related, the business is automatically a family business.  \n   **Reality:** Familial ties alone do not satisfy the ownership, management, and succession criteria.\n\n2. **Trap:** A listed company can never be a family business.  \n   **Reality:** Listing status is irrelevant; family-owned listed firms (e.g., TCS, EaseMyTrip) qualify if family ownership and management endure.\n\n3. **Trap:** Only businesses with many relatives qualify as family businesses.  \n   **Representation:** The number of relatives is not a defining factor; even two founders can constitute a family business if the three characteristics are met.",
