      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the four-quadrant classification model for family businesses discussed in the lecture. Using real-world examples from the lecture, illustrate each quadrant and explain how the model challenges the simplistic assumption that family ownership and family management always coincide.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The four-quadrant classification model classifies family firms based on the intersection of two dimensions: (1) the extent of family ownership and (2) the nature of management (family vs. professional). The four possible quadrants are: (i) High family ownership + Family management, (ii) High family ownership + Professional management, (iii) Low family ownership + Family management, and (iv) Low family ownership + Professional management.\n\n*Quadrant I (High ownership, Family management): Illustrated by Reliance, where the Ambani family owns the company and the next generation holds family posts as directors, actively running the conglomerate. Also Wipro, where the family owns and actively engages in management.\n*Quadrant II (High ownership, Professional management): Illustrated by TCS, where Tata Trusts own about 70% of the company, but the management is completely professional. Mahindra is another example where the family owns a significant proportion but it is run by professionals like Anish.\n*Quadrant III (Low ownership, Family management): Illustrated by Apollo Hospitals, where the Reddy family no longer has a controlling stake (does not have 51%), but they are actively running every arm of the business, having diversified into clinics, pharmacies, and a knowledge school. Operational control remains with the family despite lacking majority shareholding.\n*Quadrant IV (Low ownership, Professional management): Illustrated by Max Financial and Suzlon, where family shareholding is low. However, the lecturer noted they may exert control through board nominees.\n\nThis model challenges the simplistic assumption that family ownership and family management always coincide because it demonstrates that these are two independent dimensions. A family can own a large percentage but hire professional managers (TCS), or own a small percentage but retain operational control through influence and board representation (Apollo Hospitals, Max Financial). The key takeaway is that control and influence often outweigh the percentage shareholding number alone.\n\nRubric:\n- Correct identification and description of all four quadrants: 3 marks\n- Use of specific lecture examples to illustrate each quadrant: 3 marks\n- Explanation of the independence of ownership and management dimensions: 2 marks\n- Clear articulation of how the model challenges the alignment assumption: 2 marks",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Correct identification and description of all four quadrants",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Use of specific lecture examples to illustrate each quadrant",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Explanation of the independence of ownership and management dimensions",
              "marks": 2
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Clear articulation of how the model challenges the alignment assumption",
              "marks": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
