          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "Ownership control refers to the family's right to define the purpose, values, and direction of the firm, and to embed those values into culture. Strategic control, by contrast, is about the long-term direction and scope of the firm. The professor explained that strategic control is shaped by both market forces and the family's own goals and inherent strengths. Using the Reliance example, the family's strategic direction evolved from petroleum refining to tech democratization. Ownership control provides the foundation, but strategic control is the active shaping of the firm's competitive path over time.",
