      "notes_markdown": "# Theories and Frameworks: Systems Theory\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** A family business operates as a dynamic system where the interconnected components of **Family, Ownership, and Management** must align; the business functions as an open system driven by market feedback, while the family often operates as a closed system guided by legacy and emotion.\n### Systems Theory And Family Business\n- **Organisational Components:** Inputs (suppliers, raw materials) → Transformation process (technology, people, machinery) → Output (finished goods/services) → Feedback loop (customer input reshaping raw materials or process).\n- **Open vs. Closed Systems:** Open systems interact with the external environment (market, customers, suppliers), adapting dynamically to avoid stagnation. Closed systems interact only internally, leading to misalignment with market demands and potential decline.\n- **Family Business Triad:** The family business system uniquely blends three interdependent groups:\n  - **Family:** Often a closed system focused on internal dynamics, emotions, and legacy.\n  - **Ownership:** Defines shareholder value and the desired future direction of the business.\n  - **Management:** An open system interacting with customers and the external market.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n*No formulas or numerical calculations were presented in this lecture.*\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Viewing the family business as a single, unified entity rather than a system of interdependent components. → **Reality:** It is a triad of Family, Ownership, and Management, each with distinct values and incentives that must be aligned.\n2. **Trap:** Assuming the family and the business operate identically within the system. → **Reality:** The business is an open system driven by market forces, while the family often functions as a closed system focused on internal dynamics and legacy.\n3. **Trap:** Believing that feedback from customers only affects the output stage. → **Reality:** In systems theory, feedback is a reverse loop that can reshape inputs (e.g., raw materials) and the transformation process, not just the final output.",
