          "model_answer": "The family businesses of the License Raj era (1951-1991) engaged in active adaptation rather than passive benefit. They strategically lobbied the licensing bureaucracy and cultivated political relationships to secure scarce industrial licenses and import quotas, which led to the creation of sprawling, unrelated diversification conglomerate structures. However, this scale attracted public and political concern over concentrated economic power. In response to the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices (MRTP) Act of 1969, which targeted large family-controlled houses like the Tatas and Birlas, many groups chose a structural fragmentation strategy. Rather than growing a single large entity that would cross asset thresholds and invite regulatory scrutiny, they fragmented ownership across many smaller, branch-controlled entities. This structural response shaped the ownership patterns that remain visible in some business families today.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of License Raj adaptation: 3 marks\n- Identification of lobbying/political cultivation mechanism: 2 marks\n- Description of MRTP Act response and fragmentation strategy: 3 marks\n- Clear articulation of the tension between scale and regulation: 2 marks",
