      "transcript_text": "The next phase is the liberalization from 1991 to the present. The 1991 reforms opened India to foreign competition and capital. Rather than being swept aside, many family houses use the transition proactively, restructuring group structure and diversity, non-core businesses, professionalizing management and in some cases, splitting long-standing joint family holdings into separately run branches which were better able to move fast. And this is well-documented divisions occurred across several large Marwadi and Punjabi origin trading family conglomerates in the 1990s and 2000s. A new cohort of family founded, globally oriented remote groups entered post 1991. Reliance Industries founded in 1966 by the Uruguay Ambani and now India's largest private sector enterprise expanded aggressively into petrochemicals, telecom and retail post-deliberization era. Similarly, Wipro under ASEAN-Premji pivoted the family's vegetable oil business into IT services. Starting in the 1980s, an actively positioning the firm, well ahead of the software export boom that liberalization would later accelerate. Second generation and third generation leaders commonly educated abroad, raised in cosmopolitan urban settings, are now visibly and deliberately reshaping legacy businesses with digital first strategies. ESG commitments and modern governance structures actively courting international institutional capital rather than relying solely on family and community sourced funds. Another important aspect is the post 1991 Indian IPO wave and its effect on family control. Liberalization did not just open India to foreign competition. It also opened Indian family businesses to public capital markets at a scale not previously possible. Many family control businesses are not businesses that had operated as only private closely held concerns for generations conducted their first public share offerings in the 1990s and 2000s. Deliberately trading some ownership dilution were access to growth capital, brand credibility and not incidentally an implicit governance discipline signal to lenders and partners that the family was willing to operate under public disclosure norms. This period is the direct origin of some of the issues that we will delve into later regarding the promoter holding and set in motion the gradual and still ongoing decline in average promoter share holding visible in that data even as absolute family well and business scale grew dramatically over the same period.",
