      "transcript_text": "1 more point which leads in favour of the family forms is how crisis is managed. The study which was conducted in which they understood as to how family forms performed during the 2008 crisis. The one where you know, subprime crisis called crash at Lehman Brothers and then it went on to the broader market from there. And how non-family companies performed during the same period. So they studied a period between 2006 and 2010 before two years before the crisis and two years after the crisis. And continued the crisis still about 2000. To understand how these two companies performed. Again the result is that founder-led forms invested significantly less in value destroying activities whether it's margin acquisitions or anything else. They maintained a lower capital expenditure during the downturn. Had better access to credit markets than non-family firms. It also reflects lenders, greater confidence in the reputation and relationship capital we talked about. what socioeconomic capital of long control borrowers. The second crisis that we said is a COVID-19 pandemic. This again was huge, it impacted worldwide. And this is afforded another area where researchers could study the difference between a family form and a non-family form. did again the study between 2018 and 2021. Repandemic and a little bit of post-pandemic. They continued across multiple countries across the world. He finding family firms showed higher financial performance and non-family firms throughout the COVID pandemic. Even after controlling for as in the research controlling for pre-pandemic business conditions. The effect was stronger especially for firms which stronger involvement in management. And this is something even Anderson and Reeb formed during their study. And this is true for when both management and ownership are together. Actually better governance during crisis and better connections during crisis that the family firms had helped a lot. A related finding is that during COVID are also performance was concentrated among the younger and smaller especially non-industrial sector firms. Crisis periods specifically rather than ordinary years provide the best data actually to check out the alignment theory effect that we spoke about earlier. And every single time we are finding that the family form comes out on top. In a very ordinary year family firms which is more slightly conservative in its capital allocation and stronger lender relationship may only show a marginal difference in its return of capital. But when a crisis hits the two competing behaviors of alignment effect theory and a lower panic driven environment that we have and stronger access to credit these become far more consequential. And that is clearly visible because when a crisis hits family firms into do better. Because the downside scenarios that are there possibly their bankruptcy or forced sale or credit fees that a firm's governance structure can protect actually helps. They actually materialize because of the stronger connections that family businesses have. McKinsey and company and he spoke about a study that they did and this is something they published in 2024. They studied a lot of Indian family owned businesses. That is very critical about 300 Indian family owned businesses they studied. And each with a revenue of at least 2000 crores at least once in the past five years and these are large ones therefore listed once 300 of them. And this ensures this kind of a sample size ensures that these are established firms and just not something which is established last year or just last few years. What they found is family owned businesses roughly 2.3% in terms of higher revenue growth than non family firms for the same period. And between 2012 and 22 shareholder returns for family owned business where roughly twice has high as non family businesses. That is a 10 year window. So in a 10 year window you usually have more than one market cycle and not just one bull run. In spite of it this was a result that was seen. And there is a clearer separation that they found between family owned business decision making and non family owned. Family owned businesses seem to be lot more disciplined less sentiment driven and capital allocation is across business units. Very, very formalized board structures do help not necessarily but there certainly seem to be helping. Family firms also demonstrated a willingness to exit or restructure under performing legacy businesses rather than preserving them simply for sentimental. My father started it, my grandfather started it so I should still hold on to it. That is not really what the McKinsey study found.",
