      "transcript_text": "The second right is the right to decide. This involves decisions regarding the business and how much of this decision making power will the family delegate one to other family members and second also to non-family members. It also decides the owner-con decisions are made. One is who decides and the second is how they can decide. Can it be decided by the majority within the company? Does it require board intervention? It only involves certain one person who can take a decision again depending on all of this the decision the methodology itself can change. But yes, the owner still has the right to decide the method of even the decisions will be made. For example, if you think about Mariko, Harsh Marivala as the owner, exercise this right when you said that it will be a professionally run firm. So, all the decision making regarding the business is devolved onto the CEO on the professional management team while the owner retains a strategic board level oversight. So, if you take example of a larger conglomerate like Tata Sons, they are into like we said multiple businesses. Each one of them is no longer controlled by anyone in the family. The TCS is controlled by Ench and the Shakeran who runs TCS completely but reports to the board which is considered as Tata Sons. Tata Sons similarly has a stake in every of their businesses. Each one managed professionally. Certain decisions alone have to come to the Tata Sons board. Rest of it can be taken by the individual board of these companies. Yes. And on every individual board there is a Tata Sons nominee but decisions have been devolved more decentralized manner. Yes. Again a choice the majority owner which is Tata Sons has made. And irrespective of how the ownership structure is, there is always going to be this kind of an influence that the family relationships and those linkages bring into the decision making with regard to what the family does or cannot do. And therefore, I think to kind of strengthen both the family and the business within the family business. You are on one side of looking at financial performance and you are also looking at family harmony cohesiveness. It is very important to get clarity around how and who will make these decisions and the decision making structures themselves. And there are a lot of these very powerful examples that we see out there. Yeah. For example, I remember this Dr. Eddy's labs again family owned business. They have distinctly created rules. They have something called a family council. We will discuss that later. But the family council has set rules on how these decisions can be made. Who will take the decisions? And there are family aspirations on one side. There are decisions regarding the company board. All these governance actions are codified well inside the family council. Yes. And you could also have examples like the Burman family, the Dabur where they have very clear rules about the families non-engagement on the day-to-day management. And all the commercial decisions are taken by the professionals and then they come to the board. So it is a purely professional run but family owned kind of a structure. Perhaps the other big example that we should talk about is a GMR group. Yes. They were one of the earliest. I have heard saying have a family constitution. Yes. And I think that is the case study in many places where how they have actually created the family constitution. The process of making itself was fairly elaborate. Yes. More importantly, they have set the rules on for example what conditions be taken. Who comes into the business? What qualifications are needed to for someone to enter the business for employment? So yes those rules are how many disputes will be resolved. So all of this protect both the family and the business. Similarly when we look at the Mahindra group, it is professionally run while the owner still does provide strategic leadership and oversight. So the operational decision making is still with the professionals. And these kind of rules are reflection of the owners right to decide as to how the decisions will be made and who will make them.",
