      "transcript_text": "So, say what would be your recommendation for income pension on how they plan, think through this process of succession. So, there are multiple things which broadly we can put under four different things that an income and can do to make themselves ready and strengthen the succession process. First, the income and needs to prepare themselves and the business both emotionally and financially and as well as on the governance aspects. And when you prepared the business and yourself financially, emotionally and in the governance structure, then you are in a better position to start planning and preparing for moving out and phasing yourself out of the business. So, you need to understand the requirements across all the three things that you have basically circles on the ownership circle and the management circle and on the family circle. You understand all the requirements, what to get the succession successful. And based on those requirements, you assess whether there are any particular gaps and if there are, then how do you fill those gaps and therefore, you are preparing this exit across all the three circles. That is the first part of it. So, you prepare yourself first and then you focus on preparing the success. Yes, reading the success is one part of it, but true prepare yourself prepare the success and prepare the business and the family. All of these elements need to come together and that is very important to understand as a starting point. And then you have to look out for who is possible as success. That is extremely important. And here there are key trade-offs. I need to first of all make the choice whether I want to transition the management and whom do I want to transition the ownership. Because within the management, I still have the option of choosing within a professional manager versus the family. Yes. So, whom do I want to shift the succession on the management dimension. We want a professional or do I want to read it into the family. And ownership control is the same thing. And ownership control, how do I plan it out? Do I look for a co-personal kind of an agreement? Do I look for a full transfer etcetera. And among my different children, I still have the choice which child. Why? I need to consider their willingness and their ability and both of these are extremely crucial. So, I am assessing the success or are they ready for this or not. And then I also need to understand the family business and the governance structure, readiness to support that particular successor that I choose. Because in the first stage, well I am preparing all of that. I need to now align that based on my assessment of the success. And the third stage is to read the successes. I need to groom them well in advance. I may begin with some options like when I am assessing the success or it could be my first turn. It could be my second turn. But I need to get them involved across the three different circles and see how they perform. And it to assess them or evaluate them over a period of time. This is really the place where they have to start letting go. In the first two parts that you spoke about, it is more preparing themselves for a succession and identifying one person. So far they have not let go of anything. Yes. It is still in charge completely. Yes. Now you are moving to a stage where they have to let go of something. Maybe not everything. They will not let go of everything. They will test the waters with their choice of successor. Whether they are really capable of delivering what we thought, they will deliver. So, you are talking about that stage. Exactly. And a very important part for the incumbent in this entire part, in this entire succession is, are you giving independent decision making rights to your potential successors? And this is extremely crucial from an incumbent point of view. When you are reading and grooming your successor, you need to be able to give them that independence to make the decision and own the consequences. And therefore, as an incumbent that is at on you to get ready to do that. And look for possibly early wins to stabilize and make yourself comfortable in the decision. It is not, I am still not talking about it from the successor's point of view. But as an incumbent, when you start looking for early wins as evidence, you know that I can now slowly start facing myself out of the business. So there are two ways to look at it. You are saying that early wins will help. But is it the incumbent's responsibility to set up the successor for a win? Sometimes, yes. When you have the supporting structure, think about it like you have a team of professionals to back you up. At that time, your decision making is not for yourself to make a right. You are still depending upon that entire. So you need to know what is a role you are going to play in the decision making. You don't as a successor, you should not use the decision making responsibility from a very effective professional team that was set up exactly to help you. Exactly. That was what it was set up for. But if you want to prove yourself by taking out their responsibilities, you are weakening the organization. You need to assess whether your successor is actually ready to play the role that was designed and crafted for them. So if the incumbent sees early wins, then you are saying that he probably will move towards you. He has a confidence to now start moving out. He gets ready. Yes. Okay. I know that the business is in safe hands. As we said that the family business in many cases, in fact in most cases for the incumbent is like the first child. Yes. So I want to know that it is now in safe hands and the early wins are very crucial evidence for that. If you have early wins, you will move forward with a formal transition itself. Yes. And this transition is across all the three circles. I need to send a signal to the management dimension. I need to send a business dimension. I need to send a signal to the family dimension and the ownership circle. All the three circles, this transition has to happen. And these may be faster in certain dimensions and slower in some of the others. But it has to be smooth and coordinated across all the three circles. This makes it easier for the incumbent to face themselves out of the family business. You also have to make the success of the public face in terms of the stake holders who are beyond these three circles. Correct. And that is over a period of time. You know, you start projecting them. You send them to even to send them to family functions. You make them handle the important conversations. And slowly and slowly, you know the confidence in the others. Take holders also builds up that we have a credible face for the family business in the next generation. This succession happens. So, this kind of radius the incumbent to face themselves out of the business smoother and more effectively than a harsh and disturbed process.",
