        {
          "id": 6,
          "question": "A family firm is deciding on the fairness of a promotion decision. The manager explains the reasons for the decision clearly and respectfully, ensuring no insults are involved. This approach primarily addresses which type of fairness?",
          "options": [
            "A) Distributive",
            "B) Procedural",
            "C) Interactional",
            "D) Retributive"
          ],
          "correct_index": 2,
          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "Correct: Interactional fairness has four parts: truthfulness, respectfulness, no insults or irrelevant hints, and justification with reasons. The scenario describes respectful treatment and justification, which are core to interactional fairness. Distractor A (Distributive) concerns outcomes like pay and dividends. Distractor B (Procedural) concerns the process rules like consistency and lack of bias, not the tone or respect aspect. Distractor D (Retributive) concerns consequences and discipline fairness.",
          "key_concept": "Interactional Fairness"
        }
