          "model_answer": "**Part A: Four Dimensions of Trust and Three Drivers**\nThe lecture identifies four dimensions across which trust is measured: competence, openness, concern, and reliability. These dimensions form the metric for assessing trust in working relationships. The three drivers that build trust are ability, benevolence, and integrity. Ability refers to the capacity to perform the job; benevolence refers to the intention to do good for the other party; and integrity refers to the adherence to promises and principles.\n\n**Part B: Transparency and Privacy Framework**\nThe lecture establishes a tiered information flow based on role, emphasizing that not everyone needs to know everything:\n- **Owners**: Need to know everything. This includes all five parameters: pay, succession plans, dividends, disputes, and overall company performance. This is because owners hold the ultimate risk and responsibility.\n- **Board of Directors**: Need to know everything in full. Specifically, they require detailed information on valuation and dividend decisions, as these are core governance functions.\n- **Non-family managers**: Need to know only a summary. They need to know if the company is growing, if it is profitable, and how indebted the firm is. They do not need to know individual salary details, liquidity positions, or how much the family is taking out of the system.\n- **Employees**: Need to know the largest summary only. They need to know if the company is growing and profitable. Details on salary structures, liquidity, family payouts, and specific financial metrics are hidden from this group to maintain operational focus and prevent unnecessary concern.\n- **Family staff**: Need a summary of succession plans and disputes, but disputes can be kept from non-family managers.\n\nThe core principle is careful picking and choosing who needs to know what and to what extent, rather than a blanket policy of full disclosure or complete opacity.",
