      "notes_markdown": "# Foundation for working siblings\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Sibling partnerships in family businesses thrive on eight structured foundations, purpose, governance, transparency, and exit planning, replacing emotional dynamics with clear rules to preserve both family harmony and business longevity.\n### Foundational Principles & Policies\n- **Purpose:** Define shared goals (income, growth, legacy) and trade-offs (freedom, control, dividends); test alignment by asking if each sibling would buy in as an outsider on the same terms.\n- **Neutral forum & board:** Prefer an independent chair over a sibling; establish clear decision rights to avoid yearly fights.\n- **Policies replace negotiations:** Formalize employment, compensation, and dividend policies to prevent favoritism suspicions and liquidity-growth trade-offs.\n- **Code of conduct:** Keep disagreements private; avoid side deals; declare conflicts fairly; advance based on merit; never use firm information against co-owners; resolve disputes through mediation before litigation.\n- **Written agreements:** Family constitutions, shareholders’ agreements, and transfer documents serve as enforceable maps, not templates, navigating conflicts with legal counsel.\n- **Separate spheres:** Define clear metrics and distinct roles; avoid sibling reporting lines when possible. Use private forums and agreed tie-breakers for disagreements.\n- **Exit plan:** Include rights of first refusal, formulas for valuation, staged buyouts, and mechanisms like put/call options or shotgun clauses to enable strategic exits without draining the firm or destroying harmony.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming siblings can work together successfully without formal governance structures. → **Reality:** Structural foundations, boards, policies, and written agreements, are essential to replace emotional decision-making and prevent conflicts from escalating.\n2. **Trap:** Believing that an exit plan is only needed if a sibling wants to leave immediately. → **Reality:** Having a pre-agreed exit formula and rights of first refusal strengthens the family business and harmony by making exit a strategic choice, not an emotional fight.\n3. **Trap:** Thinking that merit-based advancement in a family business is purely objective. → **Reality:** Advancement must follow published, established paths; without clear merit criteria, perceptions of favoritism undermine sibling trust and business cohesion.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
      "slides_json": "[]",
