      "transcript_text": "What is the impact of professionalisation on performance? Is there an any empirical evidence that we can look to, to say whether this actually pays off and does it really work the way that we want it to work? Let us look at 3 different studies one from Denmark, one from the US, and one from Belgium. Each of these studies are in different context countries definitely, yes. But also they come with very different findings, and each finding also has its own caveat when you think about it. For example, the study in Denmark points to that family CEO successors lowers profitability in comparison to an outside CEO. This would tend to suggest that it is always good to have an outside CEO. You know, that's where the caveat is, which is not necessarily true. There are several examples where the family CEO has actually strengthened the family firm post-transition. The study by Villalonga and Amit point or find that descendant-run family firms are valued lesser than founder or professionally run ones And the third one from Belgium points that several professionalization dimensions help. However, these effects are conditional. Let's look at the first study in a little bit more detail, right. What is the finding and what does it really help us? You know, what is the problem at the core of this particular study? That firms that choose family succession may already differ in ways affecting performance. And that firstborn child's gender predicts the family's succession, but not business prospects directly, right and therefore, the study finds that family succession lowers the return on assets by roughly 4% points versus an outside CEO. However, how the study should be read in terms of professionalizing, and therefore let me contextualize it with what we have been discussing on professionalisation. When the external environment is rewarding a professional structure and talent and merit-based progression, family firms that respond in a professional manner by appointing an outside CEO when that is required rather than relying on family CEOs, those firms tend to perform better. Second, the Dekker et al.'s conditional finding. Here you're looking at 5 different dimensions. Non-family governance involvement. It does seem to have positive effect, but only with real decentralization and lesser financial controls. The presence of formal HR control systems and decentralization of authority also have positive effects. However, formal financial control systems there aren't very strong results that possibly could be explained away through multiple different mechanisms. Activeness of the top management team is kind of part of the construct, but not separately discussed over here. When you have formal professional governance involvement, especially of independent external members, internally when there is good professional HR control systems and a decentralization of authority which strengthens decision-making, the results are positive.",
