          "model_answer": "The Innovation Paradox refers to the conflict between the inherent tendencies of a family business and the requirements of innovation. Family businesses are built on concentrated control, long-term patient capital, and the protection of existing assets (socio-emotional wealth), which are almost the opposite of what innovation requires.\n\nInnovation is critical for survival because if a family firm fails to renew its products, processes, or business model, the next generation has no reason to join the business, threatening succession. The lecture identifies four key tensions:\n\n1. Concentrated Control vs. Outside Expertise: Family firms rely on internal decision rights, but innovation often requires external expertise as internal skills may be obsolete. The family tendency to protect existing knowledge hinders the openness needed for innovation.\n\n2. Patient Capital vs. Risk Tolerance: While family firms have multi-decade patient capital, innovation demands quick turnaround, willingness to accept failure, and rapid iteration. The fear that failure will threaten the business legacy leads to an aversion to the necessary risk-taking for innovation.\n\n3. Socio-Emotional Wealth vs. Product Cannibalization: Family firms derive identity from their existing brand. Innovation may require cannibalizing existing products to build better ones, which is resisted because it threatens the firm's identity and continuity.\n\n4. Internal Knowledge vs. External Openness: Family firms accumulate deep, insular knowledge. Innovators require openness to outside models and global knowledge. The insular knowledge base is a boundary condition that limits innovation.\n\nAdditionally, external factors such as the rapid pace of technology change, multi-generation overlap in risk views, global competition from well-funded e-commerce players, and employee expectations for skill development further pressure the family firm to innovate.\n\nWithout innovation, the business risks becoming obsolete, leaving the next generation with nothing to inherit and no incentive to participate.\n\nMarking Rubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the paradox (3 marks): Accurately identifies the conflict between family business foundations and innovation requirements.\n- Identification and explanation of at least two key tensions (4 marks): Correctly names and explains the core trade-offs (e.g., control vs. expertise, patient capital vs. risk).\n- Connection to succession and next-generation incentive (3 marks): Explains why innovation is linked to the desire to pass the business on successfully.",
