      "transcript_text": "Let's look at innovation and succession together. Because innovation is something that should be passed on to the next generation. Sometimes what you start with may not get completed in the same generation, must be passed on. It's not just the project, it's also the thought process. It's also  the outlook as to why should you look at R&D should also be passed on. We spoke about at some point earlier that innovation and succession are actually 2 sides of the same coin. You innovate because you are interested in the long term, and for the business to survive, you have to keep on innovating is something family firms understand. Let's again get deeper into it. What should get passed on to the next generation? Institutionalized capacity. What it means? The governance structures for a certain, let's say, a project. We are only dealing with innovation out here. So and whatever is being done as part of innovation, as part of R&D, there should be some governance structures around it as to what are the major milestones, where all it will get measured,  who takes the decision as to how much more funds can be invested at what stage, what is the outcome that we should see at every stage. So, all these are certain milestone structures. They have some guardrails around it, that has to be also something that has to be part of the succession. Secondly the relationship and the risk appetite that is there as part of innovation do not get transferred automatically. Therefore, effort should be made to ensure that importance the risk appetite to invest in R&D  and ensure that the company keeps on moving forward. Transfer of those is equally important. What succession will really test is actually which is built and what survives under pressure in terms of whether the decisions around R&D taken in terms of  the milestones, the progress map, do they survive pressure or does the risk appetite survive? If there is a pressure,  if pressure means if there is a downturn, if there is a recession, if things are not going right, if there is a hard stretch, which of these survive is very, very important. But what actually changes hands when there is a succession? There are 2 things that change hands. There is an ability and willingness resources on the ability side, resources on the willingness side, both change hands. When I say ability side resources, what does it mean in terms of ownership, capital,  the structures around it, the board, the governance the mechanisms built for monitoring, for ensuring that generation to generation things go on, the management keeps rolling. These are all the ability side, the easier one to transfer, okay. The second part is the willingness side. This is not something that is automatically inherited. The willingness of the successor to take on the family role and all parts of it. I'm just taking not just about the ownership or the management, innovation, everything. So, this is has to be, inbuilt has to come from within, can be can be built over time, but it's usually driven by the successor's own reading of the environment. The successor has grown up in a kind, slightly different environment in which the  father has grown up. As the business passes on, the environment also has changed, so successor will see it from very fresh eyes. But a succession plan that only transfers ability and willingness is automatic will mean that there will be problems. So now institutionalizing, if I say what the what carries across in a transition is, well, authority that is that will outlive the person who first chartered it. That has to be carried across  a transition when it comes to innovation. Again, a ring-fenced budget on what needs to be done and somebody is holding it today, and somebody tomorrow has to take on these projects and carry on with it. Independent of who was who started it, somebody has to carry the torch forward. Some of these will get done if there is a, if there is a way the successor can get involved from  an early stage. It is sometimes a channel for the next generation to get into these new projects early on. Start believe in them, understand that why these are important and be part of it. Bring their own young energy into it. And therefore, before it reaches a stage where it's reached its full potential when there's a transfer, the succession happening, they're able to carry forward from there. In many ways, they will see that this new project as their legacy, something they were involved right from the start, and they took it forward. And legacy is very important for every, every family generation. And the lessons about succession in terms of caring about innovation to become a much better company is very, very important. And therefore governance for innovation must survive a leadership change. As in what kind of decisions to be taken to enable innovation must survive  a leadership change.",
