          "model_answer": "The Uppsala model and the OLI framework offer distinct perspectives on firm internationalisation. The Uppsala model is a process theory assuming gradual expansion; it posits that firms start with low-commitment modes (e.g., sporadic export) and progressively increase commitment to sales subsidiaries and FDI as they gain learning and reduce the risk of foreign market operations. Its core assumption is that risk reduction occurs through learning over time. In contrast, the OLI framework (Dunning's eclectic paradigm) is a snapshot evaluation based on firm-specific advantages (O), location advantages (L), and internalisation advantages (I). It does not assume a strict sequence; rather, it predicts the mode based on the combination of advantages a firm possesses. Regarding mode choice, the Uppsala model links exporting to the early stages of the firm's life cycle before extensive learning, with FDI occurring later when trust is established. The OLI framework predicts exporting when a firm has only ownership advantages (O), licensing when it has ownership and location advantages (O + L), and FDI only when it possesses all three advantages (O + L + I). Therefore, while Uppsala explains internationalisation as a temporal learning journey, OLI explains it as a configuration of advantages determining the optimal mode.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of Uppsala model (process, learning, gradual commitment): 3 marks\n- Core conceptual understanding of OLI framework (O, L, I dimensions, mode predictions): 3 marks\n- Comparative analysis distinguishing the two theories (sequence vs. configuration): 2 marks\n- Application of mode choice logic (Uppsala: temporal; OLI: advantage combination): 2 marks",
