      "created_at": "2026-11-06 08:57:16",
      "transcript_text": "Why do we see many family firms tending to internationalize less? One is the risk-return calculus that we spoke about, which explains the willingness to internationalize, especially when the firm's survival itself is at stake. But willingness alone does not explain the internationalization of a firm. There could be ability gaps as well. What concrete gaps in the capabilities or ability actually hold many family firms back from internationalizing? Broadly put, there are four different resource or capability gaps. Let us start by looking at the financial resources. Family firms do have the strong advantage of patient capital, which is willing to take long-term bets. They can sit out. They're not under the pressure of quarter to quarter. However, because of their desire many a times to retain ownership control, they do not access outside equity or outside financial resources. They don't want to be held accountable to external parties. What that means is that they also have a smaller financial resource pool to take bets such as internationalization. Internationalization many times is a costly decision and requires resource commitment. But when you seek to protect ownership control and therefore not access outside capital, the advantage of patient capital is weakened. The next is the managerial capability. Many family firms tend to recruit only from the family or tend to have an higher percentage of family representation in management, or they tend to accommodate every family member on the management. What this does is that it leads to a narrower pool in terms of managerial capability. A thinner bench of managerial talent also means fewer people who can run a foreign operation. This is a genuine capability gap that is pointed out in the internationalization of family firms. The third is the knowledge required to succeed in internationalization. Many times this knowledge is very tacit, it is very experiential, and therefore, if the family firm has not any prior internationalization, this knowledge is absent by definition with because of the lack of prior exposure to internationalization. Similarly is the internationalization alliances or networks. When the family firm has not developed any existing network to follow or support its internationalization, then the networks that it can leverage when it needs to internationalize are very weak, and this makes the internationalization decision very costly for a family firm.",
