          "model_answer": "The four resource/capability gaps identified in the transcript are: (1) Financial Resource Gap, (2) Managerial Capability Gap, (3) Tacit Knowledge Gap, and (4) Network Gap.\n\n1. Financial Resource Gap: Family firms have the advantage of patient capital willing to take long-term bets. However, the desire to retain ownership control prevents them from accessing outside equity or financial resources. This shrinks the total financial pool available, weakening the advantage of patient capital when costly international expansion is required.\n\n2. Managerial Capability Gap: Many family firms recruit only from the family or have a higher percentage of family representation in management. This creates a thinner bench of managerial talent. Fewer qualified individuals exist to run foreign operations, creating a genuine capability gap for internationalization.\n\n3. Tacit Knowledge Gap: The knowledge required to succeed in internationalization is often very tacit and experiential. If the family firm has no prior internationalization experience, this knowledge is absent by definition due to the lack of prior exposure.\n\n4. Network Gap: Existing alliances and networks support internationalization. Family firms without prior international experience have very weak networks to leverage, making the internationalization decision very costly.\n\nInteraction with ownership control: The desire to retain ownership control acts as a compounding constraint. By avoiding outside equity (exacerbating the financial gap), the firm limits its access to external expertise and capital that could help bridge the other gaps. The control motive thus creates a self-reinforcing cycle where the protection of ownership limits the resources needed for growth.",
