      "transcript_text": "Music] Let's understand the impact of governance on the family's plan to internationalize. In the previous section, we talked about the financial resources as an area which is probably the hardest gap because finance is very closely linked to the control question that the family always comes up. But even more important from that is, is the governance question. And there is enough, there is enough research to, for us to understand how governance actually can shape the decision of a family going abroad. Let's look at it as to where, where the governance actually impedes the family business. When I say impede, as to puts a brake on internationalisation and where it can actually help or what can or what factors can bring down the, the resistance to internationalisation. All this from the governance stand-standpoint. How does it actually pan out? The finding says, from, from research says that if you have a family member on the board, and if he or she is actually the chairman of the board, is very strongly correlated with the family going less international. Why does that happen? This happens because the chair or the chair of the board or the family member is in the right position to slow down outside capital coming into the company. And we all know that to go international, you need that capital. And why does the slowdown happens? Because the family member positioned as the chair of the board doesn't want to dilute. Dilution is something that is something that will come up with a lot of resistance. So if you have a key member in a position who can take the final call that member of the family can delay or even deny a, a foreign entry. How do you moderate it or how do you take care of it? It needs a different kind of board's specific refinement as to how this control and this dilution can be handled. So we can, like we spoke about structures, we, we can also see how a board which is not completely always headed by one person or a, a strong family member as a chair, we'll also see how that changes as then. So how can we change this model where a board can also authorize a foreign expansion? Let's see those cases. First case is if a family member is on the board chair. We have seen this. What actually happens is the family member tends to impede or have a negative correlation, does not wants to, does not want to go international. The other way, suppose you have an experienced independent directors. Now, independent directors will, will not worry so much about control and, and, and dilution. They know that control can be managed through some very effective governance and monitoring. So when they share their views, and if they, their views are also given a good hearing, and there are multiple family members on the board, it is quite possible that the family may overall view in a sense that let us go international because there is seems to be some confidence. The independent directors will actually detail out as to how a different structure can be thought of or a different monitoring mechanism can be thought of or how we can mitigate some of the risk going abroad. They also have to answer the control question, which is when they have to provide that confidence to the, the family that control is not lost as a result of nor at least not completely lost as a result of outside capital coming in. Suppose there is a cross-generational family involved in the board. So there are multiple generations, multiple branches of the family. When they all come together and make a decision, it is very positively correlated in the sense that firms do go international. They understand that to, to take care of the larger family, the multiple generations as they grow, the, all the family has to be fed by effectively the same company. They know that, they need to expand. And if expansion means that they need to take in some outside capital, lose some control, but still in the end be much, much better off they know it's a good, good sign for the family over the long-term. So having multiple family members, actually cross-generational family members, is very positively correlated with a firm going overseas. Now again, if it's an independent set of directors and, and very distant markets, this is something that does play a role. The more distant market, the effect is stronger for very, very distant entries. So we have understood how governance can, can play a role.",
