      "transcript_text": "Let us move to the internationalization process and strategy. Once the decision to internationalize is made, how is it actually executed? And within this, I want to look at 2 aspects. One is the level of control, and second is the resource commitments that are required. And these 2 vary depending upon what the entry mode is. These entry modes, if you look at, they are broadly exporting, licensing or franchising, joint ventures, a wholly owned subsidiary. And here again, there is a difference between whether this wholly owned subsidiary is acquired or whether it is a greenfield. In other words, a family firm decides to set it up by themselves. And you will note that both the degree of control and expected returns and the resource commitments and risks vary across all of these entry modes. The pattern that you will note in this diagram is that with each step up in this ladder, you're trading more control and expected return for greater resource commitment and more risk. When we look at family firms and the entry mode choice, first is the tendency. Family CEOs Research shows that family CEOs tend to favor lower commitment modes, which is typically to the lower left of this diagram. The reason being that lower commitment limits exposure to both the financial as well as the non-financial or the socio-emotional costs associated with internationalization. When we put both strategy and structure together, what we need to keep in mind is that these are not two separate or distinct decisions. Entry mode is where the whole framework between strategy and the structure becomes a very clear and observable choice. What determines the position for a family firm? These include the resource gaps, the governance, as well as the risk calculus.",
