          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The four key benefits independent directors bring to a family firm board are:\n1. **Professionalizing the room**: Family members prepare and behave more formally when a credible outsider is present, raising the overall governance standard of the boardroom.\n2. **Managing family careers honestly**: Independent directors can raise hard truths about a family member's performance that would otherwise be overlooked because the decision-making would typically revolve around emotions rather than merit.\n3. **Complementary expertise**: The independent director fills gaps in skills or experience that the family's own career paths never produce, bringing external perspectives and specialized knowledge.\n4. **Lending credible control**: Independent directors have no related-party interest of their own to protect, which naturally disqualifies them from conflicts of interest and gives credibility to the board's oversight role.\n\nRegarding substantive vs. symbolic independence: Symbolic independence exists only on paper (statutory definitions, compliance checkboxes). Substantive independence exists in the room - the director is behaviorally free to speak frank and fair opinions without checking the chairman's face or looking for behavioral cues. If directors seek approval about what they can speak, independence is lost regardless of paperwork. The lecture uses the example of directors checking the chairman's face before speaking as a clear sign that substantive independence has been compromised.\n\n**Rubric:**\n- Core conceptual understanding of the four benefits: 3 marks\n- Distinction between symbolic and substantive independence: 3 marks\n- Real-world application/example from lecture: 2 marks\n- Structure and clarity: 2 marks",
