      "notes_markdown": "# Key governance questions\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Effective family board governance requires honest, written evaluation of readiness, compensation, and exit terms for family directors, while advisory boards serve as a low-risk precursor to fiduciary governance when stakes and complexity demand binding oversight.\n### Governance Accountability & Boundaries\n- **Readiness & competence:** Distinguish between desire for role responsibilities versus title/standing; assess cognizance of accountability.\n- **Process & accountability:** Demand a written evaluation process applied rigorously to family directors; verify removability is practical, not impossible due to family standing (entitlement risk).\n- **Boundary respect:** Board disagreements resurface as family grievances, damaging business systems, the business, and family harmony.\n- **Compensation & exit:** Ensure pay is set and disclosed identically for family and non-family directors; confirm defined term with real decision points; establish honorable stepping-down procedures.\n- **Advisory board function:** Provide regular cadence (quarterly common); clear topics (strategy, major decisions, succession, market developments); no binding vote, owner freely accepts or declines counsel.\n- **Advisory board members:** Retired operating executives with relevant industry experience, functional specialists (finance/tech/digital transformation), or family business consultants.\n- **Graduation trigger:** Transition to fiduciary/hybrid board when outside capital justifies binding oversight, business complexity grows (multiple geographies/businesses), outsiders are brought in, and professional organization structures are required.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Family directors can be removed from the board as easily as non-family directors. → **Reality:** Family standing often makes non-renewal practically impossible unless a formal, written removal process exists and is enforced.\n2. **Trap:** Advisory board counsel is binding and must be implemented by the founder. → **Reality:** Advisory board advice is non-binding; the founder is free to accept or decline it without constraint.",
