          "model_answer": "The core purpose of the family constitution is to serve as a social contract that binds the family together and governs the intersection of family and business. Its primary value lies in the drafting process itself, which forces different family branches to surface hidden aspirations and resolve disagreements early, preventing future conflict. As the lecturer noted, the process \"throws up so many questions that the various family branches have to answer\" and helps family members understand that \"various thoughts, aspirations, which many others didn't know even existed.\"\n\nTwo specific policy areas governed by the constitution are:\n1. Employment and Compensation: The constitution codifies entry requirements (e.g., minimum education and external work experience) to prevent family members from being \"parachuted\" into roles. It also distinguishes between a \"family scale\" and \"market scale\" for compensation. For example, if the family scale is set higher than the market scale, it effectively means taking money out of the business as dividends or salary, which reduces the cash available for reinvestment in the company's growth.\n2. Governance Body Rules: The constitution specifies the rules for the formation and running of governing bodies. For the family council, it may codify rules on size (e.g., maximum of 7 or 8 members), eligibility (e.g., requiring multiple family branches and equal gender representation), and election processes. For the family forum, it may set rules on meeting frequency, who convenes it, age limits for attendees, and whether in-laws are allowed.",
