          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The process of building a family constitution is iterative, beginning with defining the family's current state, what they are doing, what they are satisfied with, and what they are not. A first draft is relatively easy to produce, but the critical phase begins when the draft is brought before the family forum. The lecture emphasizes that involvement of authority holders must occur at every stage, not merely as an afterthought after drafting is complete. If decision-makers are excluded, the constitution is perceived as being 'hoisted on them' without consent, which leads to implementation failure.\n\nA key mechanism for longevity is the review cycle, illustrated by the GMR example. GMR has codified a process where the constitution is examined periodically using the same deliberative method used in the initial drafting. As authority holders change over generations, the constitution is rewritten to fulfil the requirements of the current owners. This ensures the constitution stands the test of time rather than becoming obsolete.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the iterative process and starting point (3 marks)\n- Explanation of the risk of excluding authority holders and the 'foisted on them' trap (3 marks)\n- Identification and explanation of the GMR review cycle mechanism (4 marks)",
