      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the key governance mechanisms covered in a family business shareholders' agreement and the enforceability lesson from the V.B. Rangaraj versus V.B. Gopalkrishnan case. Why is it critical that the shareholders' agreement and the articles of association be aligned?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The shareholders' agreement serves as the legally enforceable backbone of family business ownership, covering four key governance areas: (1) Decision rules, specifying voting thresholds for different decision types, simple majority for routine operational matters, super majority (often 75%) for consequential decisions like amendments to the agreement, major acquisitions, or creating new share classes, and unanimity for the most fundamental matters such as the core purpose of the firm and the nature of control; (2) Transfer and exit mechanics, including the right of first refusal (existing shareholders get the option to be offered shares first; unless they refuse, shares cannot be sold to an outside party), put right (minority can force a buyback under specified conditions), drag-along provision (majority can compel minority to sell alongside them), and tag-along provision (minority can join a majority sale on the same terms); (3) Valuation methodology, specifying how shares will be priced during an exit or transfer, who values the shares, what methodology is used, and whether an external party is involved; and (4) Enforceability alignment with the articles of association.\n\nThe critical enforceability lesson from the V.B. Rangaraj versus V.B. Gopalkrishnan case is that a transfer restriction only in the shareholders' agreement and not also in the articles of association may be unenforceable against the company. In other words, the articles of association need to reflect and agree with what is in the shareholders' agreement. This is because the articles of association are more important and legally binding on the company itself. Therefore, family businesses must carefully ensure that both documents are aligned; a restriction in one but not the other creates a governance gap where the intended transfer control may fail in practice, potentially allowing an outside party to acquire shares against the family's wishes.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analytical application of the V.B. Rangaraj case lesson",
              "marks": 5
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flashcards": [
        {
          "front": "What voting threshold typically applies to routine operational matters in a family business shareholders' agreement?",
          "back": "Simple majority (one share, one vote) for routine operational matters."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the key enforceability lesson from the V.B. Rangaraj versus V.B. Gopalkrishnan case regarding transfer restrictions?",
          "back": "A transfer restriction only in the shareholders' agreement and not also in the articles of association may be unenforceable against the company."
        },
        {
          "front": "Distinguish between drag-along and tag-along provisions in a shareholders' agreement.",
          "back": "Drag-along allows the majority to compel the minority to sell; tag-along gives the minority the option to join a majority sale on the same terms."
        },
        {
          "front": "What are the four decision rule thresholds typically specified in a shareholders' agreement, from lowest to highest friction?",
          "back": "Simple majority → Super majority (often 75%) → Unanimity for core purpose/control."
        }
      ],
