          "model_answer": "The shareholders' assembly is disproportionately important for family firms because of the unique combination of ownership structure and goal dimensions. In a family firm, ownership is concentrated with a very long-term horizon, and socio-emotional wealth, encompassing control, identity, and legacy, is often valued a par with or above pure financial return. This creates a scenario where minority family branches, who may hold small shares and be excluded from the ownership council that makes day-to-day decisions, still have a critical need for voice. The assembly serves as the one legally guaranteed moment where every family member, regardless of share size, can be heard. Skipping it means the family member may never be heard at any other point in time, making it disproportionately costly. In contrast, a widely held firm has dispersed ownership with short-term horizons and predominantly financial return goals. The relationship there is simply owner versus manager, and other communication channels exist beyond the assembly. Therefore, the assembly's role as the guaranteed platform for minority expression makes it far more vital for the family firm structure.",
