          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the family galaxy metaphor as discussed in the lecture. How do the dimensions of shape, luminosity, control distance, and regeneration rate help in understanding ownership governance across diverse family-owned entities? Illustrate your answer with examples from the Tata and Premji galaxies.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The family galaxy metaphor conceptualizes the family as the center of a constellation of diverse entities (operating firms, holding companies, foundations, trusts) rather than focusing on a single company. The shape of the galaxy is captured through four dimensions: (1) Capital/Strategic Weight (mass): Entities carry different masses; for example, TCS carries more weight than Tata Automation in the Tata galaxy because TCS funds the latter. (2) Luminosity/Visibility: Listed firms are far more visible to outsiders than private trusts; TCS is listed and visible, whereas the Dorabji Tata Trust is private and hardly known. (3) Control Distance: This refers to how closely the family controls an entity, ranging from professional management with family approval to allowing the entity to run independently. (4) Regeneration Rate: Entities are created or retired as the family evolves; PremjiInvest creates new entities, while the foundation runs independently with a defined mission. These dimensions dictate that governance vehicles must have different four-room boundaries; a one-size-fits-all approach results in a messy structure because the boundaries required for an operating company differ from those needed for a foundation or family office.",
