          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "The correct option is B. This tests the critical thinking principle that the structure of reasoning may appear valid (sales increased), but the premises must be checked for completeness and relevance. Entrepreneur A assumes the ad campaign is the cause without ruling out the competitor outage (a confounding variable). Entrepreneur B exercises critical thinking by checking if the premise (ad caused sales) is the only plausible explanation. Option A is a trap because it confuses a strong correlation with proven causation. Option C is a trap because factual observation alone does not establish cause. Option D is a trap because it dismisses the value of reasoning entirely.",
