      "transcript_text": "[Music] here's Here's what I'd like for you to carry forward from everything we've covered so far in this pillar on logic and critical thinking in reasoning. Reasoning is the basic activity of moving from what you know to a new conclusion. Logic checks whether that conclusion actually follows from your premises. It's a test of structure. Critical thinking checks whether those premises deserve to be trusted in the first place. It's a test of the material going into the argument. Good reasoning requires both checks, logic and critical thinking, because each one catches a failure the other one can't see. We talked about 3 obstacles that routinely interrupt that second check, often without you noticing. Confirmation bias, which shapes what evidence you search for and notice. And remember, motivated reasoning, which shapes which conclusions you want to be true and how skeptically you examine evidence for and against them. And lastly, the last obstacle, anchoring, which lets the first number or claim you encounter quietly set the fa, frame for everything that follows. We also talked about how these 3 rarely operate alone, and they tend to reinforce each other. They produce conclusions that feel fully fluent, fully confident, while resting on a process that was not neutral. And the reason for all of this matters, it's not an academic matter alone. It is useful for your professional as well as civic life, as we saw in the examples on fabricated legal citations, of biased hiring decisions or in inflated growth metrics. Critical thinking is the discipline that protects good decisions in a boardroom and good judgment in a democracy using exactly the same tools. That's why this pillar on logic and critical thinking and reasoning isn't just a professional training tool. It's civic infrastructure, and it is running inside each of you at any given time.",
