      "notes_markdown": "# Video\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Selection bias and the invisibility of omitted data are the sharpest forms of framing; the mind automatically fills gaps with coherent narratives, mistaking incomplete records for complete truth, making the critical question \"What was excluded before I saw this?\" essential for accurate analysis.\n### Selection Bias And Framing\n- **Selection bias ≠ framing:** Framing shapes how you read existing evidence; selection bias determines what never reaches you at all, rendering you unable to detect the gap.\n- **WYSIATI (What You See Is All There Is):** The mind constructs a coherent story from available information without pausing to ask if important data is missing; this affects everyone, not just careless thinkers.\n- **Survivorship bias (Abraham Wald example):** During WWII, armor was to be placed on wings/tail of returning bombers. Statistician Abraham Wald identified that planes hit in engines/cockpits never returned, the absence of damage there was the critical signal for where armor was actually needed.\n- **Business application:** Startup accelerators showcase successful alumni, excluding failures by construction, presenting a non-representative sample to prospective founders.\n- **Civic life / Risk perception:** Vivid, dramatic causes of death (plane crashes, shark attacks, terrorism) are overestimated in likelihood due to disproportionate media coverage; availability is mistaken for frequency. Underreported causes like heart disease and diabetes remain invisible despite being far more common.\n- **Consequence:** Public policy and concern get shaped by what is dramatic and reportable rather than what is actually common and serious.\n\n### Objective Record Illusion\n- **Raw evidence is never neutral:** Even a video or dataset reflects choices made at the moment of capture (field of view, what counts as a data point).\n- **Video example:** A recording shows only what is within the camera's frame; events outside that frame simply do not exist in the record, no matter how relevant.\n- **Data example:** A dataset only exists because someone decided what counted as a data point worth recording; that decision is a choice, not a neutral fact.\n- **Kahneman's narrative tendency:** The mind builds a smooth, coherent narrative from whatever data is on hand and mistakes that completeness for the evidence itself; a well-organized dataset or clearly shot video feels authoritative simply because it is clean and legible.\n- **Critical distinction:** Recognizing that a record can be entirely genuine but still partial separates the careful evidence reader from the passive consumer of \"proof.\"\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Selection bias only occurs when data is deliberately suppressed or concealed. → **Reality:** Selection bias arises naturally from visibility and reporting patterns; underreported causes like heart disease are excluded not by suppression, but because they are less visible and dramatic than vivid outliers.\n2. **Trap:** If a dataset looks complete and well-organized, it must be an objective, neutral record of reality. → **Reality:** A well-organized dataset feels authoritative due to its clarity and legibility, but that feeling of clarity is not a guarantee that nothing relevant was left outside the frame; the choices of what to capture and what to leave uncaptured occur before any presentation bias is applied.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
