      "transcript_text": "Let's look at this example of the Boeing 737 MAX case. Between October 2018 and March 2019, two brand new Boeing 737 Max aircrafts crashed. First, a Lion Air Flight 610 crashed in Indonesia, and then the Ethiopian Airlines Flight 302 also crashed. Together, the crashes killed nearly 346 people. In the aftermath of the horrible crashes, Boeing offered a public explanation which focused largely on pilot error and training. The company argued that the aircraft was fundamentally safe and suggested that the pilots had not responded correctly to the emergency. Because the first crash happened in Indonesia and the second in Ethiopia, the narrative also implied that differences in pilot training, cultural differences, and aviation standards were an important part of the explanation. Now, that was the dominant narrative by Boeing. When journalists, engineers, accident investigators, and later government inquiries started, they examined internal documents and technical evidence very closely, and what emerged was a counter-reading. Instead of blaming the pilots, the counter-reading centered on the aircraft itself. Specifically, a flight control system, which was new called MCAS, or the Maneuvering Characteristics Augmentation System, MCAS. MCAS was designed to push the aircraft's nose downward under certain conditions. In both the crashes, Indonesia and Ethiopia, faulty sensor data caused the system to activate repeatedly even though the aircraft was not actually in danger of stalling. The pilots, even though they fought against this MCAS system, the system itself, which was MCAS continued forcing the nose down. This is what the counter-reading revealed. Investigators also discovered that many pilots had received little or no training at all on MCAS. Now, why? Because Boeing, they discovered wanted the aircraft to be marketed as requiring minimal additional pilot certification. Internal communications also showed that Boeing employees themselves were concerned about aspects of the design and certification process long before the second crash in Ethiopia occurred. Notice how the counter-reading changes the story without changing the facts. The crashes are the same, horrible tragedies. The timeline is the same but the central character shifts now from the pilot to the aircraft's design and certification process. The conflict also shifts from pilot competence to engineering decisions and regulatory oversight. The choices that matter are not those that the pilot made in the cockpit during the final minutes of the flight, but the decisions that management made on the design, testing, and certification years earlier When you look at these two choices and you shift the focus, you see that the consequences also change. Instead of calling for better pilot training alone, what the counter-reading points towards is a redesign of the aircraft, strengthening regulatory oversight, and examining Boeing's corporate decision-making. Now, why does this particular counter-reading make more sense to us? Because it passed the three tests that we discussed. First, it passed the evidentiary test. It was supported by accident investigation reports, flight data recorders, and internal Boeing communications, and FAA documentation, and did not rest simply on speculation or hindsight. Second, it passed the consistency test, which explained both crashes using the same underlying mechanism and also explained why experienced pilots had struggled to recover the aircraft under very identical circumstances. And finally, this particular counter-reading passed the predictive test, where we saw that the aircraft's design was the real problem. And if the aircraft's design was the real problem, then similar accidents could happen again and again unless the design and the system was changed. That prediction shaped what happened next. Aviation authorities around the world grounded the 737 MAX fleet. Boeing redesigned the MCAS system and pilot training requirements were revised. The regulators introduced additional scrutiny before this aircraft was allowed to return to service. The important lesson is that the dominant narrative initially focused attention on one set of actors, pilots, and one type of explanation, pilot error, while the counter-reading shifted the attention to a completely different set of actors, which is the management of Boeing and their decisions and evidence which changed the course of events. While the counter-reading shifted attention to a completely different set of actors, the management at Boeing who made some really bad decisions. The events themselves never changed. What changed was the interpretation, and ultimately, which interpretation was best was the one that was supported by evidence. By now, I hope one point is clear. Recognizing that narratives are constructed, they're made up of choices and result in certain consequences, doesn't mean that every corporate story is dishonest. Not at all. Nor does it mean that every alternative interpretation deserves equal credibility, that every counter-reading is the real truth. Our job here, particularly for those of you who are going to be digital business entrepreneurs, is for us to approach every narrative, including the ones that you will make, with curiosity and discipline. Ask who is telling the story. Ask whose perspective is being prioritized, whose perspective may be missing, and what definition of success the story assumes. Then ask the most important question of all: Does the alternative interpretation, the counter-reading, stand up to the same standards of evidence and reasoning that we expect from the original? Remember the final lesson from Ebner Eschenbach's version of The Tortoise and the Hare? After a single victory, the tortoise mistakes success for proof of superior ability. The tortoise begins to assume that they are a sprinter. Now, companies can also make the exact same mistake. They look at one successful outcome and assume it was entirely the result of their own brilliance, forgetting how much of that success may have depended on timing, circumstances, or someone else's mistakes. That's why learning to read narratives critically matters. It helps to distinguish between what's a good story and what's a good explanation.",
