          "model_answer": "The Amazon Prime case study illustrates that successful persuasion requires the strategic combination of all three rhetorical appeals. **Ethos** was established through Amazon's existing reputation for reliability and on-time delivery, which built customer trust and made them willing to accept a new proposition. **Logos** was provided through the logical economic calculation that the annual subscription would pay for itself for frequent buyers, presenting a rational benefit. **Pathos** appealed to the customer's desire for convenience and the emotional value of not having to wait for deliveries or hesitate before ordering. The lecture emphasizes that these appeals are interconnected and none is sufficient on its own; the 'right balance of all three' created the persuasive argument. For business communicators, understanding this interconnection is vital because it prevents the mistake of relying on just one aspect (e.g., having great data/logos but no credibility/ethos, or great emotional appeal/pathos but no logical basis). In the age of AI, this understanding helps distinguish between fluent writing and effective argumentation.\n\n**Rubric:**\n- **Core conceptual understanding (5 marks):** Accurately identifies the role of ethos, pathos, and logos in the Amazon Prime example and explains the concept of interconnection.\n- **Analytical application (5 marks):** Articulates why relying on a single appeal is insufficient and connects this to the broader challenge of evaluating AI-generated content.",
