          "model_answer": "Validity and soundness are two distinct layers of argument evaluation. Validity concerns the logical structure: an argument is valid if, assuming the premises are true, the conclusion must necessarily follow. Validity is purely about form, it does not assess whether the premises are actually true in the real world. Soundness is the higher standard: an argument is sound only if it is valid AND all its premises are actually true. The practical implication is that a polished, well-structured argument can be completely misleading if the premises are false or unsupported. In political rhetoric or marketing, speakers often construct valid-sounding arguments with plausible premises that lead to undesirable conclusions, but the arguments may be unsound because the premises are exaggerated, taken out of context, or false. Evaluators must check both the logical connection (validity) and the factual accuracy of premises (soundness) to avoid being persuaded by structurally correct but factually flawed reasoning.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of validity vs. soundness (5 marks): Accurately defines validity as form/structure-focused, soundness as validity + true premises, and the relationship that every sound argument is valid but not vice versa.\n- Analytical application to real-world context (5 marks): Provides a clear, relevant example of a valid but unsound argument and correctly identifies the practical danger of accepting structurally appealing but factually flawed arguments in rhetoric or marketing.",
