      "transcript_text": "Now let's look at part three where we are actually learning how to evaluate the evidence. That is the supporting data. What counts as support, and how much of this evidence is good enough? And this is where things get very difficult, because unlike validity, which you can often check with nice, neat, logical text, evaluating evidence is a very messy, more judgment-based process. But still, there are some solid questions that you can run any piece of evidence through. First, you have to ask, is this evidence relevant? Now remember that evidence has to actually bear on the claim that is being made which means that evidence has to be relevant to the claim that is being made. So, for example, this new phone that you have has a great camera is not evidence for this phone is a good investment, right. Because you are evaluating whether the phone is actually a good investment. Just because it has a great camera does not make it a good investment, unless the camera quality is actually the only thing that you want out of a phone, and that is why you need to ask this question, right. Whether this is a good investment just because it has a good camera. Now, a lot of the weak argumentation isn't wrong so much as it is besides the point. It is technically true. Most statements, most argumentation might also have true statements. But does the true statement support the conclusion or the claim that they are making? Not often, and that is why they're called weak arguments. They're beside the point statements. Okay, so the first question was, is it relevant? The second question is, is it sufficient? Now, one anecdote, I saw one black crow, so therefore all crows are black, right. Remember that? So that one anecdote is rarely enough to support a general claim. So remember the crow argument that we were making? I saw one black crow, and therefore now I believe that all crows are black. Now, that's a single data point, and you are using that for a statistical claim about a whole population of crows. That is not enough, right. So the size and diversity of your evidence base also matters a lot. Now we hear this claim about most people. Most people do this or most people say. Now that is not a valid sample. It is a cherry-picked case, and that makes for weak argumentation. The third question that you need to ask is, is the source credible? You ask three questions. Now, this is the third question. The first question is, is it relevant? The second question is, is it enough? Now you're asking, is the source credible? This means you need to check for expertise, track record, and very, very importantly for bias or conflict of interest. Now say, for example, there is a study on health benefits of a product, and it is entirely funded by the same company which is selling the product. Now, that is not automatically wrong, but it deserves more scrutiny than, say, independently re-conducted research needs. So you have to ask these questions about the source. Remember, this is about public health, right. So you have to ask if the company has the relevant expertise and does it have anything to gain from the particular conclusion being believed. And if it is a study on health benefits of a product and it is being conducted entirely by the company, then your answer should be it might have relevant expertise, but it definitely has something to gain from a particular conclusion about its product, right. Okay. So here is the fourth question. Is the evidence current? Does it have a shelf life? Say, for example, a statistic from 15 years ago about internet usage or a medical study whose findings have been later revised. They might have been perfectly good evidence once upon a time but it might actually be actively misleading now, 15 years later. So you have to ask where the evidence is from, when it is from, and whether anything has changed in the time since it was first published. Now here is the fifth question that you have to use and ask. Does it hold up against counter evidence? Remember, strong evidence don't just pile up all the evidence that it has, all the supporting evidence it has. They also need to stand strong in the face of supporting evidence on the other side. So for example, if you're evaluating an argument and there is a big body of contrary evidence that simply isn't addressed, now that's a huge red flag. Now this is something called cherry picking. If you're only picking up data that fits your conclusion while ignoring the counter data that does not fit the conclusion that you want, it's called cherry picking. So let's put these five checks together and putting these five checks together you get a decent working test for any piece of evidence. So these are the five questions you have to ask. Starting with, is it relevant? Is there enough of it? Does it come from a credible and unbiased source? Is it current, and does it survive the counterevidence? Now let's bring all of our discussion today together, starting with validity, soundness, relevance, all of that, right. So here is something that you have to ask yourself when you encounter a argument. First, figure out what kind of an argument is it. Is it claiming certainty or just probability? And if it is claiming certainty, check the validity first. Does the conclusion actually follow from the premises? And then check soundness. Are these premises actually true? If it's claiming probability, check the strength first. Does the conclusion follow with reasonable likelihood? Then check cogency. Is that likelihood built on true, well-supported premises? And underneath all of that, every premise doing real work in the argument deserves the evidence test. Relevant, is it sufficient, is it credible, current and able to withstand counterarguments? Now remember, none of this is about being a cynic who trusts nothing, right. So what is it about? It's about learning to tell the difference between an argument that actually earns your agreement and one that just sounds like it has and it's very polished, right. So that distinction between feeling persuaded and being justifiably persuaded is really the whole point of this discussion.",
