          "model_answer": "The five-check framework provides a structured method to assess the quality of evidence supporting a claim. The checks are: (1) Relevance - Does the evidence actually bear on the claim? (2) Sufficiency - Is there enough evidence, or does it rely on a small anecdote? (3) Credibility - Is the source expert and unbiased, or do they have a conflict of interest? (4) Currentness - Is the evidence up-to-date, or has context changed making it misleading? (5) Counterevidence - Does the evidence survive contrary data, or is the argument engaging in cherry-picking? \n\nIllustrative Example: Consider an argument claiming \"Vitamin C cures the common cold\" based on a single anecdote of one person feeling better after taking Vitamin C. \n- Relevance: Passes, as the anecdote does relate to colds. \n- Sufficiency: Fails, as one data point is insufficient to support a general medical claim. \n- Credibility: May pass if the anecdote is from a doctor, but if the person selling Vitamin C funds the story, it fails. \n- Currentness: May fail if medical consensus has since revised findings. \n- Counterevidence: Fails, as there is a large body of scientific evidence showing Vitamin C does not cure colds, only possibly reducing duration slightly. \nFailing the sufficiency check alone undermines the argument, as the conclusion overgeneralizes from a single data point, which is the core logical error identified in the lecture.\n\nRubric: \n- Core conceptual understanding of the five checks (5 marks): Awarded for correctly identifying and describing the five criteria. \n- Analytical application to the example (5 marks): Awarded for a coherent example that correctly maps the example to the checks and explains the failure point.",
