      "flashcards_json": "[{\"front\": \"Why does \\\"We are committed to becoming the most trusted company\\\" function as a performance rather than a claim?\", \"back\": \"It fails all four criteria: \\\"most trusted\\\" is undefined (specificity), no instrument to verify truth (verifiability), no named owner responsible (named ownership), and no tangible consequence if failed (consequences for failure).\"}, {\"front\": \"According to the four criteria framework, what is the key difference between a claim and a performance?\", \"back\": \"A claim has all four criteria present (specificity, verifiability, named ownership, consequences for failure). A performance has most of these conditions absent, making it a statement designed to produce feeling without accountability.\"}, {\"front\": \"How should a critical thinker treat verifiability according to the lecture's warning against Mistake 2?\", \"back\": \"Treat verifiability as a scale, not a yes/no question. The question is whether someone other than the organization could verify the claim, with third-party audits offering stronger evidence than self-reporting.\"}, {\"front\": \"What is the risk of assuming organizational claims are all empty performances, according to the lecture's examiner gotcha?\", \"back\": \"Assuming the worst and believing everything an organization says are both uncritical extremes. The lecture cautions against overcorrection: many claims genuinely pass all four conditions, and internal resistance often fights to replace vague language with specific commitments.\"}]",
