      "transcript_text": "After having talked about benefits of diversification, now let us talk about 2 different kinds of risks, specific risk and market risk. Let us begin our discussion with specific risk. What is specific risk? Specific risk is that risk which is peculiar to a firm or an industry. For example, if we talk about, let us say, a pilot strike. A pilot strike would affect aviation industry in general, and it would have very little effect on other industries. So therefore, this particular risk is a specific risk because it is specific to the aviation industry, and this risk can be eliminated by diversification. If you keep on adding more and more securities or stocks, especially from other industries in your portfolio, what will happen is that your specific risk will keep reducing with every addition of new security. Now, since this risk can be eliminated by diversification, this risk is also called as diversifiable risk. Specific risk is also known by many other names. It is also called as unsystematic risk, unique risk or idiosyncratic risk. Now, let us talk about market risk. What is market risk? Market risk is that risk which is shared by most businesses. For example, if we talk about a crisis such as a global financial crisis or COVID-19 pandemic. So, most of the businesses were affected by these shocks. Of course, some businesses were affected more by these shocks and some were affected less by these shocks. And therefore, this kind of a risk is what we call as the market risk, the risk which stems from market-wide fluctuations. Now, this risk cannot be eliminated by diversification, and therefore this risk is also called as non-diversifiable risk. Also, market risk is also known by the name of systematic risk. Now obviously as you understand, any security will have a total risk which will comprise of specific risk and market risk. So therefore, total risk of a security is equal to specific risk and market risk. You also understand now that diversification reduces risk. However, you can only diversify away specific risk, but you cannot diversify away market risk. Now, if you have a reasonably well-diversified portfolio, so which risk do you think will be present in your portfolio? It will only have or primarily have the market risk. Because your portfolio is reasonably well diversified, your portfolio will not carry any specific risk.",
