          "model_answer": "The model answer should be structured as follows:\n\n**1. Definition and Scope (3 marks)**\n- Specific risk is peculiar to a firm or an industry (e.g., a pilot strike affecting aviation).\n- Market risk is shared by most businesses and stems from market-wide fluctuations (e.g., global financial crisis, COVID-19 pandemic).\n\n**2. Alternative Names and Decomposition (2 marks)**\n- Specific risk is also called unsystematic risk, unique risk, or idiosyncratic risk.\n- Market risk is also called non-diversifiable risk or systematic risk.\n- Total risk of a security is equal to specific risk plus market risk.\n\n**3. Effect of Diversification (4 marks)**\n- Specific risk can be eliminated by diversification. Adding more securities, especially from other industries, reduces specific risk.\n- Market risk cannot be eliminated by diversification. Even a well-diversified portfolio will primarily carry market risk.\n\n**4. Final Portfolio Composition (1 mark)**\n- In a reasonably well-diversified portfolio, specific risk is diversified away, leaving primarily market risk as the relevant risk.",
