      "transcript_text": "Let us look at another problem. Fictitious Corporation's book balance sheet reports 50% debt and 50% equity. The debt is trading close to book value at a current interest rate of 4%. The market cap of the firm's equity is double its book value. The equity beta is 1. The risk-free rate of interest is 2%, and the market risk premium is 7%. The question before us is, what is the firm's asset cost of capital? Let us first of all jot down the information which is given to us in the problem. So, beta of equity is 1. Okay. It further says the risk-free rate is 2%. And the market risk premium. What is market risk premium? If you recall, market risk premium is nothing but Rm minus Rf, and it is given to us as 7%. Now, it also says that the debt is trading close to the book value at a current interest rate of 4%. So, which means that RD is given to us as 4%. Now, we have to find out asset cost of capital for the firm. We understand that our asset cost of capital, RA equals weighted average of the cost of debt and weighted average of the cost of equity. Here, as you recall, both D and E have to be in terms of their market values, not in terms of their book values. Okay? If we look at the problem, what does it say? It says that the debt is trading close to its book value. Let us denote market value of debt by letter D. And in this case, it is given to us that the debt is trading close to its book value. So, which means that the market value of debt will be equal to its book value. BV stands for book value and MV stands for market value. Now, let us look at market value of equity. Now, let us denote market value of equity by letter E. What does the problem say? The problem says the market cap of the firm's equity. Market cap means market capitalization. It also means market value of equity. So, it says that market value of equity is double its book value. So, which means that market value of equity is double the book value of equity. Now, if we look at the book value balance sheet, what does it say? It says that the book value balance sheet reports 50% debt and 50% equity. If X denotes total book value of assets of the firm. So, in that case, what will be your book value of debt? Since debt comprises 50% of the book value of assets, so therefore it will simply be 0.5X. Here, what is book value of equity? Book value of equity again is 0.5X, and therefore if you multiply, you will simply get X here. Now, let us substitute these values in our asset cost of capital formula. What will it give us? P 0.5X divided by 0.5X plus 1X into RD. Our RD is 4% plus E, which is X divided by 0.5x plus x into RE. Now, RE is not given to us. The question is, can we compute RE here? Let us try to compute RE here. What will RE be? If you use capital asset pricing model, so using capital asset pricing model, we can compute RE. RE equals Rf plus beta of equity into Rm minus Rf. Rf is given to us as 2%. Beta of equity is 1 into Rm minus Rf is 7%. So, we get our RE as 9%. And if we substitute here the value of RE, what do we get here? If you find this out, it will be 1 by 3 into 4% plus 2 by 3 into 9%. It will be 1.33% plus 6%. What does that give you? It gives you 7.33%. So, what do we conclude? We conclude that 7.33% is our asset cost of capital.",
