        {
          "front": "Why does the price per share decrease after a cash dividend but remain unchanged after a share repurchase at market rate?",
          "back": "Cash dividend reduces total market value of equity with fixed share count, forcing price down. Share repurchase at market rate reduces both cash and market value proportionally, leaving the price per share unchanged mechanically."
        },
        {
          "front": "In a stock dividend or stock split, why does the price per share decrease while the total market value of equity remains unchanged?",
          "back": "The total equity value is conserved, but it is divided among a greater number of shares (increased share count), mechanically reducing the price per share inverse to the increase in share quantity."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the key mechanical difference between a stock dividend and a reverse split regarding the number of shares outstanding?",
          "back": "Both increase price per share impact inversely, but a stock dividend increases the number of shares outstanding, whereas a reverse split decreases the number of shares outstanding."
        },
        {
          "front": "If a firm repurchases shares at a price higher than the prevailing market rate, what happens to the price per share of the remaining shares?",
          "back": "The price per share of the remaining shares increases, as the firm is paying a premium, which mechanically reduces the share count at a higher cost per remaining share."
        }
