          "model_answer": "**Part A: Conceptual Explanation of Underpricing**\nUnderpricing occurs when the offer price set by the company and underwriters is lower than the first day closing price in the secondary market. Mathematically, it is calculated as (First Day Closing Price - Offer Price) / Offer Price. The primary purpose of underpricing is twofold: first, it encourages participation from less informed investors by mitigating the \"winner's curse,\" where investors who receive full allocation are likely in weak issues; second, it helps underwriters manage the risk of unsold shares.\n\n**Part B: Identification of Cost Bearer**\nThe economic cost of underpricing is borne by the pre-IPO shareholders. By setting the offer price below the eventual market price, pre-IPO owners forego potential proceeds. While investors benefit from immediate gains and underwriters benefit from reduced risk, the cost of the discount is effectively a transfer from the pre-IPO shareholders to the new issuers.\n\n**Part C: Numerical Illustration**\nUsing the example from the lecture: Tata Capital Limited's IPO in 2025 had an offer price of 326 INR and a first day closing price of 331.10 INR. The degree of underpricing is (331.10 - 326) / 326 = 5.10 / 326 ≈ 1.6%. This means pre-IPO shareholders effectively left approximately 1.6% in value on the table compared to what the market priced the shares at immediately after listing.\n\n**Rubric:**\n- Core conceptual understanding of underpricing definition and purpose: 5 marks.\n- Correct identification of pre-IPO shareholders as the cost bearer: 3 marks.\n- Accurate application of the formula with correct calculation: 2 marks.",
