      "notes_markdown": "# Investor Behaviour in IPOs\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** SEBI's 2024 study of Indian IPOs (Apr 2018-Dec 2023) reveals that over half of allotted shares are sold within a week of listing, driven by a strong disposition effect where investors prematurely sell winners and hold losers, with banks and individual investors from Gujarat exhibiting the most aggressive short-term trading behavior.\n\n**Formulas & Worked Numerical:** \n- Disposition effect: Investors sell 67.6% of shares within a week when returns >20%, but only 23.3% when returns are negative.\n### Investor Behaviour In IPOs\n- 54% of IPO shares (value terms) sold off within a week from listing.\n- Individual investors sold 50.2% of allotted value within a week.\n- Mutual funds sold 3.3% of allotted value within a week (long-term orientation).\n- Banks sold 79.8% of allotted value within a week (short-term orientation).\n- Disposition effect: Prematurely sell winners; hold on to losers.\n- Individual investors sold 67.6% of shares within a week when returns >20% vs. 23.3% when returns negative.\n- Retail investors predominantly from Gujarat (39.3%), Maharashtra (13.5%), Rajasthan (10.5%).\n- Nearly half of allotted Demat accounts opened post-COVID (2021-2023).\n\n**MCQ Trap Radar:**\n1. **Trap:** Banks are long-term IPO investors holding shares for years. → **Reality:** Banks sold 79.8% of allotted value within a week, making them the most aggressive short-term traders.\n2. **Trap:** The disposition effect causes investors to sell losers prematurely and hold winners. → **Reality:** Investors exhibit the opposite tendency, prematurely selling winners and holding losers.\n3. **Trap:** Over 50% of IPO allottees hold shares for more than a month post-listing. → **Reality:** 54% of shares are sold off within a week, indicating predominantly short-term trading behavior.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"According to the SEBI study covered in the lecture (April 2018 to December 2023), what percentage of IPO shares in value terms were sold off within a week from listing across all investors?\", \"options\": [\"A) 3.3%\", \"B) 23.3%\", \"C) 54%\", \"D) 79.8%\"], \"correct_index\": 2, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is C) 54%. The lecture explicitly states that SEBI observed 54% of IPO shares in value terms were allotted to investors and sold off within a week from listing. Option A (3.3%) refers to the sell-off rate for mutual funds specifically. Option B (23.3%) refers to the sell-off rate for individual investors when returns were negative. Option D (79.8%) refers to the sell-off rate for banks specifically.\", \"key_concept\": \"Overall IPO sell-off rate within 1 week\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"How did mutual funds behave differently from banks in IPOs, according to the SEBI study findings discussed in the lecture?\", \"options\": [\"A) Mutual funds sold off 79.8% of allotted value within a week, while banks sold off 3.3%.\", \"B) Mutual funds tended to invest for a longer period, selling off only 3.3% of allotted value within a week, whereas banks sold off 79.8% within a week.\", \"C) Mutual funds and banks both sold off approximately 50% of their allotted shares within a week.\", \"D) Mutual funds did not participate in IPOs, while banks were the dominant investors.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B). The lecture clearly distinguishes the two institutional investor types: mutual funds are long-term investors, selling off only 3.3% of allotted value within a week, while banks are short-term investors, selling off 79.8% of allotted value within a week. Option A reverses the percentages. Option C is incorrect as the rates differ significantly. Option D is factually incorrect as both participated.\", \"key_concept\": \"Institutional investor behavior comparison (Mutual Funds vs. Banks)\"}, {\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"What is the disposition effect, and how did it manifest in the behavior of individual investors in IPOs during the SEBI study period?\", \"options\": [\"A) The tendency to hold onto winning stocks and sell off losing stocks immediately; individual investors sold 23.3% of shares when returns were negative within a week.\", \"B) The tendency to prematurely sell winners and hold on to losers; individual investors sold 67.6% of shares when returns were more than 20% within a week, and only 23.3% when returns were negative.\", \"C) The tendency to average down on losing positions; individual investors bought more shares when IPO returns were negative.\", \"D) The tendency to invest only in IPOs from their home state; individual investors from Gujarat exhibited the strongest disposition effect.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B). The lecture defines the disposition effect as the tendency to prematurely sell winners and holding on to losers. The data shows individual investors sold 67.6% of shares by value within a week when returns were more than 20% (selling winners prematurely), and only sold 23.3% when returns were negative (holding on to losers). Option A reverses the behavior. Option C describes averaging down, which was not discussed. Option D incorrectly ties the effect to geography rather than behavioral psychology.\", \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"key_concept\": \"Disposition Effect in IPO Investors\"}, {\"id\": 4, \"question\": \"Based on the SEBI study geographic data, which Indian state comprised the highest percentage of retail investors in IPOs during the study period?\", \"options\": [\"A) Maharashtra\", \"B) Rajasthan\", \"C) Gujarat\", \"D) Tamil Nadu\"], \"correct_index\": 2, \"difficulty\": \"Easy | Conceptual\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is C) Gujarat. The lecture explicitly states that Gujarat comprised 39.3% of retail investors, followed by Maharashtra at 13.5% and Rajasthan at 10.5%. Option A (Maharashtra) is the second highest, Option B (Rajasthan) is third, and Option D (Tamil Nadu) is not mentioned in the top three states provided in the lecture data.\", \"key_concept\": \"Retail Investor Geographic Distribution\"}]",
