          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Easy | Conceptual",
          "explanation": "This question tests the direct formula comparison from the lecture. The lecture states: Value of 1 North American Right = (Px - Ps) / N. Since N > 1 (several rights are required to buy 1 new share), the denominator reduces the value. For European rights, the value is simply (Px - Ps). Therefore, for the same Px and Ps, the European right value (Px - Ps) is strictly greater than the North American right value ((Px - Ps) / N) because N > 1. Option A is a common distractor for those who confuse the ratio structure. Option C ignores the N parameter. Option D misinterprets the concept of a right as a certificate with standalone value.",
          "key_concept": "Formula comparison: North American vs. European rights value"
