      "transcript_text": "Now, let us look at a problem on rights offerings. Fictonia Corporation has decided to obtain additional equity financing to expand its operations through a rights offering. It has correctly determined that as a result of the rights offering, the share price will fall from Rs. 65 to Rs. 63.18 per share. The company is seeking Rs. 15 million in additional funds with a per share subscription price equal to Rs. 50. So, the question before you is how many shares are there currently before the rights offering? Assume that the increment to the market value of equity equals the gross proceeds from the offering. In other words, you can ignore the issue expenses. So, let us try to jot down these facts. So, we understand that the rights on price, which means that the price before the rights offering, this price is Rs. 65 per share. And also, if you look at ex rights price, what is your ex, what is your ex-rights price? So, this is the price after the rights issue. So, this is given to you as 63.18 rupees. Okay? How much funds is the company raising through the rights issue? The company is raising funds to the tune of Rs. 50 million in their rights offering. And what is your subscription price? So the subscription price, which is the offer price. Let us denote it by Ps. It is given to us as Rs. And we have to find out how many shares are there currently before the rights issue. So, can we find out the number of shares that the firm is issuing in the rights offering? Number of, number of new shares issued. This is equal to your funds raised divided by the price per share at which you are raising funds, which is nothing but your subscription price. So, this is equal to Rs. 15 million divided by Rs. 50. What does that give you? So, that gives you 0.3 million. So therefore, the firm is issuing 0.3 million new shares in the rights offering. Now, let us say that X is going to denote the number of shares outstanding before the rights offering. Number of shares outstanding, And let us say that this number is in million. Okay. Now, we understand that market value of equity, which I am going to denote by MVE. So, market value of equity after the rights issue is equal to market value of equity before the rights issue plus the funds raised. So, you understand that your market value of equity is essentially going to go up by the amount of funds you are raising in the rights offering. So, what is your market value of equity after? number of shares outstanding after multiplied by price per share after the rights offering. This is equal to MV before which we can write as shares before the rights offering into price before the rights offering plus amount of funds you are raising in the rights offering. So, what does this imply? So, how many shares are there after the rights issue? So, we understand that X is the number of shares outstanding before the rights issue and you are issuing 0.3 million new shares in the rights offering. So therefore, number of shares outstanding after the rights issue is X plus 0.3. What is the price after the rights issue? It is given to us as 63.18, okay? This is equal to number of shares before the rights offering, which is X multiplied by price before, which is nothing but 65. So therefore, it is 65X plus funds raised. How much funds are you raising? You are raising 15 million rupees in funds. So, this implies that 63.18X plus 18.954 equals 65X plus 15. So, this implies 18.954 minus 15 equals 65X minus 63.18X. So, this implies 3.954 equals 1.82 X. So, this implies our X is equal to 3.954 divided by 1.82. What does that give you? That gives you 2.173 million. So, this tells you that the firm had 2.173 million shares before the rights offering. So, this is what the question was asking us to find out.",
