          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "The lecture explicitly cautions against over-reliance on checklists: \"Just because we have this checklist doesn't mean we're going to be right. It's just that we're just being cautious... Many years ago, I'm told, I don't know if it is true, but kind of makes sense, that when Infosys went out for investments... investors who passed Infy request to invest... because they said, 'I don't think that the co-founders are going to stick.'\" The correct answer reflects the nuance that checklists exist but are not foolproof, using the Infosys example to show that even strong teams face skepticism.\n\n    Why distractors are wrong:\n    - A: The lecture directly contradicts the idea that checklists guarantee success.\n    - C: The lecture does not advise avoiding checklists entirely; it advises being cautious beyond them.\n    - D: The lecture discusses co-founder selection generally, not restricting checklists to solo founders.",
