      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain the distinction between 'outside-facing' and 'inside-facing' roles in a founding team and why this split is critical for a prospective joinee to evaluate. Use a real-world example from the lecture to support your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The lecture distinguishes between two complementary roles in a founding team: the outside-facing role and the inside-facing role. The outside-facing founder is oriented towards the external world, handling partnerships, fundraising, and market positioning, essentially telling the company's story to the outside. The inside-facing founder is oriented towards the internal engine, focusing on product, technical architecture, and operational details that customers never see. This split is critical for a prospective joinee to evaluate because the ownership of the role the joinee will report to must be real and clear, not assumed. If a joinee cannot determine who owns their reporting role, they risk being exposed to team dynamics they cannot control.\n\nReal-world example: The lecture cites the Amagi team (Bhaskar, Srividya, and Srini) as a successful diverse team. Bhaskar looks at as CEO, talking to media and people, and has the vision (outside-facing). Srividya works inside on technical things (inside-facing). Srini does marketing and moved to the US to build the market (outside-facing/role-specific). Another example is Infosys, where Mohandas Pai was brought onto the founding team because he asked insightful questions at the AGM, demonstrating that the founders recognized the value of bringing in someone with a different perspective at the founding level, not just executive level.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of outside-facing vs. inside-facing roles",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analytical application using a real lecture example (Amagi or Infosys)",
              "marks": 5
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
