      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain the fundamental difference in agency and decision-making power between a co-founder and a first-time joinee. How should a joinee navigate the limitations of their observational access when evaluating a startup team?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The fundamental difference lies in the timing and nature of structural negotiation. A co-founder actively negotiates roles and equity before day one, effectively shaping the company's governance framework from inception. They have levers to influence the org chart and reward structure from the start. In contrast, a first-time joinee inherits whatever structure already exists. They must read the organization from outside, typically across a handful of interviews and whatever is publicly available. The joinee has no levers to change the existing structure but must still make a 2-3 year commitment.\n\nTo navigate these limitations, a joinee should focus on three actionable due diligence steps highlighted in the lecture: first, reading attrition patterns to understand stability; second, examining equity documentation to understand ownership distribution and any potential dilution; third, reading the posh committee (investor composition) to identify who holds real decision-making power. Additionally, the joinee must learn to distinguish between founder mode and manager mode behaviors, recognizing that founder self-descriptions are unreliable (nobody describes themselves as a micromanager) and that conventional advice to 'get out of the way' may be dangerous if delegation happens too early. The joinee must actively search for signals of mission drift or healthy founder involvement despite having limited direct access.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of co-founder vs. joinee agency difference",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Application of actionable due diligence frameworks (attrition, equity, posh committee)",
              "marks": 5
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
