      "notes_markdown": "# Organisational Challenges in Data and Dashboards\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** A hybrid dashboard structure, combining a shared core of 5 common KPIs with department-specific tabs, optimises efficiency at TrendE, balancing minimal maintenance with the distinct analytical needs of sales, marketing, and operations.\n### Departmental Metric Requirements & Splits\n- **5 Shared KPIs (Overview Page):** Gross merchandise value, total orders, average order value, conversion rate, and return rate are required by all 3 departments and form the single source of truth on the overview page.\n- **Department-Specific Metrics:** Marketing requires customer acquisition cost, return on ad spend, and cart abandonment rate; operations requires sell-through rate by fulfillment center, on-time delivery percentage, and order cycle time; sales requires gross margin percentage and return rate by product category.\n- **Splits & Breakdowns:** Each department wants the core metrics split by their functional category (e.g., sales wants return rate by product category; marketing by channel/campaign; operations by fulfillment center/carrier).\n- **Device & Format Awareness:** Dashboard layout must account for viewing devices, sales primarily uses laptops, while operations views on phones in the warehouse, directly influencing responsive design decisions.\n- **Phase-Wise Metric Logging:** Non-priority metrics (e.g., social media engagement) are explicitly logged as Phase 2 items to maintain user trust and roadmap transparency.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** A one-size-fits-all dashboard is always the most efficient choice because it requires the least maintenance. → **Reality:** This approach creates high visual clutter and dilutes the minimal view each department needs, making it only suitable when departments have heavy workflow overlap, not just shared numbers.\n2. **Trap:** Fragmented dashboards (separate for each department) ensure data consistency and eliminate the risk of conflicting metrics. → **Reality:** Fragmented dashboards are expensive to maintain and carry a real risk of calculating metrics (like revenue) differently across data sources, with no single leadership overview point.\n3. **Trap:** The hybrid dashboard approach is overly complex and unnecessary for any organisation. → **Reality:** The hybrid approach is the right choice when teams share a real core but have genuinely distinct operational needs, as it takes careful upfront layout planning but balances efficiency with user-specific requirements.",
