      "notes_markdown": "# KPIs vs Diagnostics in the Business Context\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** A dashboard is a small connected set of screens, not a single page, built from five universal building blocks: pages/tabs, KPI cards, filters, navigation, and hover/click interactions.\n\n- **Pages/Tabs:** Most real dashboards have multiple screens; viewers move between them based on need.\n- **KPI Cards:** Few (usually under 10) prominent figures placed near the top, showing the most important metrics at a glance.\n- **Filters (Slicers):** Controls (region, date range, product category) that narrow the view; the rest of the dashboard updates to match the selection.\n- **Navigation:** Obvious, easy ways to move between pages/tabs (tabs, sidebar menus, or built-in buttons).\n- **Hover/Click Interactions:** Pop-ups or expansions revealing detail on demand (e.g., exact figures on hover) to contain clutter.\n### Dashboard Components & Layout\n- **KPI cards → Prominent top placement → Instant metric visibility.**\n- **Filters → User selection → Dynamic update of all other visual elements.**\n- **Navigation → Clear pathway → Seamless movement between dashboard pages.**\n- **Hover interactions → On-demand detail → Reduced screen clutter.**\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n*No formulas or numerical calculations were presented in this lecture.*\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Believing a dashboard should contain all data on a single screen for completeness. → **Reality:** Effective dashboards use multiple pages/tabs to segment information, improving user focus and decision-making speed.\n2. **Trap:** Thinking filters are optional decorative elements. → **Reality:** Filters are essential controls that transform a static display into an interactive tool, allowing one dashboard to serve many different analytical views.",
