          "model_answer": "The strategic rationale for phase-wise development is to deliver immediate business value via a Minimum Viable Product (Phase 1) rather than waiting for 100% completeness, which the lecture identifies as the enemy of progress. Phase 1 is defined as the intersection of highest-priority essential user requirements and currently feasible, clean data from the data audit. This approach allows stakeholders to gain functional visibility in weeks rather than months.\n\nThe lecture identifies three types of bottlenecks that can force metrics out of Phase 1: system bottlenecks (e.g., the Fairhouse system not tracking sell-through rate consistently across locations), process bottlenecks (e.g., marketing requesting return rate refreshed by the 1st of the month while the logistics partner delivers on the 7th), and governance bottlenecks (e.g., media spend numbers changing by May 1st due to vendor credit adjustments after being reported on April 1st).\n\nThe assigned Phase 1 scope acts as a shield against scope creep. When managers request additional metrics or views after seeing the live dashboard, the team points to the roadmap and logs these requests into Phase 2, protecting the core timeline. This is necessary because stakeholders naturally have unfolding needs post-launch, and without a structured roadmap, these ad-hoc requests accumulate into chaotic scope creep.\n\nThe Infosys example (over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares) illustrates why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers, which parallels the need for a structured development delegation via a roadmap. The Tyco International example (CEO spending $2M on his wife's birthday party and billing $1M to the company) illustrates agency costs, which parallels the need for structured sign-offs and scope management to prevent fiscal and operational overreach in dashboard projects.\n\nThe formal sign-off process secures explicit confirmation on Phase 1 scope from senior sponsors before any visualization is built, ensuring all teams (IT, marketing, logistics) understand the boundaries and can work in parallel to clear operational roadblocks.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of phase-wise development rationale: 4 marks\n- Identification and explanation of the three bottleneck types with examples: 3 marks\n- Explanation of Phase 1 scope as a shield against scope creep: 2 marks\n- Integration of lecture examples (Infosys/Tyco parallels or specific bottleneck cases) for depth: 1 mark",
