      "notes_markdown": "# Planning Information Flow and Visual Hierarchy\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Effective dashboards prioritize scannability over decoration; the most important number must be top-left, largest, and stylistically distinct, while the overall flow should move from high-level health checks to drill-down diagnostics using progressive disclosure.\n### Visual Hierarchy Principles\n- **Size:** Bigger numbers pull the eye first; GMV must be the largest number on the overview page.\n- **Color & Contrast:** A splash of color against a muted background pulls attention sparingly.\n- **Position:** Top-left corner is the natural landing spot due to left-to-right reading patterns.\n- **Proximity:** Related metrics (e.g., total orders and average order value) should sit next to each other to signal they are read together.\n- **Typography:** Font weight and size signal importance; a bold large number next to a small label creates hierarchy.\n\n### Information Hierarchy vs. Flow\n- **Hierarchy (within a screen):** Making a page scannable so the viewer quickly finds the important pieces. Defined by the 5 tools above.\n- **Flow (across pages):** Starting high, then letting people drill down. The viewer gets a broad read on the business in the first few seconds, then moves to specific pages only if something looks off.\n\n### 3-Question Test for Overview Pages\n1. **Do we know where to start?**\n2. **Do we know what is next?**\n3. **Can we tell when we are done?**\n\nAn overview page must show top-level numbers AND give a clear path forward to everything else.\n\n### User Tiers & Dashboard Design\n- **Executive (CMO/CXO):** Simple, minimalist dashboards monitoring business health vs. targets with trends. Minimal learning required; designed for print/export to PowerPoint.\n- **Mid-level (Category/Regional Manager):** Highly interactive dashboards allowing drill-down for diagnosis and action steps. Built for analysis; must show what the executive sees with more context.\n- **Operational (Store Manager/Support):** Simple dashboards designed for immediate decisions with alerts/notifications. Primary goal is spotting red flags and taking action, not analysis.\n\n### Progressive Disclosure\nEnable users to climb the ladder of diagnostics without dumping everything on the page:\n1. **Hover for context:** Small detail appears visually without leaving the screen.\n2. **Expand in place:** Clicking a card reveals the next layer (e.g., category breakdown) right there.\n3. **Click through to detail page:** Land on a different page built for deep investigation (e.g., conversion rate by campaign).\n\n### Wireframing\nBefore choosing chart types or colors, sketch a structural wireframe (boxes only) showing where KPI cards sit, where charts go, where filters live, and how navigation connects pages. This stage tests **structure**, not aesthetics. Sign-off on the wireframe is required before proceeding to specific visuals.\n\n***\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Placing the most important KPI in the bottom-right corner ensures it is seen last during a left-to-right scan. → **Reality:** The top-left corner is the natural landing spot; the most important number must be placed there first.\n2. **Trap:** Using five identical KPI cards in a row ensures consistent attention on all metrics. → **Reality:** Attention drops off sharply by the fourth or fifth card; varying style/size prevents repetition blindness.\n3. **Trap:** A dashboard filled with decorative elements and colors effectively engages the user. → **Reality:** Decoration competes with important numbers for attention; a dashboard's job is to communicate fast, not to impress.",
