      "transcript_text": "In this module, let us see what are institutions. What are the various kinds of institutions and how do they impact family businesses? So there are some theories about what kinds of institutions are there, the nature of the institutions. Richard Scott has a theory on institutions which are regulative, they are normative or are they cultural cognitive? Similarly, Douglas North has a different way of seeing institutions. They are formal institutions and informal institutions. So these kinds of institutions, how do they impact the family business and the formal informal gap that exists? How does this gap matter for family business is what we'll see in this module. Institutions really are more than laws. Like Richard Scott says, they are composed of cultural, cognitive, normative and regulative elements. What are each of these? We'll look in more detail. Similarly, like North says, institutions are the rules of the game in a society that are applicable for non-family as well. We'll understand as to how different they are for family businesses. [snorts] In both these definitions, they just don't point to just the just the books or the statute. The family business is actually governed as much by by the statute, as much as by the laws, but also what their peers expect, which is sometimes much more than what the law requires. So we shall see each of them in in detail now. Let's see Scott's three pillars of institutions. What is the regulative kind of an institution? This is an institution where compliance and following the rule is very important or you'll face sanction. Mechanism is usually for ensuring happens is coercion, sometimes legal force. A family business example would be the company law actually dictating the board composition and the succession formalities, for example, otherwise which would have been otherwise outside regulation if they had to do it by themselves. Normative normative is more by as a compliance by an obligation. In this case a social obligation. It's not something that is always based on self-interest. So how is the mechanism work in this case? Mechanism is more a professional socialization or a certification. So in a family business this kind of an example would be an industry and professional codes of governance, family norms about the the way business should conduct themselves is far more important here. Like I said, it's more social obligation that the family faces to how to conduct themselves. In a cultural cognitive kind of a of a system is compliance is is taken for granted, but the mechanism here is more of shared beliefs and logics of of action. And a family business example is about the belief that the founder family control is the legitimate form of ownership. Now if I go to a formal versus informal institution kind of a difference, in a formal institution things are very very explicit, written down, constitutions, rights, company law, these are all a formal institutions. These are enforced by the state or some other third party, but it's usually enforced from outside. And these can change overnight. Almost like you enact a law, one one act is passed in parliament or in the government and overnight things can change. For example, the Companies Act, the the SEBI listing rules or the the insolvency and bankruptcy code which we'll all see in in some detail as we go forward. Informal things are very very unwritten. They are conventions, the code of conducts, what is followed is tradition, what are followed are norms, what is not done is something that has been understood as taboo over years. These are enforced through social sanction, through reputation. And these are very, very deeply embedded in the culture of the organization and change very, very slowly. Even after rules change, sometimes adaptation to the of these rules of regulations by the family business is slow because of its culture. So kinship and caste community, trust networks, how is family succession, the scripts, all these are form of informal kind of a regulation. The formal informal gap is really where family firms live and and thrive. For example, where formal institutions, whether you take it as SEBI, companies law, a court, a bankruptcy regime, they are weak or perhaps not evenly enforced. That is where informal institutions step in to do the coordinating work. So the [clears throat] kinship networks we spoke about, the reputation mechanisms, all find the space that formal institutions have have left open or not not addressed. It is also why family controlled firms are not an accident in in emerging markets. So the concentrated trust based ownership is actually a good response to the to the thin formalization or a very rational response to thin formal institutions. This is not just a cultural preference. So if you see the overall five kind of mechanisms and and kind of regulations that we see, so it's regulatory, normative, it's talked about cognitive, formal and informal kind of a system that exists. Each of them has a a different way of impacting a family business. So regulatory is more rule setting and sanctioning by the state. Normative is bowling is more about expectations, social obligation. Cognitive is taken for granted that define even what is not imaginable. Formal is about written explicit rules and informal is about unwritten conventions which are more enforced by a social sanction or reputation. These are the kind of things that actually could define institutions and family firms have to abide by some of these institutions. And as we go ahead in this in this module, we'll see how family firms adapt to these.",
