      "transcript_text": "We will discuss as to what the family business must do to tackle some of these pressures. Also, there are forces that are being now acting on the family business, external forces also. So let's understand those forces which are shaping fundamentally the family business and what family businesses must do to address them. So there are four forces that we we will talk about in this in this module. Those four forces are geopolitics, AI, ESG, and the generational change itself and what the change is bringing. So these are four forces. They are also hitting every firm. So some of these are not unique to family business except the generational change. Geopolitics, AI and ESG are hitting every firm. But the way they act on the family business is slightly different and therefore what family business must do to address them will also be slightly different. So we'll go through them in in detail in this module. So let's first look at three numbers that are being talked about a lot. First is that geoeconomic confrontation, which is a mix of both geopolitical, geographic and economic confrontation is now ranked as the top risk for businesses. And businesses today expect a multipolar, fragmented world. There's a lot more inward looking, lot more protectionist environment that is forming. This maybe 25 years back we used to talk about how the world is flat. Now no longer people are saying world is flat because people are looking a lot more inward. The other other number is that two thirds of organizations are using generative AI regularly. But not many are it up. They're all started using AI, but maybe only half of them are actually scaling it up. And the last number is 35% of later generation family firms. And those with strong governance are the ones who operate AI at some scale much ahead of the same kind of firms, but with a much weaker governance than they only are at 26%. So basically governance is giving a Philip to AI. So these are three numbers that comes out of some amount of research among companies. Let's get back to the four forces and important point in this to note is while the forces are common to every firm family firms feel them differently and that's what we should see it in more detail. So let's look at geopolitics first as a force. It's really supposed to be the top ranked force today. There are incidentally some companies who have started appointing somebody in equivalent of foreign minister who understands where the company should do business, what kind of risks that entail if we go into this geography, into this kind of a political scenario. So there was a time when Dubai was considered very safe and today people are asking questions on whether that is safe. See how much geography and geopolitics can change a business's outlook. What does it mean for a family business? While this geopolitics affects all businesses family businesses have to think slightly differently. Many times family businesses are one business. They are in one sector, in one vertical, many times in one country. Then the family wealth and it's all tied to this. So if something were to go wrong in that particular area the family business has to face a lot more flak. While a non family business many times are diversified in more than one vertical, sometimes more than one country, they have two three things going. Something goes down, something something still keeps them afloat. While it's very different for a family firm. Artificial intelligence is the second force. We spoke about how two third are using it regularly, but only one third scale it and the difference is splits by both governance and to some extent generation. So when I say generation, you should also see how AI use is playing out. There are two aspects. One is adoption and one is scaling. AI adoption interacts with one generation in the sense that the younger generation more or less will adopt AI faster than the earlier generation. Something that is intuitive, we can understand that. However, governance will predict scaling. Even though one generation may the younger generation more likely will adopt AI faster, it's the senior generation which will still govern it. So scaling AI and that's a force which is important for us for family businesses to reckon with will scale because of the kind of governance and institutions, those will determine how AI scales. Third force that is acting is ESG and climate policy. Now this is this force is not proceeding in a linear way. It's it's going back and forth. Countries like I said when they become protectionist, they are dropping these mandates. So let's say US SEC has dropped its climate disclosure defense. EU EU Omnibus has not relegated it, but they have certainly narrowed it, narrowed the scope of it. So all this reversal has a different way of affecting the family business. So, if if I'm a family business and I've said that ESG norms are something I'm very that's very important to me, close to our heart. We want to do things that are sustainable. We want to do that are environmentally friendly. And because things are reversed in this ESG policy worldwide, if I also reverse it and I start adopting a less or a lighter agenda on ESG, what happens is the family name gets a hit. The reputation gets a hit. So, this is the way this kind of a force will affect the family business. World over it is getting changed because everyone is turned inward looking and are looking at what kind of policy will help us grow and not something for the whole world. They are looking at themselves much more in insular, I would say, the way things are started looking in in foreign policy across the world. So, these have an impact on on family businesses too. Lastly, demographics and wealth transfer. This is supposed to be the era when the greatest wealth transfer is supposed to happen between now and about 2050 kind of a thing world over. Wealth generated by the so-called boomer generation is going to be transferred down the line to the next current generation. Also, the other thing is that skills that were once seen very important are are soon going to be fairly outdated in less than a decade. How how do these impact family business? The next generation will not only inherit the capital that was built by the earlier generation. It also therefore inherits the forces. So, it inherits the forces that are acting on the business on the businesses and the and the pressures that we talked about earlier. Therefore, succession will also need to not just confront us to what they have to build, but also they have to build capability for that also. So while they may have succeeded, they have to really start from zero because capability is something they have to build from scratch because what they require for their generation and going forward for the next 30 years is not going to be the same as what the earlier 30 years were for the previous generation. These forces are playing very differently on on on family businesses. >> [music]",
