          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The lecture defines patient capital as family firms being willing to accept a larger payback time, unlike private capital which wants a shorter turnaround time. The professor explained that patient capital makes multi-year commitments like \"China plus one\" financeable because family firms are not under \"quarter pressure.\" Distractor A is incorrect because the lecture emphasizes that family firms prioritize durability and control over short-term high returns. Distractor C is incorrect as the lecture does not suggest family firms typically replace management to please external investors; rather, they persevere through challenges. Distractor D, while plausible, misses the core conflict of investment horizon highlighted in the lecture.",
