      "transcript_text": "In this section, let's talk about family offices. Great wealth transfer that is supposed to happen in this era and in such also a turbulent era. We spoke about the kind of forces that are acting on on businesses in general. How does succession actually pan out? So we know that trillions of dollars are changing hands from what is supposed to be a boomer generation to the current generation and we spoke about the four forces. Let us see some of them in more detail in this section. On family offices, we know that there is a huge increase in the number of family offices. But there is also other forces. There are also geopolitical uncertainty that is happening. There is a home bias which is largely being seen increasingly. We spoke about it. Things being insular. People are more inward looking. So family firms, family offices plan to hold portfolio risk at 2024 levels even though risk is actually increasing with time. Even though there is a global trade war, trade war is their number one identified investment risk. Still want they want to hold the portfolio risk at that levels. 53% of family offices globally have a formal succession plan. And among those who do, only 20% consulted 26% consulted the next generation. Which means they are doing their succession of family office at a much slower level than succession of business itself happens. What it is that family offices are being patient. They are not junking their portfolio. They are holding. There is uncertainty. There is a risk of war. They identify that as a huge risk. Still, what we see is family offices are holding on. They are being patient. Another comparison that we can do between next gen obligations of people in India and in China. And this is a very interesting one. In India, only 7% of the second and third generation entrepreneurs are are feeling obligated to take over the family business. While in China, this number is about 60%. So Indian subsequent generation people are don't feel so important that they should come into the family business. While that sense of belonging and identity of the family business and their wanting we spoke about how effective feeling should be there, wanting to join the family business is much higher in China. In India, 95% feel trusted when they do step in versus 81% global average. In India also 83% feel empowered to pursue outside interest also when leading. And 78 79% still plan to pass on the business to the to the family. Again, when they step out, pass on the business to the next generation in the family, which is similar to what the UK and Switzerland numbers are. China is presenting a very different picture. 60% feel obligated to take over. And the same result shows from both places saying that India is 1/8 of that. Only 7 or 8% are looking to join the family business. So what does it show? It shows that the cultural script over succession is very different in these country in these two countries. What they owe the family in terms of coming and taking over and and leading seems to be a very different thought process in both India and China. So what we are trying to say here is that the next generation is not something that is homogeneous. It's not that we can treat the entire family business across the world as one uniform homogeneous cohort which will behave the same way. You see in just two countries it's very very different. Succession now is requiring a very different kind of readiness. They are coming into the business at the same time and there are a lot of changes happening. There are a lot of other forces happening. AI is a force, geopolitical exposure, geopolitical turmoil is a force, ESG is a force. So it's not like we have reached a steady state and the family the successor is just coming into and maintaining the steady state. By the time the earlier generation is handed over so much has changed in the outside world the successor successor is unable to say that this is the same world that my father came into or stepped into or has left me. And the change is not something that is being gradual either. It's extremely fast. So Deloitte says that 42% companies, family enterprises in the US which are above 100 million cite that AI use is a is a top priority for them. More than two thirds are adding on board members with tech or emerging tech expertise because they understand the need to control this new tool and how to govern this new tool. The same report also says that 83% have formal governance frameworks and 73% have family councils. But the binding constraint is a tech literature board candidate a tech literate bound candidate. Finding them isn't easy, because you need someone who is extremely well versed with AI. And that's been a challenge for family companies because they know they have to step into this in this domain. They have to they can't shy away from it for too long. The successors understand that their world has changed. So then how does how do they do this? How do they ensure governance of AI also happens? So the real succession actually we see is how can you govern? It's not just about how do you just get the new title. The tide is changing outside. How do you govern that governance quality? Not just the generation. That will predict whether a family firm scales AI successfully. And therefore reframes the succession planning itself. The planning stage itself they have to be aware that AI will change things. The organization of the successor will look very different from the organization of the incumbent who has handed over. So succession planning itself change. So the important thing in a handover must be the governance capability. So this is really the the bridge on how family firms are actually responding to the forces. The bridge is better governance. >> [music]",
