      "notes_markdown": "# Introduction to the Course\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** This course provides a conceptual foundation in corporate finance, covering the three core decisions, **investment, financing, and payout**, while introducing key frameworks like time value of money, bond/stock valuation, capital budgeting, risk/return (CAPM), and M&A analysis.\n### Three Pillars Of Corporate Finance\n- **Three Pillars of Corporate Finance:** Investment decisions (where to allocate capital), financing decisions (how to fund assets), and payout decisions (how to return cash to shareholders).\n- **Time Value of Money:** Compounding grows value; discounting reduces future cash flows to present value. Interest can be quoted as **APR** (Annual Percentage Rate) or **EAR** (Effective Annual Rate), where EAR accounts for compounding frequency.\n- **Business Organization Forms:** Sole proprietorships (single owner), partnerships (shared ownership), and corporations (separate legal entity with limited liability).\n- **Financial Goal of the Corporation:** Maximize firm value, balancing stakeholder interests while navigating **agency costs**, the conflicts of interest between managers (agents) and shareholders (principals).\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Maximizing profit is the sole goal of a corporation. → **Reality:** The primary goal is to maximize shareholder wealth/ firm value, not short-term profit.\n2. **Trap:** Agency costs only arise in large corporations. → **Reality:** Agency costs exist whenever there is a separation of ownership and control, including in partnerships and small firms.\n3. **Trap:** The time value of money only applies to long-term investments. → **Reality:** TVM applies to any decision involving cash flows across time, even simple savings or loan choices.",
