      "created_at": "2026-11-15 08:07:18",
      "transcript_text": "[music] >> So, let us start. Now, firms use mergers and acquisitions as a major tool of growth. For example, if we talk about Google, it has made more than 250 acquisitions. If we talk about Oracle, it has acquired more than 150 companies. An M&A deal typically involves several stakeholders. The buyer, which is also called as the acquirer or the bidder, the seller, which is also called as the target. In addition to these two parties, we have other stakeholders also present in an M&A deal. And these other stakeholders include advisors. For example, if we talk about investment bankers and lawyers, and also an M&A deal typically involves regulators as well. Government, Securities Market Regulator, antitrust watchdogs, and so on and so forth. Now, there are two primary mechanisms by which ownership and control of a firm can change. First of all, the target can merge with another form, which we call as the merger. Or alternatively, another firm can acquire the target, which we refer to as the acquisition. Now, irrespective of whether it is a merger or an acquisition, the acquiring entity must purchase the stock or existing assets of target for some consideration. Now this consideration can be either cash or stock or a combination of cash and stock or something else of equivalent value. Now in this module we are going to use the terms mergers, acquisitions and takeovers interchangeably throughout. Now let us look at as to what makes M&A different from ordinary investments. Why is it important to study these transactions? So M&A, as you can visualize, represent economically large transactions relative to ordinary investments. And these transactions can create as well as destroy value on a large scale. And therefore it becomes an important topic to study M&A. Now let us look at M&A activity. If we look at the global takeover market, it is highly active and on average we witness more than $2 trillion of deals every year. Also we observe that M&A often occur in waves. What are M&A waves? So M&A waves are essentially peaks of M&A activity followed by quiet troughs of few transactions in the takeover market. There are some periods when the M&A activity is high and there are some periods when the M&A activity is low. Also we observe that M&A activity is higher during economic expansions than it is during contractions. And M&A activity correlates with bull markets, which means that when the stock market is high, you will have a lot of M&As taking place. On the other hand, when the markets are low, you will have less of M&A activity. Now you must be wondering why do M&As occur in waves? Two theories have tried to explain as to why M&As occur in waves. The first one is neo-classical theory. Neo-classical theory says that M&A waves occur as firms in specific industries react to economic shocks. For example, a shock like deregulation. If an industry gets deregulated, it is likely that the industry will attract a lot of M&As. That is also a reason as to why M&A activity clusters by industry. If you look at an M&A wave and within that wave, if you look at the industry composition of the firms which are undergoing M&As, you will realize that a vast majority of these M&As are from a few industries. Now the second theory which explains as to why M&As occur in waves is overvaluation theory. This theory says that market valuations cause M&A waves, which means that more M&As happen in periods of bubbles. So according to this theory, when firm valuations deviate from fundamentals, managers use the overvalued stock of their firms as a currency to buy assets of undervalued or less overvalued firms. So you understand that when the market itself is overvalued, few firms will be more overvalued than other firms. And therefore firms which are more overvalued, they may acquire those firms which are less overvalued. So therefore overvaluation theory says that M and A occur in periods of bubbles more often. Now let us look at this figure which shows global M and A transactions by number starting from the period 1985 until the end of 2025. On average we see that there has been upward trend in the M and A activity over the years and for the last two decades or so we have had more than 30,000 deals happening every year at least. Also if we look at global M and A transactions by value over the period starting from 1985 until 2025 we observe that again over the last two decades year after year the M and A transactions by value have exceeded $2 trillion every year. Now let us also look at some of the largest cross-border M and A deals as of August 2026 end. The largest cross-border deal, which means when an acquirer from one country acquires a target from some other country, we call that deal as a cross-border deal. The largest cross-border deal in the history happened in the year 1999. It was when the British company Vodafone AirTouch acquired a German company by the name of Mannesmann. And the value of this M&A deal was 202.8 billion dollars. The second largest cross-border deal happened in the year 2015 when Anheuser, a Belgian company, acquired SABMiller, a British company. And the value of this transaction was 101.5 billion dollars. If we look at the third largest cross-border M&A deal, it was when Dutch company Royal Dutch Petroleum acquired a British company by the name of Shell Transport and Trading in the year 2004. And the deal value was 74.6 billion dollars. Now let us look at the leading global M&A investment banks by the amount of M&A fees they earned in the year 2025. So the largest M&A investment bank by M&A fees is Goldman Sachs. And if you look at the amount of M&A fees Goldman Sachs earned in 2025, it was 4,000 million dollars. If we look at the second largest investment bank by M and A fees, it happens to be JP Morgan. And the value of fee it earned in year 2025 was 3,200 and two million dollars. The third largest M and A advisor in the year 2025 happens to be Morgan Stanley and the amount of fees it earned was 3,000 80 million dollars. >> [music]",
