      "created_at": "2026-11-15 08:07:18",
      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now, let us talk about how does synergy get shared between the merging firms. You understand that in an M and A transaction, what typically happens is that an acquirer has to pay more than what the target is worth. Why? Because the reason is simple. If the acquiring firm is merely paying to the target shareholders, the fair value of the target shares, the target shareholders will not surrender their shares. They can sell off their shares in the secondary market anyway at the fair price. So therefore, in an M and A transaction, an acquirer has to pay more than the true price of the target shares. And this is what we call as the premium. So what is premium? Premium is the price over and above the fair value of the target which the acquirer pays to the target shareholders. Now the question is what is portion of synergy that acquirer retains with itself? It is equal to synergy minus the premium which the acquirer is paying to the target. And similarly, therefore, what is portion of the synergy that target is retaining? The target is retaining premium as its portion of synergy. Now clearly, as you can figure out, what will happen if synergy is less than the premium? If synergy is less the premium, it clearly tells us that the acquirer is going to lose. Let us take one example. So if the target stock is selling for rupees 100 and let us say that the acquirer pays rupees 120 to target. What is the takeover premium here? The takeover premium here is 120 - 100 which is 20. And in percentage terms, it is going to be 20% of the original stock price of the target. And therefore, what is gain to the target per share? The gain to the target per share is rupees 20. Now let us assume that the synergy from this merger is rupees 30. And therefore, can you tell me what portion of the synergy is the acquirer going to retain with itself? It is going to retain rupees 10 which is 30 - 20 with itself. What is the portion of the synergy which is going to the target? The portion of the synergy which is going to the target is rupees 20. Clearly, if the synergy were to be less than rupees 20 which is our premium, in that case the acquirer is surely going to lose. Now let me ask you one question. My question is what happens to the target firm stock price upon announcement of a takeover? What do you think? Will the stock price rise? Will the stock price fall? Or will the stock price remain unaffected? In practice, the target firm stock price increases as soon as the takeover bid on the target is announced. Can you tell me as to why does this happen? Why does the target firm stock price go up upon announcement of a takeover? The reason is very very simple because when an acquirer acquires a target the acquiring firm is going to pay a substantial takeover premium over and above the true value of the target to get the deal done. And therefore target shareholders upon completion of the deal are going to receive the offer price which is more than the pre-announcement stock price of the target. And therefore as soon as acquisition of this target is announced the stock price of the target jumps up. Now let me ask you another question. Does the stock price go up as much as the offer price upon announcement of a takeover? The answer is no. While the target stock price goes up it does not go up to the offer price. The target stock price is usually below the offer price. Now you must be wondering why is the target stock price below the offer price? Why doesn't the target stock price go up as much as the offer price upon announcement of a takeover? The reason is simple because there is some probability that the deal may not go through. And what will happen if the deal does not go through? If the If the does not go through, target shareholders are not going to receive the offer price. And therefore, the stock price of the target upon withdrawal of a takeover deal decreases. So therefore, the higher the probability of deal completion, the closer the stock price to the offer price. Okay, now let me ask you another question which is related to M and A's implication for shareholder wealth. So the question is do M and A's create value for shareholders? If we talk about the target firm shareholders, they are clear winners in M and D deals. Why? Because acquiring firm often has to pay a premium to acquire the target. And therefore, target firm shareholders are going to benefit as a consequence of the M and D deal. However, the evidence on the acquiring firm shareholders is far less clear. On average, acquiring firm shareholders do not benefit from M and D deals. But if we look at the shareholders of the combined firms, then also we can say that M and A's create shareholder value on behalf of shareholders of the combined firms. However, for shareholders of the acquiring firm on a standalone basis, M and A's on average do not appear to create value. >> [music]",
