      "transcript_text": "[music] >> So far we have talked about, synergy as the rational motive for M and A's. But you understand that M and A's happen for a variety of other reasons as well. Let us talk about few such reasons which may not be always rational. So those reasons include to begin with managerial overconfidence. Are overconfident managers more likely to undertake M and A's? The answer is yes. Does managerial overconfidence affect shareholder value through M and A's? Again the answer is yes. Overconfident managers are overly optimistic about their ability to create value. They end up overestimating synergies and therefore they end up overpaying in M and A deals. This is what we call as hubris hypothesis. Overconfidence is also called as hubris. And therefore the name of this hypothesis is hubris hypothesis. Now the question is how do we measure managerial overconfidence? Managerial overconfidence is very difficult to measure. Prior research has employed some proxies to see how overconfident a manager is. So let us talk about those proxies one by one. The first proxy managerial overconfidence is high takeover premium. Those acquirers who end up paying high takeover premium, their managers are possibly overconfident. Now the question is how do we measure takeover premium? Takeover premium is offer price minus pre-offer value of the target. This whole divided by pre-offer value of the target. The second proxy for managerial overconfidence is managers holding in the money stock options until the year before the expiration date. I understand that most of you may not understand what stock options are. So here we are talking about employee stock options. Employee stock options are essentially call options. What are call options? A call option gives the owner a right but not the obligation to purchase the shares of the firm at a predetermined price called as the strike price or the exercise price on or before a certain date called as the expiration date. Now if the strike price or exercise price is below the market price, in that case we will call this option as in the money option. Why? Because you can buy the shares of the firm at less than the market price and then you can sell them off at the market price thereby making profit. So in other words, if have in the money stock option, it makes sense for you to exercise that option. However, managers who hold in the money stock options until the year before the expiration date, these managers are probably overconfident about their ability to increase the stock price of the firm. And that is a reason as to why they are holding on to even in the money stock options. So therefore, holding on to in the money stock options until the year before the expiration date is another proxy for managerial overconfidence. Now the third proxy for managerial overconfidence is media portrayal. So managers whom the media seem to portray as overconfident in the press is our another proxy for managerial overconfidence. Now the last proxy for managerial overconfidence is managers with high acquisitiveness. Those managers who make a greater number of acquisitions over a relatively short span of time. Typically literature has looked at those managers which make five or more acquisitions within three years as overconfident managers. Now what evidence does the literature provide on managerial overconfidence? The literature says managers affected by hubris, managers who are overconfident are more likely to destroy the shareholder value. >> [music]",
      "key_moments_json": "{\"total_video_duration_seconds\": 364.9, \"high_yield_duration_seconds\": 114.0, \"fluff_saved_percentage\": 69, \"key_moments\": [{\"start_seconds\": 3.0, \"end_seconds\": 22.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:03 - 00:22\", \"title\": \"Managerial Overconfidence as M&A Motive\", \"yield_score\": 9, \"why_essential\": \"Establishes that overconfident managers are more likely to undertake M&A and destroy shareholder value, framing overconfidence as a key non-rational motive.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Managerial overconfidence drives M&A activity even when it is not value-accretive, as overconfident managers overestimate their ability to create synergies.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 22.0, \"end_seconds\": 41.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:22 - 00:41\", \"title\": \"Hubris Hypothesis and Value Destruction\", \"yield_score\": 8, \"why_essential\": \"Introduces the hubris hypothesis, linking managerial overconfidence to the overestimation of synergies and subsequent overpayment in deals.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The hubris hypothesis posits that overconfidence leads managers to overpay for targets, ultimately destroying shareholder value.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 41.0, \"end_seconds\": 60.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:41 - 01:00\", \"title\": \"Measuring Overconfidence: Takeover Premium\", \"yield_score\": 7, \"why_essential\": \"Defines takeover premium as (offer price - pre-offer value) / pre-offer value and identifies high premiums as a proxy for managerial overconfidence.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"A high takeover premium signals that the acquiring manager may be overconfident, as they are paying above the target's intrinsic value.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 60.0, \"end_seconds\": 79.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:00 - 01:19\", \"title\": \"Measuring Overconfidence: In-the-Money Stock Options\", \"yield_score\": 7, \"why_essential\": \"Explains that managers holding in-the-money stock options until one year before expiration are likely overconfident about their ability to boost firm stock price.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Holding in-the-money options past the optimal exercise point is a behavioral signal of managerial overconfidence regarding future stock price appreciation.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 79.0, \"end_seconds\": 98.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:19 - 01:38\", \"title\": \"Measuring Overconfidence: Media Portrayal\", \"yield_score\": 5, \"why_essential\": \"Identifies media portrayal of managers as overconfident as a proxy, though this is a softer, less quantifiable measure.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Media narratives can serve as a proxy for overconfidence, but rely on subjective interpretation rather than financial data.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 98.0, \"end_seconds\": 117.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:38 - 01:57\", \"title\": \"Measuring Overconfidence: Acquisitiveness\", \"yield_score\": 6, \"why_essential\": \"Defines managers making five or more acquisitions within three years as overconfident, linking frequent deal-making to overconfidence.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"High acquisitiveness, measured by a high frequency of deals in a short period, is used as a behavioral proxy for overconfident management.\"}]}",
