      "transcript_text": "In addition to agency motive, and managerial overconfidence, M and A's can also be undertaken so as to increase the EPS of the combined firm. EPS, as you know, stands for earnings per share. To understand that, let us first of all talk about PE ratio. What is PE ratio? You understand that PE ratio is stock price divided by earnings per share. What is the intuitive meaning of PE ratio? It tells us that as an investor, how much are you willing to pay for one rupee of earnings of the firm? Now the question is, why are investors willing to pay different prices for one rupee of earnings of different firms? In other words, why do different firms trade at different PE ratios? The reason is that the growth in earnings of those firms which are trading at higher PE multiples could be higher than the growth in earnings of firms which are trading at lower PE multiples. In other words, investors expect higher growth in earnings of firms with higher PE ratios. Now, can the EPS of the merged firm exceed the pre-merger EPS of the either firm even if the deal creates no economic value. This might look like a difficult question. But the answer to this question is yes. The EPS of the combined firm after the M and A deal can be higher than the EPS of both the acquirer and the target even if the deal does not create any economic value. Even if the deal does not have any synergy. It can happen when an acquirer acquires another firm with a lower P ratio. Such acquisitions in which the EPS of the acquiring firm goes up after the M and A deal are termed EPS accretive deals. On the other hand, acquisitions that bring down the EPS of the acquiring firm after the M and A deal are called as EPS dilutive deals. Let us try to understand this with the help of an example. There are two all equity financed firms. Firm A and firm T. Each having earnings of rupees 80 per share. Firm A has 100 shares outstanding priced at rupees 1000 per share. Firm T also has 100 shares outstanding but its stock price is rupees 600 per share. Firm A acquires firm T using its own stock. You have to answer the following questions. Assuming that the acquisition adds no value, which means that there are no synergies, firm T receives no premium and there are no transaction and other costs related to the acquisition. Let us try to jot down this information. So we have two firms. We have firm A and we have firm T. Okay. Now what is EPS of firm A? If you look at earnings per share of firm A, it is given to us as rupees 80 per share. What is EPS of firm T? EPS of firm T again is given to us as rupees 80 per share. Okay. How many shares are outstanding of each firm? Let us look at number of shares which are outstanding for firm A. These are 100 for firm A as well as for firm T. Okay. Now what is the share price? The share price which is given to us is rupees 1000 for firm A and rupees 600 for firm T. Now the first part says what are the P ratios of firm A and firm T before the acquisition? We know the price per share and we know the earnings per share for each of the two firms and therefore we can easily find out the price to earnings ratio for each of the two firms. Let us try to find it out. The P ratio for firm A is essentially going to be 1000 divided by 80 which gives us 12.5 If we look at firm T the P ratio is going to be 600 divided by EPS which is 80 this is going to be 7.5. So as you can see the P ratio of firm A is 12.5 and the P ratio of firm T is 7.5. So firm A has higher P ratio than firm T. So that was our first part. Now the second part asks us in a perfect market what is the value of firm A after the acquisition? So we have to find out the market value of firm A after deal. Market value of firm A after the acquisition, which means that we have to find out market value of the combined firm after the acquisition. So obviously since there are no synergies and since no premium is being paid out, therefore market value of firm A after the acquisition is simply going to be the sum of market values of firms A and T before the acquisition. So it is going to equal market value which I'm going to denote by MV. Market value of firm A before the acquisition plus market value of firm T before the acquisition. Now what is market value of firm A before the acquisition? Firm A as well as firm T are equity finance. They do not have any debt in their capital structure. Therefore market value of firm A before the acquisition will be equal to market value of equity of firm A before the acquisition, which is equal to number of shares outstanding of firm A before the acquisition multiplied by price per share which is rupees 1000 per share. Similarly, market value of firm T before the acquisition will be equal to market value of equity of firm T before the acquisition, which in turn equals to number of shares outstanding, which is again 100 multiplied by price per share before the acquisition, which is 600. So this is going to be 1 lakh plus 60,000. So therefore, market value of equity of firm A after the acquisition is going to be 1 lakh 60,000 rupees. Again, since the firm does not have any debt in its capital structure, so market value is going to be same as market value of equity.",
      "key_moments_json": "{\"total_video_duration_seconds\": 696.7, \"high_yield_duration_seconds\": 200.0, \"fluff_saved_percentage\": 71, \"key_moments\": [{\"start_seconds\": 107.0, \"end_seconds\": 167.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:47 - 02:47\", \"title\": \"EPS accretive vs dilutive deals\", \"yield_score\": 9, \"why_essential\": \"Defines the core concept that EPS can increase post-merger even without economic value creation, simply due to P ratio differences.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"EPS accretive deals occur when the acquirer has a higher P ratio than the target, increasing combined EPS even with no synergies.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 219.0, \"end_seconds\": 299.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"03:39 - 04:59\", \"title\": \"Merger example with P ratio differential\", \"yield_score\": 8, \"why_essential\": \"Provides a concrete numerical example demonstrating how EPS accretion occurs when a high P ratio firm acquires a low P ratio firm.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Using the example: Firm A (P=12.5) acquires Firm T (P=7.5); combined EPS rises to 85.71 from 80, showing accretion without value creation.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 355.0, \"end_seconds\": 415.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"05:55 - 06:55\", \"title\": \"Post-acquisition firm value calculation\", \"yield_score\": 7, \"why_essential\": \"Explains the mechanics of calculating the combined firm's market value in a no-synergy, no-premium scenario.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Market value of combined firm = sum of individual pre-acquisition market values (100,000 + 60,000 = 160,000 rupees).\"}]}",
