      "transcript_text": "Now, let us come to part C. It says how many shares must firm A offer to firm T's shareholders in exchange for their shares? So since firm T is not receiving any premium, so it tells us that firm T's shareholders are going to receive firm A's shares with a market value same as the market value prior to the acquisition. So therefore, let us assume that let let number of new shares let number of new shares issued by firm A to firm T is n. So therefore, we can say that number of new shares multiplied by price per share after the M&A deal is going to equal market value of firm T before the acquisition. >> Now we know that number of new shares being issued Price per share is going to equal total market value of firm A after the acquisition which is nothing but 1 lakh 60,000 rupees divided by total number of shares outstanding of the combined firm after the acquisition. There were 100 shares outstanding before the acquisition. Now firm A has issued N more shares and therefore total number of shares outstanding of the combined firm after the acquisition is going to be 100 + N. This is going to equal market value of firm T before the acquisition which we have already computed as 60,000 in the previous step. Let us try to solve this. So this implies 1 lakh 60,000 N equals 60 lakh plus 60 thousand N. So this implies 1 lakh N equals 60 lakh. So this implies that N equals 60. So therefore how many shares must firm A offer to firm T shareholders in exchange for their shares? This number is 60. Now let us come to part D. This says what is firm A's EPS after the acquisition? That is the combined firm's EPS. To find out the combined firm's EPS, we have to first of all find combined firm's earnings. What is going to be total earnings of firm A after the acquisition? Total earnings of firm A after the acquisition will be equal to earnings of firm A before the acquisition, which was EPS of 80 into number of shares outstanding which was 100. And again plus EPS of firm T which is again 80 multiplied by number of shares outstanding before the acquisition. So therefore it is going to be 8,000 plus 8,000. So therefore total earnings of the combined firm are going to be rupees 16,000. Now therefore let us try to find out firm's A's EPS after the acquisition. Firm A's EPS after the acquisition is going to be total earnings of firm A after the acquisition which are 16,000 rupees divided by total number of shares outstanding of firm A after the acquisition which is 100 + 60 which is 160. And therefore our earnings per share comes out to be rupees 100 rupees 100 per share. So what did you notice here? You notice that the EPS of firm A and T before the acquisition was rupees 80 per share but after the acquisition EPS of the combined firm has increased to rupees 100 per share. Now let us look at the last part. It says what is firm's A's PE ratio after the acquisition which means the combined firm's PE ratio. Can we not find out the combined firm's PE ratio? To find that out we need two things. We need to know the price per share of the combined firm and the earnings per share of the combined firm. Earnings per share of the combined firm we have already computed which is rupees 100 per share in part D. If we compute price per share of the combined firm we can easily find out our answer. So therefore let us try to find out first of all >> Price per share of firm A after the acquisition. It is going to be as we computed in part C it is 1 lakh 60,000 divided by 100 plus n and we computed the value of n as 60. So therefore we will divide it by 160. What does that give us? That gives us price per share of rupees thousand. From here can we not compute the PE ratio? The PE ratio of firm A after the acquisition is going to be price per share which is thousand rupees divided by earnings per share which is 100 rupees. So the PE ratio is going to be simply 10. So what did you notice here? You notice that firm A's PE ratio before the acquisition was 12.5 and its earnings per share was rupees 80 per share. However after the acquisition while the earnings per share of the combined firm has gone up to rupees 100 per share, however, the PE ratio of the combined firm has come down from 12.5 to 10. So therefore, it tells us that increase in EPS is coming at a cost. It tells us that the future earnings of the combined firm are going to grow at a slower rate. Therefore, EPS increase is not necessarily a rational motive for mergers.",
      "key_moments_json": "{\"total_video_duration_seconds\": 651.1, \"high_yield_duration_seconds\": 97.0, \"fluff_saved_percentage\": 80, \"key_moments\": [{\"start_seconds\": 7.0, \"end_seconds\": 43.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:07 - 00:43\", \"title\": \"EPS calculation post-merger\", \"yield_score\": 9, \"why_essential\": \"Derives the exact number of new shares (N=60) firm A must issue to firm T shareholders based on market value equivalence, showing the mechanical process of share exchange in an accretive EPS merger.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The number of new shares issued is determined by equating the post-deal share price value to the target's pre-deal market value, resulting in 60 new shares issued by firm A.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 43.0, \"end_seconds\": 70.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:43 - 01:10\", \"title\": \"Combined firm EPS computation\", \"yield_score\": 9, \"why_essential\": \"Calculates the combined firm's total earnings (16,000) and EPS (100) by summing pre-acquisition earnings of both firms and dividing by the new total share count (160), demonstrating the mechanical EPS increase.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The combined firm's EPS increases from 80 to 100 rupees per share after the acquisition, calculated as total earnings of 16,000 divided by 160 total shares outstanding.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 70.0, \"end_seconds\": 104.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:10 - 01:44\", \"title\": \"PE ratio compression post-merger\", \"yield_score\": 10, \"why_essential\": \"Reveals the critical trade-off: while EPS increases from 80 to 100, the PE ratio compresses from 12.5 to 10, proving that EPS accretion comes at the cost of lower future earnings growth multiple.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The increase in EPS is not a free lunch; the PE ratio drops from 12.5 to 10, indicating that future earnings of the combined firm will grow at a slower rate, making EPS increase an insufficient rational motive for mergers on its own.\"}]}",
