      "transcript_text": "So in the first two modules you learned how to read a business visualization, how to judge whether it works and how to plan a dashboard around what its users need. Now it's time to build one. So we are going to be using Power BI which is Microsoft's business intelligence tool. Power BI desktop is free and it runs on Windows. There's also an online service which is available on cloud for sharing the reports and dashboards which we'll be using in the next module. Note that Power BI desktop has new releases every month. So if yours is newer, a few menus may look slightly different, but everything we do should still work the same way. A quick word on two terms. In Power BI a report is one or more pages of interactive visuals and that's what we are going to build. In Power BI's own language a dashboard is always a single screen made by pinning visuals from the reports in the online cloud service. Now we'll see this in the next module as well. So for now I will be referring to dashboard or report as we kind of work through. But think about it this way that what we normally call multi-screen dashboards are called reports in Power BI's language. In this module we'll load and clean the data, we'll connect the tables, we'll also create some calculations. Then we're going to build seven main chart types ranging from tables to line charts to KPI cards and so on. We'll assemble these together our first report page. As we do this, we'll also learn how to format each of the visuals and the page itself. Please download the files from the LMS before you start and build along with me. You can pause the video whenever you need to to keep pace. Throughout this module and the next, we'll work with one company data set called Superstore. Now this is a fictional US retailer that sells office supplies, furniture, and technology to three customer segments. The segments are consumers, businesses, and home offices. Now this data comes to us in four separate files, and that's quite realistic because in most companies orders sit in one system, customer details are in another, product information may be in a third file, each looked after by a different team. So bringing them together is also part of an analyst job. So that's what we are going to do today. Let's first look at what is there in each of these four files. So we have four years of data from 2015 to 2018. The order file, when we look at it, you will see that there's one row for each product in an order. We've also got, of course, the order date, the customer ID, the product ID. And then we have the numbers, which is what is the net sales, quantity bought, profit, and the discount amount. Note that here if you see in the file look at the order ID for the first few rows, the order ID repeats. That's because we have one row per product which has been bought in the order. So that's why we have multiple rows for the same order. Now let's look at the next file which is the customer lookup file. In this we see there is a customer name, segment, the city, state and region. Now we have the three segments here as I mentioned and those are consumer, corporate and home office. The third file is the product lookup file. Here we see that we have each row containing a product ID and for each product we also know which category it belongs to, whether it's technology, office supplies or furniture. We also know a sub category and we [clears throat] know the product name as well. The last file is the shipping file where for each order we know when was it shipped and how was it shipped. So these are the four files that we have from 2015 to 2018 and that's about 9000 plus order lines and sales totally comes to about $2.2 million and the question we're going to keep coming back to is quite a simple one. How is this business performing? Where is it making money? Where is losing money? So let's get started.",
      "key_moments_json": "{\"total_video_duration_seconds\": 325.4, \"high_yield_duration_seconds\": 114.0, \"fluff_saved_percentage\": 65, \"key_moments\": [{\"start_seconds\": 3.0, \"end_seconds\": 22.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:03 - 00:22\", \"title\": \"Power BI Desktop vs Service Roles\", \"yield_score\": 8, \"why_essential\": \"Defines the critical distinction between a 'report' (interactive multi-page visuals) and a 'dashboard' (single-screen pinned visuals from the cloud service), preventing terminology confusion throughout the course.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"In Power BI's terminology, what are commonly called multi-screen dashboards are referred to as 'reports,' while 'dashboards' are single-screen canvases built by pinning visuals from reports in the online service.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 27.0, \"end_seconds\": 52.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:27 - 00:52\", \"title\": \"Data Loading and Connection Strategy\", \"yield_score\": 9, \"why_essential\": \"Introduces the realistic multi-file data integration challenge, explaining that business data typically resides in separate systems (orders, customers, products, shipping) and must be brought together to answer performance questions.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"Business data is typically fragmented across four separate files (order details, customer lookup, product lookup, and shipping data) that must be connected and integrated to answer business performance questions.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 57.0, \"end_seconds\": 92.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"00:57 - 01:32\", \"title\": \"Superstore Dataset Structure\", \"yield_score\": 7, \"why_essential\": \"Provides the definitive schema of the analysis dataset: 4 years (2015-2018) of fictional US retail data across three segments (consumer, corporate, home office) with ~9000+ order lines and $2.2M in total sales.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The dataset covers 4 years (2015-2018) of a fictional US retailer's sales across three customer segments (consumer, corporate, home office), totaling approximately 9,000 order lines and $2.2 million in sales.\"}, {\"start_seconds\": 97.0, \"end_seconds\": 132.0, \"timestamp_display\": \"01:37 - 02:12\", \"title\": \"Core Business Question Framework\", \"yield_score\": 10, \"why_essential\": \"Establishes the enduring analytical framework that will structure every visualization and analysis throughout the module: understanding where the business is making and losing money, providing a constant decision-making north star.\", \"key_takeaway\": \"The central analytical question guiding the entire module is how the business is performing, where it is making money, and where it is losing money, serving as a constant north star for all subsequent visualizations.\"}]}",
