      "notes_markdown": "# Theme of the Dashboard\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Setting a Power BI theme at the start standardizes colors, text, and visual properties across every chart, saving significant time and ensuring brand consistency throughout the report.\n### Theme Application And Customization\n- **Theme application:** Accessible via **View** tab → **Current theme** → **Customized current theme**; edits are possible at any later stage.\n- **Base theme naming:** Users assign a name (e.g., \"superstore course theme\") and save the base selection to lock in the foundation.\n- **Color palette:** \"Accessible default\" adapts colors for colorblind readability; the first color in the palette becomes the default for most charts (e.g., blue for column/line charts).\n- **Sentiment colors:** Customizable per user preference (e.g., green for positive, dark red for negative, amber for neutral).\n- **Divergent colors:** Used for gradient formatting (e.g., green for max, amber for middle, red for min values in conditional formatting).\n- **Text sizing:** General text set to ~16pt; title text adjusted to ~18pt for visual hierarchy.\n- **Visual properties:** Default white chart background with light gray border (kept subtle); rounded corners enabled (adjustable radius, e.g., 10px); shadow enabled for depth perception.\n- **Page/canvas settings:** Standard default resolution of 1920×800px suitable for large monitors; canvas background set to light gray with increased transparency to make white visuals pop; wallpaper left at default.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n*No formulas or numerical calculations were taught in this lecture; this section is omitted per synthesis rules.*\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming theme colors automatically apply to axis labels and individual visual titles. → **Reality:** Theme settings control general text and palette, but axis labels and visual titles often require separate formatting on each chart.\n2. **Trap:** Believing the default 1920×800 canvas size is optimal for all display environments. → **Reality:** While suitable for large monitors, the canvas size should be adjusted based on the target display medium to ensure detail retention.\n3. **Trap:** Thinking once a theme is saved, it cannot be modified. → **Reality:** The theme is fully editable at any point; users can return to **View > Current theme** to adjust colors, text, or visual properties as needed.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"Scenario: A student selects the \\\"accessible default\\\" color palette in Power BI theme settings and applies it to a new report. Later, the student creates a column chart. What color will the column chart default to, and why?\", \"options\": [\"A) Blue, because blue is the default color for column charts in Power BI\", \"B) The first color in the accessible default palette, which is blue, as the first palette color becomes the default for most charts\", \"C) Green, because accessible palettes prioritize green for data representation\", \"D) Red, because the sentiment colors are automatically applied to all new charts\"], \"explanation\": \"\", \"key_concept\": \"\"}]",
