      "transcript_text": "Before we build our first visual uh we need to decide how our report is going to look like. Now formatting is the most timeconuming part of building a report in PowerBI. So anything that we are able to set up uh right in the beginning saves us time on every visual we build later. Now, PowerBI does this through a theme. So, a theme is really one set of colors, text, and visual settings that every chart that we use in PowerBI will follow. What we're going to do is to start from PowerBI's current default theme and we will customize it a bit and save it. And then this is what gets applied on anything that we do from this point on. So to get to the theme go to view in the topnav and uh there are these themes which are there. So if you see here the um I'm going to yeah keep the current theme as default. You should see right here which is current theme is default. Go down and here you have customized current theme. We'll click that. And then this theme page opens. Note that this theme can be edited at any point uh later as well. So currently we just setting it up first. But if we do find something is not comfortable later, we can come back and edit this as well. So go to theme settings. So you can see here that there is a base theme and uh we will now enter a name for superstore course theme. Save base theme selection. Let's uh keep this on and close this. Then we go to the colors. Now you can see a range of color patterns which are available. So each of these color palette has been picked for specific uh uses and reasons. Wherever you see accessible, it means that it has been adapted so that even slightly colorblind people can still read most of the colors. We are going to use accessible default. Note that any color palette that we apply, the very first color becomes the default color for most of the charts. For example, when I use this accessible default, you will note that column charts will be blue in color. The line chart is again going to be blue in color and so on. So let's pick accessible default and close um yeah before closing let's also change uh the sentiment colors here. So you can see sentiment is here showing orange. Let's make it red. So you can see this is my color palette. I don't really have a deep red here. So if I want to access that, I can always go to this more colors and let's go to the extreme red. So that I want a dark red here. So let's go back and positive. We want it green. So, I'm just going to use the color palette green for now. If need be, I'll change it later. And for neutral, I'll just use this amber color. Let's close this sentiment formatting. Divergent colors. These are whenever in case I want to show it as a gradient. For example, if you remember in Excel, we do conditional formatting of the cell color in a gradient. So there then how should it kind of range is what is defined here. So maximum values we want to make it green. The middle values let's say we'll make amber and minimum values again we'll take red. So I have this red available now. So I'm simply going to use this. The next thing now that we are finished with color palette let's take uh the text. Now text we will change this general text to about 16 points. So this is the text size which is going to be used for for example table values and so on. Right? So I'm keeping it as 16. If we find it too big later, we can always come back and change it. So let's close this. Let's look at title. The title is at about 12 points. Now I'm going to change it to 18. and close this. Then let's go to the visual properties. So visual properties define what the chart itself looks like. Currently the chart background is white. I will keep it as white. Now border is on. Let's see what sort of borders. So there is a light gray color border which is good because we don't want the border to dominate the visual. Now the rounded corner. So, this basically gives a border with a slight rounded edges. And we'll leave that on. And I'm just increasing maybe just a little bit of rounding to 10 pixels. We'll also uh close this. Let's keep a shadow also on. This is nice because each visual then you know there's a slight shadow so that it looks slightly off the page which looks nice. So, I'm just going to keep that. Again these are individual design choices and that they can be changed as per what the user needs or what you think is the best. So let me go back and close background and we now go to the page. So the page is what each of the tabs is called here you can see. So within the page there is canvas setting. Now you see by default this is 1920x,80. Now, this is a good setting to work in because if you do project these kind of reports and dashboards, even on large monitors, it'll still have enough detail. So, we'll leave this as uh standardized and default. Let's go to canvas background. Now, here's where your uh the page the background itself changes. So, I'm going to make it slightly gray. I'll take the lightest gray and I'll increase the transparency some more so that it becomes really light. And then our visual background is white. So the white will stand out against the gray. So this color most companies will vary according to the color palette for their brand. So here is where all the changes can be done in terms of the colors that you want to see. We will leave the wallpaper on. So wallpaper you can see is really area behind the canvas. So in case their screen is large and this whole page is not going to show fully then this area surrounding that is what is uh determined by the wallpaper we'll just leave it as default for now. So that's your uh theme settings. So let's also go and save this themes because in case we need this theme later to apply to other reports etc we can have the backup. So this is download current theme and I'm going to call this superstore course theme. [snorts] You will note that this is a JSON file. Uh so this basically has the details. Whatever we have set up here is available here. We can uh load this and when we browse the theme then this is going to be available. Save. Now one thing I do want to tell you about the theme. The fonts and the colors that we have chosen typically reach every visual. Right? And the text size like I said the general size etc reaches tables but often you will find the chart title size or axis labels for example x-axis y-axis labels or the titles for the axis may not follow these sizes. So those are uh pieces that we can format on each visual uh individually. Our theme is set. So this is what is going to apply from now on.",
