      "youtube_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kgbR-P99C80",
      "video_id": "kgbR-P99C80",
      "duration_seconds": 963.101,
      "status": "completed",
      "notes_markdown": "# Donut Charts and Interactions in Power BI\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Donut charts in Power BI effectively show proportional sales contributions across a small number of categories (e.g., 4 regions, 3 segments), with the center displaying the total value; interactions between visuals can be controlled via the \"Edit interactions\" feature to filter, highlight, or isolate specific selections across the report page.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\nNo formulas or numerical calculations were taught in this lecture.\n### Donut Chart Configurations and Interaction Modes\n- **Donut vs. Pie:** Donut charts preferred over pie charts because they display the total value in the center (e.g., 2.2 million) while showing regional or segment slices.\n- **Label Configuration:** Turn off the legend to save space; enable \"all detailed labels\" to show region, absolute sales numbers, and percentage contribution directly on slices.\n- **Interaction Modes (Edit Interactions):** Three default options control how selections propagate across visuals:\n  - **Filter:** Selecting a slice (e.g., West region) applies a filter, updating all other visuals to show only that category's data.\n  - **Highlight:** Default mode; dims all other data and highlights only the segments contributing to the selected slice (e.g., shows how segments break down within the West region's 33.49% share).\n  - **None:** Selection has no impact on the other visual.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Assuming a donut chart can effectively display many categories (e.g., >10 slices) with clear comparison. → **Reality:** Donut charts are only effective with a handful of slices; too many categories make labels cluttered and comparisons difficult, use bar charts for many categories.\n2. **Trap:** Believing the legend is the best way to identify slice colors. → **Reality:** Turning off the legend and using \"all detailed labels\" with region/number/percentage directly on the slices improves user clarity and saves space.\n3. **Trap:** Thinking that selecting a slice in one visual always \"filters\" the entire report. → **Reality:** The default interaction is \"highlight,\" which only dims unrelated data and shows the breakdown within the selected category; \"filter\" mode must be explicitly chosen to slice the entire dataset.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"A professor builds a donut chart showing sales by region (West, East, North, South) with total net sales of 2.2 million displayed in the center. A student is asked: \\\"What unique quantitative insight does the donut chart provide that a pie chart of the same data does not?\\\" Which of the following is the correct answer?\", \"options\": [\"A) The donut chart shows the exact percentage contribution of each region to total sales\", \"B) The donut chart displays the absolute total sales value (2.2 million) in the central region\", \"C) The donut chart allows for more than 6 slices to be displayed clearly\", \"D) The donut chart uses a different color palette than the pie chart\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The defining feature of a donut chart versus a pie chart is the central void, which can display the aggregate total value (here, 2.2 million). Both chart types show percentage contribution via the slices, so A is a shared feature, not unique. C is incorrect because the lecture explicitly states donut charts work well only with a \\\"handful of slices\\\" (4 regions in this case), and increasing slice count reduces readability. D is incorrect as color palettes are typically determined by the software theme, not chart type.\", \"key_concept\": \"Donut vs. Pie Chart Unique Encoding\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"While viewing a donut chart for sales by region, a user hovers over the \\\"West\\\" slice. A tooltip appears showing \\\"West total net sales is 736 K and 33.549 percent.\\\" The professor then reduces the tooltip size from the default 16 to 10 in the theme settings. What is the primary reason for this adjustment?\", \"options\": [\"A) To increase the number of characters that can fit in the tooltip text\", \"B) To make the tooltip appear larger and more prominent on the screen\", \"C) To improve readability of the tooltip contents for the user\", \"D) To reduce the file size of the Power BI report\"], \"correct_index\": 2, \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is C. The professor explicitly stated that the default tooltip text was \\\"way too large\\\" and that reducing the size to 10 made it \\\"quite readable.\\\" The adjustment is a design choice aimed at user comprehension, not at increasing character capacity (A), making the tooltip more prominent (B - the goal is readability, not prominence), or affecting file size (D - tooltip font size has negligible impact on report file size).\", \"key_concept\": \"Tooltip Customization for Readability\"}, {\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"A donut chart is configured with the legend turned off and \\\"all detailed labels\\\" enabled, showing region names, absolute sales numbers, and percentage contribution on each slice. If a user selects the \\\"West\\\" slice, what happens to the data displayed in a table visual on the same page, assuming the default interaction setting of \\\"highlight\\\" is active?\", \"options\": [\"A) The table filters to show only data for the West region, hiding all other regions\", \"B) The table highlights the rows corresponding to the West region's segment contributions, but still shows data for other regions dimmed or partially visible\", \"C) The table remains unchanged, showing the full 2.2 million total across all regions\", \"D) The table displays only the percentage contribution of the West region, omitting absolute values\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The lecture explains that the default interaction option is \\\"highlight.\\\" When West is selected, the table does not filter to show only West (which would be the \\\"none\\\" or \\\"filter\\\" options). Instead, it highlights the specific data related to West's contribution (showing segment-wise breakdown totaling 736.77k) while other regions remain visible but dimmed or less prominent. Option A describes the \\\"filter\\\" interaction, and Option C describes the \\\"none\\\" interaction.\", \"key_concept\": \"Default Interaction: Highlight vs. Filter\"}, {\"id\": 4, \"question\": \"A report designer copies a formatted donut chart showing sales by region and pastes it to create a second donut chart for sales by customer segment (Corporate, Consumer, Home Office). The designer forgets to update the legend/field well from \\\"region\\\" to \\\"segment.\\\" What is the most likely immediate result?\", \"options\": [\"A) The second chart will fail to render and display an error message\", \"B) The second chart will display the exact same regional sales data as the first chart, since the data source and formatting were copied\", \"C) The second chart will automatically swap the axis and show segment data while keeping the regional title\", \"D) The second chart will display no data because the segment field is not in the customer lookup table\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The professor demonstrated this exact scenario: copying a donut chart via Control+C and Control+V preserves the formatting and the data fields initially. Since the designer substituted \\\"segment\\\" for \\\"region\\\" by dragging the segment field, but in the question scenario the field was NOT changed, the chart retains the \\\"region\\\" field well. Therefore, the second chart displays the same regional data as the first. The professor later showed how to edit the title to change \\\"region\\\" to \\\"segment,\\\" but if the field well is not changed, the underlying data remains regional.\", \"key_concept\": \"Copy-Paste Format Retention and Field Well Updates\"}]",
