      "notes_markdown": "# Bar charts in Power BI\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Power BI defaults column charts to four-to-five bars before switching to horizontal layouts for readability; conditional bar coloring and subtitles effectively highlight loss-making subcategories like tables, bookcases, and supplies.\n### Bar Chart Features & Formatting\n- **Default sorting:** Column charts sort bars by the main measure (net sales) in descending order.\n- **Horizontal bar switch:** Recommended when categories exceed four-to-five bars to accommodate long labels and improve readability.\n- **Conditional formatting:** Bars turn red if profitability < 0, enabling instant identification of loss-making subcategories (tables, bookcases, supplies).\n- **Subtitle usage:** Add a subtitle (e.g., \"Red bars indicate loss-making subcategories\") in 13pt italics, centered, to explain color coding.\n- **Interactivity:** Selecting a category highlights its subcategory bars; clicking white space resets the view.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n*No formulas or numerical calculations were taught in this lecture.*\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Column charts are always the best choice for showing trends over time. → **Reality:** Power BI recommends switching to horizontal bar charts when there are more than four-to-five categories to maintain label readability.\n2. **Trap:** Conditional formatting colors bars based on the category name. → **Reality:** Formatting rules are based on measure values (e.g., profitability < 0 triggers red), not categorical labels.\n3. **Trap:** The default chart title suffices for explaining color coding. → **Reality:** A dedicated subtitle (e.g., \"Red bars indicate loss-making subcategories\") is necessary to clarify the visual logic for the audience.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"A student creates a column chart of net sales by subcategory in Power BI. The chart displays ten subcategories with long labels. Which statement best explains why the chart is difficult to read?\", \"options\": [\"A) Column charts in Power BI always sort alphabetically, causing confusion with long labels.\", \"B) Column charts default to descending order of the main measure, and with more than four to five bars, labels become angled and long labels (e.g., appliances) become hard to read.\", \"C) Power BI does not support conditional formatting on column charts, so labels overlap incorrectly.\", \"D) The chart title cannot be edited, so the subtitle overlaps with the bar labels.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct option reflects the lecture's core mechanic: column charts default to descending order of the main measure (net sales), and a boundary condition exists where beyond four to five bars, readability declines because labels angle and long category names become difficult to decipher. Option A is a distractor because Power BI sorts by measure, not alphabetically by default. Option C is incorrect as conditional formatting can be applied. Option D is a trap regarding title editing, which is not the primary readability issue discussed.\", \"key_concept\": \"Column chart readability boundary condition\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"When building a horizontal bar chart in Power BI by swapping a column chart, which of the following formatting elements is retained automatically, and which must be manually updated?\", \"options\": [\"A) Data labels, axis labels, and chart title are all retained automatically.\", \"B) Data labels and axis labels are retained; the chart title must be manually updated.\", \"C) Only the chart title is retained; data labels and axis labels reset.\", \"D) No formatting is retained; the user must start from scratch.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy\", \"explanation\": \"The lecture explicitly states that when using Control C, Control V to swap the categorical field (e.g., from category to subcategory), data labels and axis labels retain their formatting. However, the title was copied and needed to be manually changed from \\\"net sales by category\\\" to \\\"net sales by sub category\\\" via the format pane. Option A is incorrect because the title is not automatically updated. Option C and D contradict the lecture's demonstration of retained formatting.\", \"key_concept\": \"Formatting retention when swapping categorical fields\"}, {\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"A user applies conditional formatting to a bar chart in Power BI, setting the rule: if profitability is less than zero, change bar color to red. The user leaves the condition blank instead of selecting \\\"less than zero.\\\" What is the likely outcome?\", \"options\": [\"A) The bars turn red immediately because leaving the condition blank defaults to a rule that always applies red.\", \"B) The formatting rule fails to apply red to loss-making bars because leaving the condition blank defaults to \\\"minimum,\\\" which may not capture negative profitability values unless explicitly set.\", \"C) The bars turn green automatically as a system default when the condition is blank.\", \"D) Power BI prompts the user to input a specific number, and the chart becomes unresponsive until a number is entered.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The lecture explains that if the user leaves the condition blank, it replaces the condition with \\\"minimum.\\\" The rule must be explicitly set to \\\"less than zero number\\\" to change the bar to red for loss-making subcategories. Option A is a distractor; the default is minimum, not an always-apply rule. Option C is incorrect as there is no automatic green default. Option D describes a system behavior not mentioned in the lecture.\", \"key_concept\": \"Conditional formatting rule logic and boundary conditions\"}, {\"id\": 4, \"question\": \"In a Power BI bar chart demonstrating interactive selection, a user selects the bar representing \\\"Technology\\\" subcategories. Which of the following best describes the system's response?\", \"options\": [\"A) The dataset is permanently filtered to show only Technology subcategories, and the view cannot be reset.\", \"B) The bars for Technology subcategories are highlighted, and other bars are dimmed, but clicking on any white space resets the view to the original unfiltered state.\", \"C) The chart type automatically changes from a horizontal bar chart to a column chart.\", \"D) A tooltip appears showing the total net sales for Technology, but no bars are highlighted.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy\", \"explanation\": \"The lecture clearly states that selecting a bar highlights the related bars and filters the view (e.g., selecting Technology highlights tech subcategories). Crucially, the lecturer notes that clicking on any white space returns the user to the normal view. Option A is a common misconception; the interaction is temporary and resettable. Option C and D describe behaviors not observed in the lecture.\", \"key_concept\": \"Interactive selection and resetting the view\"}]",
