      "notes_markdown": "# Line Charts in Power BI\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Line charts in Power BI reveal temporal trends by plotting dates on the x-axis and values on the y-axis, with category legends enabling multi-line comparisons to isolate specific drivers of change.\n### Line Chart Configuration & Formatting\n- **X-Axis:** Date hierarchy (Year → Quarter → Month → Day) from the order date field.\n- **Y-Axis:** Total Net Sales (default measure).\n- **Format Painter:** Transfers formatting consistency across visuals on the same dashboard.\n- **Data Labels vs. Y-Axis Values:** Toggle data labels off to display Y-axis values instead.\n- **Line Interpolation:** \"Smooth\" creates a curved line; \"Linear\" connects discrete data points with straight segments.\n- **Markers:** Enable to highlight specific data points on the line.\n- **Legend:** Adding a categorical field (e.g., Category) splits the single line into multiple lines (e.g., Furniture, Office Supplies, Technology) for comparative analysis.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** Line charts can only show one data series at a time. → **Reality:** Adding a field to the Legend well splits the chart into multiple lines for comparison.\n2. **Trap:** Smooth interpolation changes the underlying data trend. → **Reality:** It only alters visual appearance; the actual data points remain unchanged.\n3. **Trap:** Markers display the exact Y-axis value for every point. → **Reality:** Markers denote data point positions; precise values are read from the Y-axis or tooltip.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"A business analyst wants to visualize the monthly revenue trend for the past two years to present to the board. Which Power BI chart type should they select, and why?\", \"options\": [\"A) Bar chart, because it compares discrete categories effectively\", \"B) Line chart, because it shows trends over a continuous time dimension\", \"C) Pie chart, because it shows the proportion of total revenue\", \"D) Scatter plot, because it shows the relationship between two variables\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The lecture established that line charts are used when we want to see how something changes over time, specifically using a date hierarchy on the x-axis. A bar chart (A) is for comparing distinct categories at a point in time. A pie chart (C) shows proportions of a whole. A scatter plot (D) explores relationships between two numerical variables, not time trends.\", \"key_concept\": \"Chart Type Selection for Time Series\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"A line chart is built with Order Date on the x-axis and Total Net Sales on the y-axis. The professor noted a slight dip between 2015 and 2016. If the analyst changes the x-axis hierarchy from \\\"Year\\\" to \\\"Month,\\\" what is the most likely effect on the visual?\", \"options\": [\"A) The dip between 2015 and 2016 will disappear completely\", \"B) The trend line will become smoother and less volatile\", \"C) The trend line may show more granular volatility within each year\", \"D] The Y-axis values will automatically resize to accommodate the new data\"], \"correct_index\": 2, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is C. The lecture explained that Power BI provides a natural hierarchy: year → quarter → month → day. Moving from \\\"Year\\\" to \\\"Month\\\" increases the granularity of the x-axis. This means the trend will be plotted at a more detailed level, likely revealing intra-year fluctuations or monthly variations that were aggregated out when viewing by year alone.\", \"key_concept\": \"Hierarchy Granularity and Trend Volatility\"}, {\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"In the lecture, the professor demonstrated the Format Painter tool to transfer formatting between visuals. If a student turns off data labels (markers) on a line chart to clean up the visual, but the Y-axis values also become invisible, what is the primary risk for a viewer trying to interpret the exact value at a specific time point?\", \"options\": [\"A) The line will disappear from the visual\", \"B) The viewer cannot determine the exact value at that specific time point without the markers or Y-axis values\", \"C) The chart will default to a bar chart format\", \"D] The legend will become unreadable\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy | Conceptual\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The lecture explicitly stated that data labels (markers) show specific data points, and the Y-axis displays values. If both are turned off or hidden, the visual loses its quantitative precision. The viewer is left looking at a shape without numerical anchors.\", \"key_concept\": \"Data Labels, Markers, and Visual Clarity\"}, {\"id\": 4, \"question\": \"A stakeholder wants to see the exact path of sales over time for three product categories (Furniture, Office Supplies, Technology). The current line chart uses \\\"Smooth\\\" interpolation. Which of the following describes the effect of switching to \\\"Linear\\\" interpolation?\", \"options\": [\"A) The lines will curve smoothly between data points, hiding the exact values\", \"B) The lines will connect data points with straight segments, making the exact path more apparent\", \"C) The markers will automatically turn off\", \"D] The Y-axis will switch to a logarithmic scale\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy | Conceptual\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The lecture demonstrated that \\\"Smooth\\\" interpolation creates a curved line between points, while \\\"Linear\\\" interpolation connects the points with straight segments. The professor specifically noted that switching to linear makes the line more jagged based on the points, which helps the user see the exact path and values at each data point.\", \"key_concept\": \"Interpolation Type: Smooth vs. Linear\"}]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
      "slides_json": "[]",
