      "status": "completed",
      "notes_markdown": "# KPI Cards in Power BI\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** KPI cards in Power BI consolidate multiple metrics into a single visual, offering tile or table layouts with customizable formatting, including gap control, accent bars, shadows, and conditional value coloring, to highlight business performance at a glance.\n### KPI Card Configuration\n- **Card visual selection:** The modern \"Card\" visual supports multiple KPIs; adding total net sales and total profit generates three KPI cards instantly.\n- **Multi-card layout:** Choose **tiles** (default) to maintain spacing between cards; switch to **table** to eliminate gaps and stack cards compactly.\n- **Arrangement:** Horizontal layout displays KPIs across the top; vertical layout stacks them, typically placed above the rest of the dashboard.\n- **Gap control:** Adjust the gap size (default 8 points) to increase or decrease spacing between cards.\n- **Formatting options:** \n  - **Background color:** Applies to the overall card container (tiles mode) or individual cards.\n  - **Accent bar:** New feature that adds a colored bar (e.g., top or left) to highlight the card.\n  - **Shadow:** Increases depth and visual prominence; adjustable size.\n  - **Call out values:** Increase size to make the number prominent; adjustable color and conditional formatting.\n- **Conditional formatting:** Apply rules (e.g., profitability > 0 → deep red) to dynamically change call-out value colors based on metrics like profitability.\n\n### Formulas & Worked Numerical\n- **Profitability calculation:** Profitability = (Total Profit / Total Net Sales) × 100.\n- **Verified example from transcript:** ($273,000 / $2,200,000) × 100 = **12.4%**.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1. **Trap:** The multi-card layout \"table\" mode stacks KPIs vertically with no spacing between them. → **Reality:** The \"table\" layout eliminates gaps and arranges cards in a compact grid, whereas \"tiles\" maintains intentional spacing.\n2. **Trap:** Conditional formatting on call-out values applies globally to all KPI cards automatically. → **Reality:** Conditional formatting must be applied per metric (e.g., profitability) or set to \"all,\" but changing settings to apply only to a specific card requires selecting that card individually; otherwise, changes apply across all cards.\n3. **Trap:** The accent bar always appears on the left side of the KPI card. → **Reality:** The accent bar position is adjustable and can be set to \"top\" or \"left\" depending on the desired dashboard layout.",
      "mcqs_json": "[{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"A business student adds total net sales and total profit to a Power BI Card visual. The interface automatically generates three KPI cards. What are the three metrics displayed, and what does the third metric represent based on the lecture?\", \"options\": [\"A) Total net sales, Total profit, and Average profitability percentage\\nB) Total net sales, Total profit, and Total assets\\nC) Total net sales, Total profit, and Customer count\\nD) Total net sales, Total profit, and Total employees\"], \"correct_index\": 0, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct option is A because the lecture explicitly stated that adding total net sales and total profit to the Card visual results in three KPI cards. The lecture concluded by stating: \\\"There is 2.2 million in terms of made sales. We have 273 K in profit and we have the average profitability of 12.4 %.\\\" Therefore, the three metrics are total net sales, total profit, and average profitability percentage. Option B (Total assets) is a distractor as it was not mentioned in the lecture. Options C and D are distractors as customer count and employee count were not discussed as default KPI outputs in this context.\", \"key_concept\": \"KPI Card Multi-Metric Output\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"When formatting KPI cards in Power BI, a user wants to apply conditional formatting to turn the profitability value red if it is below zero. However, they accidentally set the formatting to apply to \\\"all\\\" cards instead of the specific profitability card. What is the likely outcome?\", \"options\": [\"A) Only the profitability card turns red; the other cards remain unchanged.\\nB) All three KPI cards (net sales, profit, profitability) turn red if the profitability value is below zero, potentially misrepresenting the net sales and profit cards.\\nC) The formatting is rejected by the software and no changes occur.\\nD) The net sales card turns red, but the profit and profitability cards remain white.\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Moderate | Tricky\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B. The lecture demonstrated this exact trap. When the user applied conditional formatting rules to \\\"all\\\" cards, the settings propagated across all three KPI cards. The lecturer noted: \\\"Why is this? Because I had put the settings as apply to only... Let me go back. Yeah, here to all and I will increase the size. This time everything is changing, right?\\\" This illustrates that selecting \\\"all\\\" applies the rule uniformly. If profitability were below zero, the net sales and profit cards would also incorrectly turn red, confusing the business reader. The correct method is to select the specific card/metric before applying the rule.\", \"key_concept\": \"Conditional Formatting Scope\"}, {\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"A Power BI report designer is setting up KPI cards for a dashboard. They want to ensure the cards are spaced out with visible gaps between them, similar to separate tiles, rather than being compressed together. Which multi-card layout style should they select?\", \"options\": [\"A) Table\\nB) Tiles\\nC) Horizontal\\nD) Vertical\"], \"correct_index\": 1, \"difficulty\": \"Easy\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is B) Tiles. The lecture explicitly explained the difference between the two layout styles: \\\"If I put table my space between these cards go away, right? If I want the spaces, then I've got to format this as tiles.\\\" Selecting the tiles layout maintains the spacing/gaps between the KPI cards, whereas the table layout compresses them together with no space in between. Option A (Table) is the distractor for those who misremember the layout function. Options C and D refer to the arrangement orientation (stacking direction), not the spacing mechanism.\", \"key_concept\": \"Multi-Card Layout: Tiles vs. Table\"}, {\"id\": 4, \"question\": \"During a lecture on Power BI KPI cards, the professor demonstrated that increasing the \\\"gap\\\" parameter value expands the spacing between the KPI cards. If the gap is currently set to 8 points and is increased to 20 points, what is the effect on the visual layout?\", \"options\": [\"A) The KPI cards shrink in size to accommodate the extra space.\\nB) The overall dashboard page size increases automatically.\\nC) The physical distance between the KPI cards increases, creating more white space between them.\\nD) The KPI cards rearrange themselves from a vertical to a horizontal layout.\"], \"correct_index\": 2, \"difficulty\": \"Easy\", \"explanation\": \"The correct answer is C. The lecture stated: \\\"So, if I want to increase the gap, let's see what happens. Right now it's eight points and see as it as I increase it what happens the this gap keeps increasing, right? So, depending on how much gap we want etc. this can be adjusted.\\\" This indicates that the gap parameter directly controls the spacing distance between the cards. Increasing the value from 8 to 20 points simply increases the white space between the cards; it does not shrink the cards (A), change the page size (B), or alter the arrangement orientation (D).\", \"key_concept\": \"Gap Parameter Sensitivity\"}]",
