      "duration_seconds": 0.0,
      "status": "completed",
      "notes_markdown": "# Additional Charts in Power BI\n\n> **Core Takeaway:** Selecting the correct visual type is critical for communicating insights; chart choice must align with data type (categorical, numerical, time-series) and the specific business question to avoid misinterpretation.\n\n| Chart Type | Purpose | Key Configuration Steps |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bar/Column** | Comparing categorical values; ideal for many categories. | Drag category field to Axis; drag numerical field to Value. |\n| **Pie/Donut** | Showing part-to-whole relationships; effective only with few categories (< 6-7). | Drag category to Legend; drag value to Values. Limit slices for readability. |\n| **Funnel** | Visualizing sequential stages and drop-off rates (e.g., sales pipeline). | Drag sequential stage field to Stage; drag value to Value. |\n| **Filled Map** | Displaying geographic distribution of data across regions. | Drag location data (City/State) to Location; drag value to Color/Bubble size. |\n| **Combo** | Combining chart types (e.g., column and line) in one visual. | Select visual; check \"Combo chart\"; assign fields to respective value/axis wells. |\n| **Line** | Showing trends over time; comparing multiple series. | Drag date/time field to Axis; drag numerical field to Value. |\n| **Area** | Emphasizing magnitude of change over time; showing volume. | Drag date/time field to Axis; drag numerical field to Value; ensure \"Stacked\" option is set if needed. |\n### Encoding Matching & Chart Selection\n* **Encoding Matching:** Map data roles (category, value, time) to visual channels (length, angle, area) that humans perceive accurately.\n* **Category Threshold:** Pie charts become unreadable beyond 7-10 categories; bar charts are the reliable alternative for dense categorical data.\n* **Business Alignment:** The \"correct\" chart is determined by the analytical goal (comparison vs. part-to-whole vs. trend vs. geography), not default settings.\n* **Tool Limitations:** Base Power BI library covers standard needs; complex hierarchies or specific custom requirements may require drill-through features or custom visuals.\n* **Pre-attentive Attributes:** Length (bars) is perceived faster and more accurately than angle (pie slices) or area, influencing chart effectiveness.\n\n### MCQ Trap Radar\n1.  **Trap:** Defaulting to the first chart suggested by Power BI without considering the business question or data volume.\n    **Reality:** Always justify the choice; use a map or funnel only if the scenario specifically requires geographic distribution or sequential stage analysis, respectively.\n2.  **Trap:** Selecting a pie chart for datasets with many categories (e.g., 15+ product lines).\n    **Reality:** This creates visual clutter and distorts perception; switch to a bar chart where length encoding allows accurate comparison.\n3.  **Trap:** Assuming \"part-to-whole\" always means a pie chart.\n    **Reality:** A stacked bar chart often provides a more effective part-to-whole representation when comparing multiple items, as it encodes comparison via length rather than difficult angle estimation.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
