          "explanation": "The correct answer is Rs 104.51. This stock is a differential growth stock. The valuation requires two steps. Step 1: Find the present value of the growing dividends for years 1 to 6. Using the growing perpetuity formula truncated at T=6, the PV is Rs 34.28. Step 2: Find the present value of the perpetuity starting from year 7. The dividend at the end of year 6 (which becomes the perpetual cash flow) is $5 \times 1.2^5 = 12.44$ rupees. The perpetuity value at the end of year 6 is $12.44 / 0.10 = 124.43$. Discounting this back 6 years to time 0 gives $124.43 / 1.1^6 \\approx 70.23$. Total Price = $34.28 + 70.23 = 104.51$. Distractor B (Rs 34.28) arises from only calculating the first phase and ignoring the perpetuity. Distractor C (Rs 70.23) arises from only calculating the perpetuity phase and ignoring the initial growth phase. Distractor D (Rs 134.51) arises from incorrectly adding the two phase values without proper discounting logic or summing the undiscounted perpetuity value.",
