          "model_answer": "The module follows a structured logical progression beginning with the analysis of security-specific risk, which represents the risk of holding an isolated asset. The narrative then moves to portfolio risk, recognizing that combining assets can reduce unsystematic risk through diversification, leaving primarily systematic market risk. The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) is introduced as the pivotal framework that quantifies this systematic risk through the beta coefficient, translating it into a required rate of return, specifically, the cost of equity. This cost of equity is then one of the two primary components (alongside the after-tax cost of debt) used to calculate the Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC). Therefore, the CAPM serves as the bridge between the conceptual understanding of risk and the practical computation of the discount rate used in corporate valuation.",
