          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the entrepreneurial family galaxy lecture, explain why the family-centered galaxy model is a distinct way of looking at family businesses compared to the traditional firm-at-center model. Use a specific example from the lecture to support your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The entrepreneurial family galaxy model puts the family at the center, visualizing multiple vehicles, such as companies, trusts, family museums, family constitutions, and family councils, as planets that revolve around the family. This is distinct from the traditional firm-at-center model, which places a single operating company at the core. The lecture explains that families engage not only with one firm but with various vehicles for different functions: companies for wealth creation, trusts for community engagement and CSR, and family museums for legacy preservation. For example, the family may own multiple companies while also using trusts to engage with the local community and establishing a family museum to preserve their history. The galaxy model recognizes this multiplicity of vehicles and their distinct roles, whereas the firm-at-center model oversimplifies the family's engagement to a single business entity.",
