          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain why the history of family firms in the industrial era is inseparable from national political history, using specific examples from the lecture.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The history of industrial-era family firms is inseparable from national political history because their fortunes were deeply entangled with state actions and global conflicts. The Rothschild family built a pan-European banking network that financed wars and government debt across Britain, France, and Austria, directly linking family wealth to national military efforts. In the United States, the Ford family (founded 1903) and the German steel and armaments firm (founded 1811) saw their fortunes deeply entangled with the political and military history of their countries through both World Wars. Furthermore, John D. Rockefeller's Standard Oil trust (founded 1870) illustrates the concentration of economic power that triggered regulatory responses like India's MRTP Act, showing how family firm growth intersected with national competition policy. Therefore, family firm history cannot be viewed as a pure economic story; it is fundamentally shaped by the political and military context of the nations in which they operated.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding: Explains the entanglement between family firm history and national political history",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analytical application: Uses specific lecture examples (Rothschild, Ford, German steel, Standard Oil) to substantiate the argument",
              "marks": 5
            }
          ]
