          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the dynamics of the 'family of orientation' and 'family of procreation' as discussed in the lecture. How does a person simultaneously belong to both, and what are the implications for values and rule-setting in a family business context?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The family of orientation refers to the family into which a person is born, which shapes the values and cultures learned from childhood. The family of procreation refers to the family created upon marriage, where the individual and their spouse collectively decide to set the rules. A person is simultaneously part of both dimensions. This creates a dynamic where early-learned values from the family of orientation interact with actively-set rules in the family of procreation. In a family business context, this means that founding generation values (learned from their orientation) may conflict with or blend with the new rules the next generation sets in their procreation family. The lecture highlights that this dual membership is a key structural feature, as seen in the example of siblings who share the same orientation but create different procreation families upon marriage, potentially leading to different business approaches across branches.",
