          "model_answer": "The decision to internationalise in a family business is significantly shaped by board governance structures. First, if a family member serves as the Chairman of the Board, there is a strong negative correlation with internationalisation. This occurs because the Chairman is positioned to slow down outside capital, resisting dilution of family control. Second, the presence of independent directors moderates this resistance. Independent directors are less concerned about control dilution and can detail effective governance and monitoring mechanisms. When their views are given a good hearing alongside multiple family members, the family gains confidence that control can be managed, making internationalisation more likely. Third, having a cross-generational family on the board is very positively correlated with going international. Multiple generations and branches understand that expansion is necessary to sustain the larger family as it grows, and they are willing to accept some loss of control in exchange for long-term benefits. Therefore, the interplay between a resistant family Chairman, confidence-building independent directors, and the sustaining need of cross-generational family members determines whether a firm moves overseas or stays domestic.",
