          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The internationalization process for family firms involves a sequential ladder of entry modes: exporting → licensing/franchising → joint ventures → wholly owned subsidiaries. As firms move up this ladder, they trade greater resource commitment and risk for increased control and expected returns. A wholly owned subsidiary can be established either through acquisition (buying an existing firm) or greenfield (building from scratch).\n\nThe key distinction for family firms is the tendency of family CEOs to favor lower commitment modes located to the lower left of the control-risk diagram. This preference stems from the need to limit exposure to both financial costs and socio-emotional costs associated with internationalization. Unlike non-family firms that may prioritize maximum control and returns, family firms balance international growth with the preservation of socio-emotional wealth. The entry mode choice is not separate from strategy and structure but represents where the whole framework between strategy and structure becomes an observable choice. Determinants of the specific position on this ladder include resource gaps, governance mechanisms, and the risk calculus faced by the family firm.\n\nModel Answer Marking Rubric:\n- Core process description (ladder of entry modes, control-risk trade-off): 3 marks\n- Distinction between acquisition and greenfield: 2 marks\n- Family CEO tendency and socio-emotional cost consideration: 3 marks\n- Integration of entry mode with strategy-structure framework: 2 marks",
