          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain why family firms are distinct from ordinary firms as stakeholder managers, focusing on the three characteristics mentioned in the lecture. Use a real-world example from the lecture to illustrate your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "Family firms are distinct from ordinary firms as stakeholder managers due to three key characteristics. First, their longer time horizons allow them to plan and invest across generations rather than just fiscal quarters, changing how they weigh long-term community relationship payoffs. Second, their identity is often fused with the family name, meaning the family's brand and personal reputation are the same asset, so stakeholder missteps carry both personal and corporate cost. Third, due to concentrated and continuous ownership, a controlling family can commit to community relationships for decades, which a dispersed ownership structure of a publicly listed company can rarely rely on. For example, the lecture cited Infosys, which has over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares, illustrating why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority - a contrast to family firms where concentrated ownership enables long-term stakeholder commitments. This distinct positioning means family firms protect socio-emotional wealth alongside financial returns.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding (5 marks): Accurately identifies all three distinguishing characteristics (time horizon, identity fusion, concentrated ownership).\n- Analytical application (5 marks): Successfully uses the Infosys example to illustrate the principal-agent relationship and contrasting ownership structures, demonstrating understanding of how ownership concentration affects stakeholder management.",
