        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the availability heuristic and discuss why it leads to systematic errors in judgment. Use the plane crash vs. car accident example and the Zerodha/Nithin Kamath example from the lecture to illustrate your answer. What is the recommended antidote?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The availability heuristic is a mental shortcut (System 1) where the question \"how likely or frequent is this event?\" is substituted with the easier question \"how easily can I recall an example of this?\". This leads to systematic errors because ease of recall is influenced by factors unrelated to real probability, such as recency, vividness, and media coverage.\n\nIllustration 1 (Transport risk): Most people perceive dying in a plane crash as a bigger risk than dying in a car accident. This is incorrect; car accidents cause far more deaths. The plane crash is more vivid and dominates news cycles, making it easily recalled, while car accidents are dispersed and rarely treated as national news.\n\nIllustration 2 (Corporate strategy): A manager judges a strategy as likely to succeed based on the vivid, widely covered success story of Zerodha and Nithin Kamath, while ignoring the many unremarkable failures that Nithin Kamath had which were never headline news. The failures are not available in memory, distorting the perceived odds.\n\nAntidote: The recommended corrective practice is to stop when judging likelihood and ask, \"Am I basing this on actual data or on whichever example comes to mind fastest?\" Whenever possible, replace the vivid anecdote with actual base rates from broader data, not what the loudest or most vivid example suggests.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of availability heuristic",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Correct use of plane crash vs. car accident illustration",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Correct use of Zerodha/Nithin Kamath illustration",
              "marks": 2
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Identification and explanation of the antidote",
              "marks": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the mechanism of anchoring bias using the spinning wheel experiment described in the lecture. Why does the bias persist even when participants know the number is random?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The spinning wheel experiment demonstrates that anchoring is an automatic, unconscious mental process. Participants were shown a rigged wheel stopping on a high or low number and then asked to estimate an unrelated quantity (percentage of African countries). Those exposed to the high number gave significantly higher estimates, while those exposed to the low number gave lower estimates. This occurs because the mind clings to the initial number, using it as a reference point or \"anchor\" for subsequent judgment. The bias persists even when participants are explicitly aware that the wheel number is random and irrelevant because the anchoring effect operates at a fast, intuitive level (System 1 thinking) that is difficult to override with conscious knowledge or warnings. The initial number becomes mentally fixed, shifting the entire range of subsequent estimates, regardless of the logical disconnect between the anchor and the target question.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of anchoring mechanism",
              "marks": 5,
              "description": " accurately describes the wheel experiment and the direction of the effect (high number → high estimate, low number → low estimate)."
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Explanation of boundary condition (awareness vs. bias)",
              "marks": 5,
              "description": "explains why knowing the number is random does not easily eliminate the effect, linking to the concept of automatic vs. controlled thinking."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the distinction between legitimate persuasion and manufactured consent as discussed in the lecture. Why is it important for future business professionals to understand this distinction?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "Legitimate persuasion gives genuine evidence and reasoning that stands up to scrutiny, even if the evidence is challenging. The case will still hold because it is not made up and uses evidence critically. Manufactured consent, on the other hand, depends on the audience not seeing the full picture with all evidence. It relies on selective framing, repetition of certain claims, or borrowed authority from trusted spokespeople. The crucial \"kicker\" is that if people saw the whole and balanced picture, they might not reach the conclusion that the decision-maker wants. This distinction matters for future business professionals because they will sit on both sides of this dynamic: as audience members reading industry reports funded by the companies they favor, and as producers of such reports. Understanding this helps them critically evaluate information and avoid manipulative framing techniques in their own professional communications.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the persuasion vs. manufactured consent distinction: 5 marks\n- Articulation of the \"kicker\" regarding full picture scrutiny: 3 marks\n- Application to real-world business professional context: 2 marks",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of the persuasion vs. manufactured consent distinction",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Articulation of the \"kicker\" regarding full picture scrutiny",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Application to real-world business professional context",
              "marks": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the five cognitive mechanisms discussed in the lecture and how they operate together at the individual level to shape a final belief or decision. Then, explain how these same mechanisms are scaled up to form the phenomenon of propaganda. Use a specific business example in your explanation.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The five cognitive mechanisms discussed operate as an interconnected system at the individual level to shape judgment. First, System 1 thinking provides the fast, confident answer to reduce cognitive load. The availability heuristic then populates that answer with vivid, easily recalled examples from memory, constructing the initial narrative. Anchoring sets a numerical starting point from which judgment proceeds. Confirmation bias immediately moves to protect the selected answer, filtering out contradictory evidence. Finally, social proof reinforces the answer when the individual observes others holding the same belief, making a shaky narrative feel solid through group consensus.\n\nAt the societal scale, these same mechanisms are deliberately and repeatedly exploited by propagandists. A communicator pulls these levers, using biased framing to trigger availability, setting anchors to skew numerical judgment, exploiting confirmation bias to make the audience seek only supporting evidence, and leveraging social proof to make the narrative appear universally accepted. The critical vulnerability is that by the time the audience questions the message, the combined effect of these levers may have already solidified their perception.\n\nA specific business example illustrating this dynamic is a leadership announcement for a corporate restructuring that feels convincing to employees. The announcement may use vivid anecdotes (availability) about the 'future' (anchoring the narrative), assure managers that 'people like us' are supportive (social proof), and frame the change as necessary for 'growth' (confirmation bias of the existing corporate culture). An employee who recognizes this pattern would pause to ask what is left out of the story, such as specific financial risks or alternative strategies, to exercise critical thinking.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the five mechanisms and their individual-level interaction: 4 marks\n- Clear explanation of the scaling up to propaganda: 3 marks\n- Specific, relevant business example demonstrating the concept: 2 marks\n- Structure and clarity of expression: 1 mark",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of the five mechanisms and their individual-level interaction",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Clear explanation of the scaling up to propaganda and the role of the communicator",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Specific, relevant business example demonstrating the mechanism interaction",
              "marks": 2
            }
          ]
        }
