      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the difference between an epistemic bubble and an echo chamber, using the examples of Maya and Manan from the lecture. How does this distinction affect our ability to question expertise?",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The difference between an epistemic bubble and an echo chamber lies in the nature of the missing or discredited outside voices and the subject's responsiveness to new evidence.\n\n*   **Epistemic Bubble (Maya):** An epistemic bubble occurs when relevant voices are simply left out of a person's information diet. In the lecture, Maya is an example of this; her Instagram feed, friends, and habits never brought up mainstream news. Because the information was merely absent (missing), not actively forbidden, the bubble is fragile. When Maya is shown the missing news or evidence, she is open to changing her mind because she was not pre-disposed to distrust the source.\n\n*   **Echo Chamber (Manan):** An echo chamber is not merely a lack of outside voices, but a constructed environment where outside voices have been discredited in advance. Manan's example illustrates this; his family and friends taught him that anyone outside the close-knit group is not to be trusted. When shown the same evidence as Maya, Manan does not change his mind; instead, he digs in deeper and rejects the evidence.\n\n*   **Effect on Questioning Expertise:** This distinction is critical for a critical thinker. If one assumes a person is in an epistemic bubble, the strategy to help them is to populate their information diet with the missing voices or evidence. If one assumes a person is in an echo chamber, simply presenting more evidence will likely fail and may entrench the person further. Effective questioning requires diagnosing whether the person is missing information (bubble) or actively rejecting it due to group affiliation (chamber).",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of epistemic bubble vs. echo chamber",
              "marks": 4
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Accurate application of Maya/Manan examples to define the concepts",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analysis of the implication for questioning expertise",
              "marks": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the Joshua Bell metro station story and the lecture on social construction of value, explain why the perceived value of the music changed so dramatically between the concert hall and the metro station. In your answer, discuss the role of institutions, reputations, and setting in shaping value, and explain why this demonstrates that value is 'socially constructed' rather than 'inherent'.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The perceived value of Joshua Bell's music changed dramatically because value is not inherent in the music or the violin, but is socially constructed by the surrounding context. In the concert hall, the high ticket price (over $100), the reputation of Joshua Bell as a world-class musician, the institutional prestige of the concert hall, and the crowd of paying attendees all signaled that the performance was valuable. This collective signal told the audience to value the music highly. In the metro station, the absence of these trappings, no high ticket price, no established reputation context, no prestigious venue, and a crowd of commuters focused on work, led almost nobody to stop. The same music and same violin produced opposite value outcomes. This demonstrates that value is 'socially constructed' because it is built by outside elements, institutions, reputations, and settings, rather than being an intrinsic 'weight or color' of the object itself. As the lecture states, value is",
          "value.": "he critical thinker's job is to recognize that value is made by these external mechanisms, not found in the object.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding",
              "marks": 5,
              "description": "Student correctly identifies that value changed due to setting/context, not the music itself, and defines socially constructed value."
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analytical application",
              "marks": 5,
              "description": "Student applies the Joshua Bell example to explain the mechanism of institutional and reputational signaling, and distinguishes between intrinsic and extrinsic sources of value."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain the role of the researcher as a 'neutral voice' and discuss how methodological rigor serves as the foundation for trust when researchers must critique the work of territorial experts (engineers, product, design). Use real-world examples from the lecture to support your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The researcher acts as the neutral, unbiased voice in the room, bridging the engineering, product, and design pillars. These experts often become territorial because they have invested significant effort into their work, engineers in the code, product in the vision, and design in the UI/UX. The researcher's core dilemma is providing critical feedback without being perceived as attacking personal work. Methodological rigor serves as the foundation for trust because it shifts the basis of critique from personal opinion to empirical evidence. As the professor explained, credibility is not based on authority alone but on the data: \"I have spoken to a statistically significant sample base... when you reach a number of 100 which essentially in statistics says gives you a 95% confidence level.\" This rigor allows the researcher to say, \"We've told you this. Watch out for this,\" acting as a 'voice of warning' rather than simply rejecting ideas. The professor illustrated the limitations of relying on expertise alone using the Henry Ford example: asking customers what they want yields \"flying horses,\" not automobiles, because customers cannot imagine novel products. Similarly, Steve Jobs never researched the iPhone with consumers. In cases of true innovation, researchers must look at 'closest immediate behaviors', such as the case histories of HR, IT, and admin staff, to build proxy models for adoption. Thus, the researcher balances the growth of the organization with the voice of the customer, using data rigor to navigate the epistemic bubbles and territorialities of the expert teams.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of the researcher's role and territoriality",
              "marks": 5
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Analytical application of methodological rigor as a trust-building mechanism and its limits",
              "marks": 5
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
