          "question": "During a dashboard review, a professor mentions that for a composition question involving the share of menu categories in pizza orders, a pie chart is the default choice. However, if there are 6 categories, the lecture recommends an alternative. What is the primary reason the pie chart fails in this scenario, and what is the recommended alternative?",
          "options": [
            "A) The pie chart fails because it requires computer software to generate; the recommended alternative is a stacked bar chart created manually.",
            "B) The pie chart fails because the human eye is better at comparing bar lengths (linear distance) than pie chart angles (radial distance); the recommended alternative is a stacked bar chart.",
            "C) The pie chart fails because it cannot show more than 3 slices; the recommended alternative is a donut chart with a hole in the middle.",
            "D) The pie chart fails because it only works for time-series data; the recommended alternative is a line chart showing the growth of each category."
          ],
          "correct_index": 1,
          "difficulty": "Easy | Conceptual",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The lecture explicitly states the principle of \"position beats angle.\" Research shows that the human visual system is much better at comparing the length of bars (a linear, one-dimensional comparison) than the angles of pie slices (a radial, two-dimensional comparison). When there are too many slices (beyond 4), comparing the small angles becomes extremely difficult and error-prone. The lecture recommends the stacked bar chart as the superior alternative because it allows the user to compare the length of the bars, which is perceptually accurate. The alternative of a donut chart (Option C) still relies on angle comparison, and Option A incorrectly suggests software limitations as the reason, whereas the lecture focuses on human perception.",
          "key_concept": "Position beats angle principle and the recommendation of stacked bar charts over pie charts"
