            "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The Module 2 Intro lecture emphasizes that startup conflicts rarely stem from one person being 'bad'; instead, they arise from default structural dynamics that go unmanaged. Founders often assume good intentions will suffice, but without active governance, agreements become fragile and disputes inevitable, even among well-meaning parties. Option A contradicts the explicit 'zero blame' framing of the lecture. Option C ignores the internal dynamics the module focuses on. Option D reduces the complex source of disputes to a single variable (financials), which the module suggests is insufficient without governance.",
            "trap_warning": "Students may focus on the Zepto case study's specific details, missing the general principle that the lecture extracts: that structure, not character, is the primary driver of conflict.",
            "distractor_analysis": "A is"
