            "id": 6,
            "type": "single_choice",
            "difficulty": "Moderate",
            "key_concept": "User Segmentation and Dashboard Scope",
            "question": "A dashboard builder is tasked with creating a single interface for three user roles: the Chief Marketing Officer (CMO), a Category Manager, and a Warehouse Manager. The CMO requires overall market trend analysis, the Category Manager requires SK-level funnel data, and the Warehouse Manager requires real-time fulfillment exceptions. According to the core intuition of the Identifying User Segments and Key Stakeholders lecture, what is the fundamental design flaw in attempting to satisfy all three roles with one single dashboard page?",
            "options": [
              "A) It will violate the corporate brand guidelines regarding layout consistency.",
              "B) The resulting dashboard will be too dense for the CMO, too shallow for the Category Manager, and ultimately useful to nobody due to misaligned information density.",
              "C) It is technically impossible to mix different chart types on a single dashboard page without causing software errors.",
              "D) The Warehouse Manager will refuse to use any dashboard that does not display real-time data exclusively."
            ],
            "correct_indices": [
              1
            ],
            "correct_index": 1,
            "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The lecture on Identifying User Segments and Key Stakeholders explicitly states that a single dashboard cannot serve users with fundamentally different information needs simultaneously. The CMO needs high-level trends (requiring less density), the Category Manager needs detailed SK-level data (requiring more density), and the Warehouse Manager needs real-time exceptions. Attempting to combine these results in a dashboard that is 'too dense for the CMO, too shallow for the category manager, and ultimately useful to nobody.' Option A is a distractor because brand guidelines, while important, are not the primary conceptual failure mode described in the lecture regarding user needs. Option C is a distractor because modern dashboard tools (Power BI, Tableau) are specifically designed to handle mixed chart types on a single page; the issue is informational alignment, not technical capability. Option D is a distractor because it assumes a rigid user requirement that does not address the core problem of misaligned information density across roles.",
            "trap_warning": "A common error is to assume that dashboard tools can technically display all necessary data, overlooking the cognitive and informational mismatch for the user.",
            "distractor_analysis": "Option A distractors shift focus to branding compliance. Option C distractors focus on technical software limitations rather than user cognitive load. Option D distractors focus on a single user's preference rather than the holistic design failure."
